VALUES (
    '24934535', '11816365', 'Small bowel diverticulitis', 'DIVERTICULITIS',
    'A patient was admitted via ew emer. for evaluation of abdominal symptoms, later diagnosed with diverticular disease.', 'The patient is a ___ w/ h/o GERD, IBS who presents to the ED  with 1 day of epigastrium abdominal pain associated with fevers,  general malaise, and leukocytosis and now seen in consultation  by ___ for further evaluation. The patient states, he woke up yesterday morning with fever and  general malaise associated with abdominal pain located to his  epigastrium and periumbilical area. He describes this pain as  crampy in nature, waxing and waning and he does not recall any  aggravating or alleviating factors. He comes to the ED for further evaluation. Upon arrival to the ED, 97, 91, 131/104, 16,  97% RA. Labs remarkable for leukocytosis to 16.000 and RUQ U/S with findings  of gallstone, with no signs of acute cholecystitis.', 'Past Medical History: GERD, IBD  Past Surgical History: remote open appendectomy     Social History: ___ Family History: non-contributory', '[VITALS] Temp: NA BP: 131/104 HR: NA O2Sat: 97 Weight: NA  [EXAM] Admission  Vitals: 97, 91, 131/104, 16, 97% RA  GEN: A&O, NAD HEENT: No scleral icterus, mucus membranes moist CV: RRR, No M/G/R PULM: Clear to auscultation b/l, No W/R/R ABD: Soft, nondistended, nontender, no rebound or guarding, normoactive bowel sounds, no palpable masses Ext: No ___ edema, ___ warm and well perfused',
    'active', 'system_generator'
);


INSERT INTO clinicalcases (
    clinicalcaseid, patientid, title, type,
    description, symptom, medicalhistory, pe,
    status, createdBy
)
VALUES (
    '29756843', '11817384', 'Primary diagnosis: 1. Diverticulitis 2. Pseudomonas bacteremia 3. Incidental pulmonary nodules Secondary diagnosis: Hypertension Sleep apnea Obesity Inappropriate sinus tachycardia', 'DIVERTICULITIS',
    'A patient was admitted via ew emer. for evaluation of abdominal symptoms, later diagnosed with diverticular disease.', '___ y/o F w/ h/o obesity, HTN, hypercholesterolemia,  inappropriate sinus tachycardia. Patient was recently seen by  ___ as an outpatient (___). At that time had c/o  intermittent chest tightness. Plan had been to obtain an  outpatient stress-echo for further evaluation, and ___ of  hearts to monitor for tachycardia during these episodes. Had  stress-echo this AM, which was negative, but immediately  following stress echo developed sinus tachycardia to 150''s,  nausea, light headedness and vertigo. symptoms did not resolve.  Sent to ED for eval.   .   Review of systems is otherwise notable for 1 sick contact  (nephew with viral syndrome vs. strep throat) mild L-knee pain  that has improved. Patient denies f/c/ns/n/v/d/c/brbpr prior to  this episode. No recent flights, car rides etc. Doe snot smoke.    .   In the emergency department, initial VS were: T98.5, HR 154, BP  133/74, RR 20, O2 97% RA. Exam notable for guaiac positive brown  stool but otherwise normal. ECG showed likely sinus tachycardia  after adenosine given. Labs notable for leukocytosis, elevated  lipase. Patient was given adenosine on arrival and then rate  controlled with metoprolol and verapamil. She received  ceftriaxone. Got 3L NS in ED. Patient could not get CTA due to  h/o anaphylaxis to contrast so got lovenox and was admitted for  V/Q scan, IVF''s.   .   Last VS were 118 98/63, 22 98% 3LNC.   .   On arrival to the floor, patient comfortable. c/o ___ chest  tigthtness but appeared 110% comfortable on exam.', 'inappropriate sinus tachycardia   hypertension   hypercholesterolemia   obesity   vasovagal syncope   positional vertigo,   chronic headaches,   sleep apnea for which she takes BiPAP, settings of ___ at  home.      Social History: ___ Family History: Non contributory', '[VITALS] Temp: 99.0 BP: NA HR: 108 O2Sat: 72 Weight: NA  [EXAM] VITAL SIGNS: T99 BP102/72 HR108 RR18 O299% 4LNC ->   GENERAL: Pleasant, well appearing obese woman in NAD   HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic. No conjunctival pallor. No  scleral icterus. PERRLA/EOMI. MMM. OP clear. Neck Supple, No LAD    CARDIAC: Regular rhythm, normal rate. Normal S1, S2. No murmurs,  rubs or ___. Cannot assess JVP ___ to habitus   LUNGS: CTAB, good air movement biaterally.   ABDOMEN: NABS. Soft, obese NT, ND. No HSM   EXTREMITIES: No edema or calf pain, 2+ dorsalis pedis/ posterior  tibial pulses.   SKIN: No rashes/lesions, ecchymoses.   NEURO: A&Ox3. Appropriate. CN ___ grossly intact. Preserved  sensation throughout. ___ strength throughout. Normal  coordination. Gait normal.   PSYCH: Listens and responds to questions appropriately, pleasant',
    'active', 'system_generator'
);


INSERT INTO clinicalcases (
    clinicalcaseid, patientid, title, type,
    description, symptom, medicalhistory, pe,
    status, createdBy
)
VALUES (
    '21343003', '11825167', 'Acute on Chronic Pancreatitis Community Acquired Pneumonia', 'PANCREATITIS',
    'A patient was admitted via ew emer. for evaluation of abdominal symptoms, later diagnosed with acute pancreatitis.', '___ with PMH DM p/w chest pain. CP is across his chest, is  non-radiating, worse with deep breaths, eating, and exertion. No  change with position such as sitting forward or back. It has  been going on for a few weeks. Also admits to cough prod of  green phlegm, nausea and vomiting. No diarrhea. Also having  diffuse abd pain. ROS neg for fevers and sweats but admits to  chills. Also admits to feeling like room spinning. Of note,  history obtained from ED was different so pt prob unreliable  historian. He told them his pain was not pleuritic and was worse  when sitting up quickly. Also reported an episode of syncope  last night but is again unclear on the details.   Of note, on suicide screening, he initially told the nursing  staff that he wanted to kill himself yesterday by slicing his  wrists. He denied this to MD evaluation in ED but did state that  he has a history of slicing his wrists.   In the ED initial VS 97.0 68 165/97 18 99% 4L Nasal Cannula. EKG  was unremarkable and showed no ischemia. Trops neg x 1. Labs  significant for WBC 13.8, Hct 36.6 (unknown baseline), plats  216, cr 1.6 (unknown ___. LFTs normal except lipase 337. CXR  showed multifocal pneumonia in the right lung and probable small  right pleural effusion. D-dimer was elevated at 534.    At time of initial interview, pt. was lethargic.  As pt. became  more aware, this following HPI was taken:  Mr. ___ is a ___  y/o male with unknown PMH who presents with the CC of abdominal  pain, chest pain, and right shoulder pain. Pt. was in his usual  state of health over the past until the last year. At this time,  he reports the onset of abdominal pain. He is unable to provide  details around this pain. However, approx 1 week prior to  admisison, the pt. describes the onset of a sharp burning  constant abd. pain radiating to the chest. The abdominal pain is  located diffusely thorughout the abdomen and at times increases  in intensity. The pt. states that eating or drinking makes the  pain worse and that lying flat helps the pain. The chest pain he  describes began after the abdominal pain and is a burning type  pain. Additionally, the pt. reports that he has developed cough  within the last ___ days that is productive typically of "chunks  of blood." He now reports a sharp pain in his right shoulder and  proximal right arm. He endorses flatus but denies any bowel  movements. He has had several episodes of vomiting but denies  hematemesis. Pt. denies nausea, diarrhea, or changes in his  stool or voiding patterns leading up to symptom onset.', 'Diabetes hx ___ c/b peripheral neuropathy   chronic back pain   hypothyroidism   Abdominal surgery ___ knife stabbing injury    Social History: ___ Family History: unknown', '[VITALS] Temp: NA BP: 126/79 HR: NA O2Sat: 98 Weight: NA  [EXAM] ADMISSION    Vitals: 98.1, 126/79, 72, 18, 95% RA.   General: somnolent and frequently falling asleep initially but  after awaking later in interview was more alert and AAOx3.   HEENT: NC/AT, sclera anicteric, MM dry   Lungs: decreased breath sounds at right base, no wheezing or  rhonchi   CV: RRR no m/r/g   Abdomen: soft, TTP diffusely, palpably distended bladder, NABS,  NT, ND Ext:  WWP, no edema',
    'active', 'system_generator'
);


