VALUES (
    '29431145', '12086251', 'Acute appendicitis', 'APPENDICITIS',
    'A patient was admitted via observation admit for evaluation of abdominal symptoms, later diagnosed with acute appendicitis.', 'Ms. ___ is an ___ yr old female who was in her usual state  of health until 9:30 pm last night (___), when upon coming home  from dinner, she developed acute, ___, ___ pain.  She says she felt tightness and had to loosen her shorts in an  attempt to relieve the pain and shortly thereafter felt  nauseated and vomited. During the night, the pain begin to move  to her supra-pubic area before localizing to the RLQ. She  reports feeling chills (she did not measure her temperature) and  her head felt warm. She was not able to sleep well during the  night due to her continued vomiting (x7) and pain. She says she  did not notice any blood in her emesis. She reports having had a  small bowel movement in the middle of the night which was hard  (she is occasionally constipated) but has not had diarrhea or  any additional BM since.  She currently says that her pain is ___ if she remains still  and ___ if she moves (on fentanyl) with pain primarily in RLQ.  She is accompanied by her daughter who is a family ___  ___.', 'H/o R arm Melanoma in situ (___) s/p excision  H/o BCC L mid nose ___ seborrheic keratosis, actinic keratosis Osteoporosis, traumatic t7 compression fracture Pulmonary nodule Meningioma Colonic adenoma Shingles Multiple fractures: humerus, ankle, ribs, compression fractures    Social History: ___ Family History: Colon Cancer (mother, passed at ___ y/o) Thyroid Cancer - adenoCa (Daughter)', '[VITALS] Temp: NA BP: NA HR: NA O2Sat: NA Weight: NA  [EXAM] On admission Well-nourished well-developed female in moderate apparent  distress. Vital signs as above. Skin: warm, dry, normal turgor. HEENT: atraumatic Chest: Resps nonlabored Heart: No tachycardia Abdomen: symmetric, non-distended, normal bowel sounds, tender  to palpation/percussion (mostly in RLQ), involuntary guarding,  no rebound tenderness, negative Rovsings sign, Obturator sign,  and Psoas sign  Extremities: no edema.',
    'active', 'system_generator'
);


