-- INSERT RADIOLOGY REPORTS

INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20890008',
    '10040626-RR-9',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were obtained from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis after administration of 130cc intravenous contrast.  Axial images were interpreted in conjunction with coronal and sagittal reformats.  DLP: 404 mGy-cm CTDIvol:  8 mGy  FINDINGS:   CHEST: The visualized lung bases are clear.  There is no pleural or pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN: The liver and gallbladder are normal.  The portal and hepatic veins are patent. The pancreas and spleen are unremarkable.  The adrenal glands are normal bilaterally.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically with no hydronephrosis.  The stomach and small bowel are normal in caliber. Within the right lower quadrant, there is a dilated, hyperdense structure rising from the base of the cecum, which measures up to 9 mm, likely representing the appendix demonstrating mucosal hyperenhancment with respect to adjacent bowel.  What is most striking is hyperenhancement and wall thickening rather than fluid content.  Inflammatory changes are noted at the base of the cecum, and adjacent to the whole wall of the appendix, and probably particularly at the tip, which has an indistinct appearance.  Within the mesentery adjacent to the ascending colon, there are two strikingly enlarged and briskly enhancing lymph nodes measuring 1.4 and 1.8 cm, the former with low-density material centrally, and both demonstrate surrounding inflammatory changes, including marked fat stranding about the node with suspected necrotic or suppurative changes.  The ascending, transverse, and descending colon are unremarkable. There is no mesenteric free air.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder, and adnexa are unremarkable. The uterus contains an intrauterine device, and standard position. Small amount of simple free fluid is seen in the pelvis, likely physiologic.  VESSELS: The aorta is normal in caliber and its major branches are patent.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: No focal lytic or sclerotic lesion concerning for malignancy.  NOTIFICATION: Findings were discussed with Dr. ___ by Dr. ___ telephone at 23:52 on ___.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27892518',
    '10070247-RR-22',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were obtained of the abdomen and pelvis after the administration of intravenous contrast.  Reformatted coronal and sagittal images were reviewed.  FINDINGS:   CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: The bases of the lungs are clear.  Within the liver, there are calcifications within the left hepatic lobe which are oriented in a linear configuration and possibly represent prior trauma or infection.   No suspicious focal lesions are identified within the liver.  The portal vein is patent.  The gallbladder is unremarkable.  There is no intra or extrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  The pancreas, bilateral adrenal glands, spleen and bilateral kidneys are normal in appearance.  The stomach, duodenum and small bowel are also normal in appearance with no evidence of wall thickening or obstruction.  Within the right lower quadrant, there is an enlarged and fluid-filled appendix with apparent fecal material within the base of the appendix, measuring up to 2.1 cm in greatest diameter at the base (2:49).  Surrounding inflammatory fat stranding is also present.  There is no evidence of extraluminal air or drainable fluid collection.  Multiple small reactive lymph nodes are also present within the right lower quadrant.  The remaining large bowel is unremarkable with no evidence of wall thickening or obstruction.  There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymph node enlargement.  The intra-abdominal vasculature demonstrates a normal caliber aorta throughout and a widely patent celiac axis, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric artery.  Two right renal arteries are present.  There is relative atrophy of the left rectus abdominis muscle, likely secondary to prior surgery with multiple left-sided abdominal wall clips in place.  CT PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The terminal ureters are unremarkable.  The bladder wall is slightly thickened, possibly due to incomplete distention.  The rectum and sigmoid are unremarkable.  There is no pelvic sidewall or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  A small fat-containing left inguinal hernia is present.  There is no pelvic free fluid.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There is evidence of prior spinal fusion at the L4-L5 level with apparent donor graft site within the right iliac bone.  No suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesion is present.   There has been interval removal of orthopedic hardware from the lumbar spine.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21807759',
    '10073256-RR-10',
    'CT',
    'Venous',
    'UNILAT UP EXT VEINS US LEFT',
    'LEFT UPPER EXTREMITY ULTRASOUND: Gray-scale, color, and Doppler ultrasound were used to evaluate the left internal jugular, subclavian, axillary, basilic, brachial and cephalic veins.  Vessels in the forearm are also evaluated.  The cephalic vein contains expansile echogenic material, extending from the forearm to the mid upper arm, compatible with occlusive thrombus.  More proximally in the upper arm, cephalic vein is compressible and demonstrates flow.  Clot does not extend to the deep system.  Deep veins of the left upper extremity, including the basilic, brachial, axillary and subclavian veins are compressible (where applicable) with normal waveforms.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21807759',
    '10073256-RR-11',
    'CT',
    'Venous',
    'UNILAT UP EXT VEINS US RIGHT',
    'RIGHT UPPER EXTREMITY VENOUS ULTRASOUND.:   FINDINGS: Grayscale and color sonograms of bilateral subclavian and right-sided internal jugular, cephalic, axillary, basilic, and brachial veins were evaluated.  There is unchanged appearance of the waveforms in the subclavian veins.  Remaining vessels demonstrated normal flow, compressibility, and augmentation.'
);


