VALUES (
    '21807759',
    '10073256-RR-7',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'EXAM: Abdomen and pelvis CT.  TECHNIQUE: This is an interpretation of images acquired at an outside institution, for second opinion reading.  Data was acquired from the abdomen and pelvis after the administration of intravenous contrast.  Images were displayed in the axial and coronal planes.  Sagittal reformats were not included with the outside hospital images as is protocol at ___.  FINDINGS: There is bilateral dependent atelectasis in the lung bases.  The heart is grossly normal.  ABDOMEN WITH CONTRAST: There are two hypodensities within the liver parenchyma, the more superior in segment VIII (2:14) measures 5 x 8 mm and is too small to fully characterize.  There is a second lobulated hypodensity within segment IVb (2:33), which measures 14 x 14 mm, not fully characterized on this single phase study, but may represent a cyst.  However, consider ultrasound if there is history of malignancy for further evaluation. Comparison with prior CT studies would be helpful.  The gallbladder is normal. The pancreas, spleen, and adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically.  There is a 32 x 27 mm cyst in the upper pole of the right kidney.  No bowel obstruction is seen.  There is no ascites.  There is no free abdominal air.  PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: There is a hyperemic dilated appendix with a maximum diameter of 16 mm (2:61).  There is a proximal appendicolith (2:58) and adjacent cecal wall thickening.  There is surrounding mesenteric stranding and a small amount of adjacent free fluid without a well-defined rim (2:62).  No drainable fluid collection or extraluminal gas is seen.  High density material in the prostate which are most likely brachytherapy seeds.  The bladder and rectum are normal.  There is no pelvic lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOWS: There are no fractures, lytic or sclerotic lesions.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
