INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '23394885',
    '10314106-RR-8',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multidetector CT of the abdomen and pelvis was performed without contrast.  Multiplanar reformations were provided.  DOSE: DLP 337.79 mGy-cm  FINDINGS: Lung Bases:  On series 2, image 3, there is a ground-glass nodule in the left lower lobe measuring 3.6 mm. Mild bibasilar atelectasis noted. The imaged portion of the heart is normal.  Abdomen: A tiny hypodensity within the hepatic dome is too small to characterize and the liver otherwise enhances normally. No biliary ductal dilation. The gallbladder is unremarkable as is the spleen and pancreas. Adrenal glands appear normal. The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast promptly. The abdominal aorta is normal in course and caliber with widely patent major branches.  The stomach and duodenum appear normal.  Pelvis: There is a large fluid collection within the right hemipelvis seen best on series 2, image 60 measuring approximately 4.2 x 6.8 x 5.1 cm.  Surrounding fat stranding is present and adjacent bowel wall thickening is seen along the cecum and portions of the ascending colon, likely reactive. On series 2, image 56, a rounded structure is seen centrally in the abdomen adjacent to this large collection which likely represents a portion of the appendix which has likely perforated resulting in this fluid collection.  No gross free air.  No signs of bowel obstruction.  No significant free pelvic fluid is seen. The urinary bladder is well distended appearing normal.  Bones: No worrisome lytic or blastic osseous lesion is seen. Transitional anatomy is noted at the lumbosacral junction with partially lumbarized S1 noted.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29628037',
    '10317338-RR-10',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Contiguous MDCT images of the abdomen and pelvis were performed after administration of intravenous contrast.  Axial, coronal, and sagittal reformats were acquired.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN: The lung bases are clear.  The liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, bilateral adrenal glands, and kidneys are normal.  The kidneys are homogeneously enhancing and excreting urine without evidence of stones. There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  The systemic arterial, systemic venous and portal venous system of the abdomen and pelvis are normal.  The esophagus, stomach, and small bowel are normal.  There is no free air and no free fluid.  CT OF THE PELVIS: The appendix is dilated and fluid filled measuring up to 10 mm in maximal dimension.  There is minimal periappendiceal fat stranding, but no abscess ___ collection.  There is no evidence of perforation or free air.  The remainder of the colon and rectum are normal.  The urinary bladder is normal.  A diaphragm is seen in the vagina.  The ovaries are normal.  There is no pelvic lymphadenopathy.  There is mild S-shaped scoliosis of the lumbar spine, possibly positional.  There are no suspicious lytic or sclerotic bony lesions.'
);


