    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were obtained from the lung bases to lesser trochanters after the administration of intravenous contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were prepared.  DLP:  439 mGy-cm.  CT ABDOMEN: The lung bases are clear.  The visualized portions of the heart and pericardium are normal.  The liver enhances homogenously and there is no focal liver lesion.  The portal veins are patent.  The gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, and adrenals are normal.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast without evidence of hydronephrosis or mass.  There is no bowel obstruction.  CT PELVIS: The appendix is dilated to 8 mm with hyperemia and adjacent fascial thickening.  There is a moderate amount of intermediate density free fluid in the pelvis.  There is no free air or organized fluid collection.  The colon, rectum, uterus, and urinary bladder are normal.  A NuvaRing is noted in the vagina.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There is no lytic or blastic lesion worrisome for malignancy.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25472455',
    '10653951-RR-12',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multi detector CT images through the abdomen and pelvis were obtained after the administration of intravenous contrast. No oral contrast was administered.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were generated and reviewed.  DOSE: 350 mGy cm.  FINDINGS: Chest:  The bases of the lungs are clear bilaterally. There is no pericardial effusion.  Abdomen:  The liver is homogeneous in attenuation with no focal lesion identified. There is no intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation. The portal veins are patent.  No radiopaque cholelithiasis is identified. The pancreas, spleen, and bilateral adrenal glands are within normal limits.  The kidneys present symmetric nephrograms excretion of contrast. No focal lesion or hydronephrosis is identified.  The stomach, duodenum, and loops of small and large bowel are unremarkable.  Within the right lower quadrant, a dilated fluid filled appendix is visualized with surrounding small fluid and stranding as well as hyperemic wall, findings which are consistent with ____ (601b:22 and 2:56).  No adjacent cecal wall thickening is present.  The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber without aneurysmal dilatation. There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric adenopathy.  Pelvis: The bladder is moderately well distended and grossly unremarkable. Prostate gland and seminal vesicles are within normal limits. There is no pelvic free fluid. No inguinal or pelvic sidewall adenopathy is present.  Osseous structures: No suspicious lytic or blastic lesion is identified.  Focal well defined sclerotic lesion within the left femoral head felt likely a bone island.  NOTIFICATION: Findings communicated to the ordering physician ___ by Dr. ___ at 22:53 on ___.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20921058',
    '10669544-RR-12',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were acquired from the lung bases through the pubic symphysis following administration of intravenous contrast.  Multiplanar reformations were generated.  FINDINGS: The lung bases are clear.  There is no pleural effusion.  The heart is normal in size.  The liver demonstrates no focal lesion.  The gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, adrenal glands, and kidneys appear unremarkable.  The small and large bowel loops are normal in caliber.  There is normal contrast progression distally to the colon.  There is diffuse inflammation of the appendix and mural hyperemia with subjacent fat stranding, with the appendix measuring up to 7 mm in diameter, consistent with ____.  There is no free air or periappendiceal abscess.  Allowing for limited intra-abdominal fat, there is no intra-abdominal lymphadenopathy.  Great vessels are patent.  CT PELVIS: The bladder, prostate, and rectum appear unremarkable.  There is no free fluid within the pelvis.  There is no inguinal or pelvic sidewall lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOW: Osseous structures are intact.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20000602',
    '10687335-RR-5',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Axial CT images through the abdomen and pelvis were acquired after administration of intravenous contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were reviewed.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: The lung bases are clear.  The visualized portion of the heart is unremarkable.  There are no pleural or pericardial effusions.  The liver, spleen, gallbladder, pancreas, adrenals, kidneys, visualized portions of the ureters, stomach, and visualized loops of small and large bowel are unremarkable.  There is no free air or ascites.    A high attenuation focus is seen in the mid appendix which may represent an appendicolith (versus retained material/contrast).  Beyond this hyperdense focus there is relative dilation of the appendix with mild surrounding fat stranding.  There is no periappendiceal fluid collection.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The bladder, prostate, seminal vesicles, and visualized loops of small and large bowel are unremarkable.  There is no free fluid.  There is grade 1 retrolisthesis of L5 on S1.  Visualized bony structures are otherwise unremarkable.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20655074',
    '10694432-RR-7',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Helical MDCT images were acquired from the lung bases through the greater trochanters with intravenous and without oral contrast.  5-mm axial, coronal, sagittal multiplanar reformats were generated.  FINDINGS: Mild atelectasis is noted at the lung bases.  There are no pleural effusions.  The heart is normal in size, with small pericardial effusion.  Note is made of a small sliding hiatal hernia.  ABDOMEN: The liver enhances homogeneously, without focal lesions identified on this single phase examination.  The gallbladder is partially collapsed.  The pancreas is normal.  There is no intra- or extra-hepatic biliary dilatation.  The spleen is normal in size.  The adrenals are normal.  The kidneys enhance and excrete contrast promptly and symmetrically, without masses or hydronephrosis.  The stomach and small bowel are normal.  PELVIS: The appendix is noted in retrocecal position, coursing medially and posteriorly relative to the ascending colon.  It is fluid filled and dilated up to 10 mm, with mild circumferential wall thickening and mucosal hyperemia.  Mild reactive wall thickening is noted at the base of the cecum.  Inflammatory fat stranding is centered around the appendiceal tip, and extends along the right paracolic gutter and lateral wall of the proximal ascending colon.  Scattered prominent mesenteric and retroperitoneal lymph nodes are present, measuring up to 6 mm, and likely reactive.  There are no extraluminal air or fluid collections to suggest perforation.  There is no pneumatosis, portal or mesenteric venous gas to indicate ischemia.    Scattered colonic diverticula are present, without acute inflammation.  The bladder and distal ureters are normal.  The prostate and seminal vesicles are unremarkable.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27562444',
    '10723288-RR-18',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'PELVIS, NON-OBSTETRIC',
    'EXAMINATION: PELVIS U.S., TRANSVAGINAL:  TECHNIQUE: Grayscale ultrasound images of the pelvis were obtained with transabdominal approach followed by transvaginal approach for further delineation of uterine and ovarian anatomy.  Spectral and color Doppler was performed on the ovaries bilaterally. Targeted ultrasound of the right lower quadrant.  FINDINGS: The uterus is retroverted and measures 6.7 x 3.2 x 4 cm. The endometrium is homogenous and measures 6 mm.  The ovaries are normal.  Normal spectral arterial and venous Doppler waveforms are demonstrated in the ovaries bilaterally. There is no free fluid.  Targeted ultrasound of the right lower quadrant demonstrated no free fluid or focal fluid collection. The appendix is not visualized.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27562444',
    '10723288-RR-19',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis after the uneventful administration of 130 ml of Omnipaque. Coronal and sagittal reformations were provided and reviewed.  Oral contrast was administered.  DOSE: DLP: 530.61 mGy-cm  FINDINGS: Abdomen: A millimetric perifissural nodule on the left is likely a lymph node (2:8).  Otherwise, the included lung bases show no pleural effusion or pneumothorax. The imaged heart is normal size there is no pericardial effusion.  The liver enhances homogeneously without focal lesions. The gallbladder is normal and there is no intra or extrahepatic biliary ductal dilation. The spleen, pancreas and adrenal glands are unremarkable.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast without hydronephrosis.  Note is made of a duplicated right renal collecting system. The portal vein, splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein are patent. The abdominal aorta is normal caliber.  The stomach, large and small bowel are normal.  Contrast has progressed into the ileum. There is no bowel wall thickening or obstruction.  The base of the appendix is dilated to 9 mm (602b:28, 2:58), compatible with ____.  There is a subtle amount of para-appendiceal stranding, however, there is a paucity of mesenteric fat. There is no extraluminal air or abscess.  No free fluid.  The bladder, uterus, and necks and rectum are normal. There is no free pelvic fluid. There is no inguinal or pelvic sidewall lymphadenopathy.  Bones:  There are no lytic or blastic osseous lesions.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27565936',
    '10743310-RR-7',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were acquired from the lung bases through the lesser trochanters following the administration of 130 cc of intravenous Optiray contrast material.  Multiplanar reformations were performed.  ABDOMEN CT: Aside from minimal left basilar atelectasis, the visualized portions of the lungs are clear.  A tiny hypodensity within hepatic segment VII (2:11) is too small to characterize, but statistically is a simple cyst.  The liver is otherwise unremarkable, without evidence of intrahepatic biliary duct dilatation.  The portal vein is patent.  The gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, adrenal glands, and left kidney are normal. Subcentimeter right upper pole renal hypodensity is too small to characterize. Remainder of the right kidney is unremarkable. The stomach, small bowel, and colon are grossly unremarkable.  There is marked dilatation of a fluid-filled appendix, measuring up to 11 mm in diameter (2:61).  A 4-mm appendicolith is seen within the mid appendix (2:67).  There is periappendiceal fat stranding without a fluid collection or evidence of perforation.  No significant free fluid is seen within the abdomen.  There is no evidence of pneumoperitoneum.  Small lymph nodes adjacent to the cecum do not meet CT size criteria (2:57).  The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber and its main branches are patent.  PELVIS CT: The bladder, uterus, and adnexa are unremarkable aside from a likely right corpus luteal cyst.  A small quantity of free fluid is seen within the dependent portion of the pelvis.  No pathologically enlarged pelvic lymph nodes are seen.  BONE WINDOW: A 6-mm sclerotic focus in the right femoral neck is likely a bone island.  No suspicious lytic or blastic lesions are identified.'
);


