INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '28143455',
    '10748922-RR-15',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS. No oral contrast was administered.  DOSE: Total DLP (Body) = 497 mGy-cm. IV Contrast:  130 mL Omnipaque  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Lung bases are clear.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions. There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation. The gallbladder is unremarkable.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  No hydronephrosis.  GASTROINTESTINAL: Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness and enhancement throughout. Colon and rectum are within normal limits. The appendix is slightly dilated in size to approximately 7 mm (previously 4 mm), with surrounding inflammatory stranding and mild hyperemia (601b:36, 602b:20). Findings are compatible with ____.  No drainable fluid collection or free peritoneal air. There is no evidence of mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  RETROPERITONEUM: There is no evidence of retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm. There is no calcium burden in the abdominal aorta and great abdominal arteries.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable. There is no evidence of pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy. There is no free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: Reproductive organs are within normal limits.  BONES AND SOFT TISSUES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions.  Incidentally noted is left L5 spondylolysis (602b:42). Abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.  NOTIFICATION: The above findings were communicated via telephone by Dr. ___ to Dr. ___ at 12:58 on ___.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21689255',
    '10748951-RR-13',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'CT OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS:   TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired 5-mm axial images of the abdomen and pelvis were obtained following the uneventful administration of 130 cc of intravenous contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed at 5-mm slice thickness.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: Included views of the lung bases demonstrate minimal atelectasis.  There is no pericardial or pleural effusion. The heart size is normal.    The liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands, kidneys, stomach, and intra-abdominal loops of small bowel are normal.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy, and no free air.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The appendix is fluid filled and abnormally enlarged, measuring up to 12 mm in diameter, with wall thickening and hyperemia, compatible with acute ____.  The cecum is normal.  There is a small amount of fluid within the right aspect of the pelvis (2:60), which may be a small focus of free fluid or less likely right hydrosalpinx, but small bowel which is hard to delineate.  The uterus is normal.  A 2.6 x 2.4 cm cystic structure within the left aspect of the pelvis (2:55) is probably ovarian in origin.  There is no free air.  The urinary bladder is normal.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There is no acute fracture.  No concerning blastic or lytic lesions are identified.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24969863',
    '10749568-RR-6',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/O CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Non-contrast imaging of the abdomen and pelvis was performed.  No contrast was administered secondary to renal failure.  FINDINGS:   CT ABDOMEN: Visualized lung bases are clear, without consolidation, effusion, or pneumothorax.  The liver again contains multiple calcifications, compatible with granulomas.  There are no further focal liver lesions identified within the limits of a non-contrast study.  The spleen, pancreas, and left adrenal gland are similarly unremarkable.  A small fat-containing lesion in the right adrenal gland is again noted, stable in size and appearance from ___, and again compatible with a myelolipoma.  Inferior to this there is stable adrenal thickening.  The kidneys are symmetric in size.  There is no hydronephrosis or nephrolithiasis.  Minimal nonspecific perinephric stranding is stable.  The stomach, duodenum, and intra-abdominal loops of small bowel are normal in caliber and configuration.  There is no small bowel distention or wall thickening.  There is no free fluid or free air in the abdomen.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal adenopathy.  The aorta is normal in caliber.  Vascular patency is not assessed without contrast.  Calcifications at the origin of the renal arteries are noted.  In the right lower quadrant, there is significant fat stranding and induration centered about the appendix, which is seen to be dilated to 11 mm.  This represents a marked change from prior study when the appendix appeared normal. There is no air seen within the appendix.  While there is significant inflammatory change, there is no adjacent free fluid or free air, and no evidence of abscess formation.  No appendicolith is identified.  The colon is unremarkable, without distention or wall thickening.  CT PELVIS: A Foley catheter is seen decompressing the bladder.  The rectum and sigmoid are unremarkable.  The prostate and seminal vesicles are normal.  Internal iliac vascular calcifications are noted.  There is no pelvic or inguinal adenopathy.  Additional vascular calcifications are seen in the femoral vessels.  BONE WINDOWS: There are no suspicious lytic or sclerotic osseous lesions.  Degenerative change in the lumbar spine, most severe at L4-L5, where an inferior endplate L4 Schmorl''s node has progressed, is again noted.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
