INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24969863',
    '10749568-RR-8',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT imaging of the abdomen and pelvis was performed with intravenous but no oral contrast.  Multiplanar reformats were prepared and reviewed.  CT ABDOMEN WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: There is linear atelectasis at the visualized lung bases.  There is no pleural or pericardial effusion.  The superior dome of the liver is excluded from the field of view.  Given this limitation, there is no change in the appearance of the liver, with multiple coarse calcifications again identified, compatible with granulomas, but no concerning hepatic focal lesions.  The gallbladder is unremarkable, without wall thickening, stones, or other CT evidence of acute cholecystitis.  There is no intra- or extra-hepatic biliary ductal dilation.  The hepatic vasculature is widely patent.  Coarse calcification is also seen within the spleen, though the spleen is otherwise unremarkable.  Pancreas enhances homogeneously, without focal mass and without ductal dilation.  The adrenal glands are notable for a stable right adrenal nodule containing macroscopic fat, compatible with a myelolipoma.  There is symmetric renal parenchymal enhancement and contrast excretion.  There is no hydronephrosis.  There are no renal mass lesions.  A nasogastric tube is seen passing into the stomach, though the sideholes are in the region of the gastroesophageal junction.  The stomach is distended, with contrast administered through the nasogastric tube seen layering dependently within.  There is also distention of the fluid-filled duodenum and proximal small bowel, measuring up to 3.8 cm.  This extends to a transition point in the left upper quadrant anteriorly, where there is a sharply angulated loop with relative distal decompression of the small bowel (301B:14).  The colon is also relatively collapsed.  Findings are concerning for small-bowel obstruction.    In the right lower quadrant, there is extensive inflammatory change identified, in keeping with prior ____.  In the mesentery, there is no loculated fluid collection identified, though there is extensive stranding and a single locule of air identified (2:58).  Additionally, there is a small amount of fluid and associated extraluminal air seen in the right lower quadrant, just anterolateral to the cecum.  This does not appear to represent a well-defined drainable fluid collection/abscess and may be post-surgical, though evolving abscess formation cannot not excluded.  There is adjacent wall thickening of the cecum, likely reactive.  There is no abdominal adenopathy.  The aorta is normal in caliber and mesenteric vessels are patent, including the superior mesenteric and portal veins.  A small amount of free air is seen superiorly in the anterior abdomen, tracking over the liver in the right upper quadrant, likely post-operative.  CT PELVIS WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: A small amount of stool and fluid are seen in the rectum.  There is sigmoid diverticulosis, without diverticulitis.  Distal ureters and bladder are normal, as are the prostate and seminal vesicles.  There is no pelvic or inguinal adenopathy.  BONE WINDOWS: There are no suspicious lytic or sclerotic osseous lesions identified.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21448873',
    '10754113-RR-20',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Helical MDCT images were acquired of the abdomen and pelvis following the uneventful administration of 130 mL of Omnipaque intravenously.  Images were reformatted into coronal and sagittal planes.  FINDINGS:   LUNG BASES: There is bibasilar atelectasis.  ABDOMEN: The liver, spleen, adrenals, pancreas, collapsed gallbladder, and kidneys appear normal.  The stomach contains ingested contents, and is otherwise unremarkable.  Loops of small bowel are normal in caliber.  There is no intraperitoneal free air.  The small bowel mesentery appears normal.  PELVIS: There is a 1.1 cm appendicolith in the tip of the appendix with surrounding inflammatory stranding.  The proximal appendix appears decompressed.  There is no drainable fluid collection or extraluminal gas.  The bladder, prostate, and rectum appear normal.  The colon is normal in appearance.  There is fecalization of distal ileum, consistent with a focal ileus due to inflammatory change.  The aorta is normal in caliber along its visualized course; its major branches appear patent.  Nonenlarged left paraaortic and left inguinal lymph nodes are noted.  BONE WINDOWS: There are no concerning lytic or blastic osseous lesions.'
);


