INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26570017',
    '10797056-RR-36',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS. Oral contrast was administered.  DOSE: DLP: 504 mGy-cm (abdomen and pelvis). IV Contrast:  130 mL Visipaque  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Lung bases are clear, without pleural effusions.  Heart size is normal, and there is no pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions. There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  Vague peripherally hyperdense structure in the gallbladder is suspicious for cholelithiasis (5b:22). The gallbladder is otherwise within normal limits, without wall thickening.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  Bilateral sub-centimeter hypodensities are too small to characterize (5b:48, 43).  There is no evidence of focal renal lesions, or hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: Stomach is decompressed.  There are several dilated loops of small bowel, measuring up to 3.2 cm in diameter.  No discrete transition point is identified.  However, there is gradual tapering towards the extensive inflammatory changes in the right lower quadrant, suggesting a functional ileus rather than a mechanical obstruction.  Severe inflammatory changes/fat stranding and edema in the right lower quadrant are centered around a severely dilated appendix measuring up to 2 cm in diameter.  It is hyperemic in appearance with areas of hypo-enhancing wall. A portion of the appendiceal wall is not visualized, and appears to be contiguous with a focus of extraluminal fluid measuring 2.2 x 1.2cm (5b:34), findings the suggestive of ruptured a gangrenous ____; no definite peripheral rim of enhancement is seen around this fluid to suggest organized abscess formation.  Focal extraluminal air is seen in this region and at the appendiceal base, without evidence of free intraperitoneal air elsewhere.  There is wall thickening of the cecum, the terminal ileum and thin adjacent traversing small bowel loop in this region which are all likely reactive.  Remainder of the colon and rectum is otherwise unremarkable.  Several mesenteric lymph nodes predominantly on the right are likely reactive (5b:35).  RETROPERITONEUM: There is no evidence of retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm. There is minimal calcium burden in the abdominal aorta and great abdominal arteries.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable. There is no evidence of pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy. Trace amount of free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The uterus is heterogeneously enlarged and distorted by numerous fibroids.  An oval-shaped low-density region more centrally (6b: 37) may represent endocervical fluid or possibly endometrial thickening.  Lippes Loop IUD is seen in the region of the endocervical canal, which appears to be located more inferiorly compared to the pelvic ultrasound in ___, and currently likely within the cervix.  BONES AND SOFT TISSUES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions.  Mild multilevel degenerative changes are seen throughout the thoracolumbar spine.  Abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26570017',
    '10797056-RR-37',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT abdomen and pelvis.  TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technqiue. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS. Oral contrast was administered.  DOSE: Total DLP (Body) = 1,057 mGy-cm. IV Contrast:  130 mL Omnipaque.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: A new, moderate right pleural effusion with adjacent atelectasis is noted.  Atelectasis is also noted within the anterior right middle lobe and left lower lobe.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is within normal limits, without stones or gallbladder wall thikening.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  Multiple tiny bilateral renal hypodensities are too small to characterize but likely cysts, the largest arising from the lower pole of the left kidney measuring 9 mm, unchanged since the ___ study.  There is no evidence of stones, focal renal lesions, or hydronephrosis.  There are no urothelial lesions in the kidneys or ureters.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: A nasogastric tube is noted terminating within the stomach. Oral contrast is seen within the stomach and proximal small bowel, without evidence of progression into the distal small bowel or colon.  Multiple contiguous loops of small bowel are fluid-filled and dilated throughout the abdomen, up to approximately 4.0 cm in diameter, increased in comparison to the ___ study.  No sharp transition point is detected, with smooth tapering of the caliber to the terminal ileum.  The proximal colon contains stool, whereas the distal descending and sigmoid colon are collapsed.  The appendix remains enlarged and inflamed (series 2, image 58), with foci of adjacent gas noted along the base of the appendix.  A small adjacent rim-enhancing fluid collection containing multiple foci of air measures 2.6 x 2.2 cm (303b:34), and is compatible with a ___ abscess at the base, smaller in comparison to the ___ examination, but now more organized.  RETROPERITONEUM: Multiple prominent mediastinal and retroperitoneal lymph nodes are again identified, likely reactive in nature.   A small amount of free fluid is noted within the left pericolic gutter and the pouch of ___.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm. There is minimal calcium burden in the abdominal aorta and great abdominal arteries.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is no evidence of pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: Redemonstrated is a fibroid uterus containing a Lippes loop IUD in the vicinity of the endocervical canal, overall unchanged in position from the prior examination.  BONES AND SOFT TISSUES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions. The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.  NOTIFICATION: Initial findings were discussed by Dr. ___ with Dr. ___ on the telephone on ___ at 8:51 ___, 2 minutes after discovery of the findings.  Updated findings were discussed by Dr. ___ with Dr. ___ on the telephone on ___ at 8:00 AM.'
);


