VALUES (
    '29468247',
    '10958320-RR-28',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis were displayed with 5-mm slice thickness with oral and 130 cc Optiray intravenous contrast.  Coronally and sagittally reformatted images were displayed for evaluation.  CT ABDOMEN: The visualized portions of the lung bases are clear.  There is no pleural or pericardial effusion. The liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, and bilateral adrenal glands are unremarkable.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast promptly.  The appendix is mildly dilated to 9 mm with minimal wall thickening but no periappendiceal stranding. There is air and fluid within the lumen, but no oral contrast is seen.  The small and remainder of the large bowel are normal in course and caliber.  There is no free air and no free fluid within the abdomen.  The aorta is normal. No mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy is present.  CT PELVIS: The rectum, sigmoid colon, bladder and adnexa are unremarkable.  The uterus is retroflexed and normal.  Trace free fluid within the pelvis is likely physiologic.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOWS: No sclerotic or lytic lesion suspicious for malignancy are seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25188622',
    '10963257-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images of the abdomen and pelvis were obtained after administration of IV contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were also submitted for interpretation.  CT ABDOMEN WITH CONTRAST: The lung bases demonstrate minimal dependent atelectasis.  The heart is not enlarged.  There is no pericardial effusion. The liver, spleen, adrenals, pancreas, kidneys are normal.  Cholelithiasis with no evidence of pericholecystic fluid is seen.  There is no bowel obstruction.  Abdominal aorta and iliac vessels are unremarkable.  There is no lymphadenopathy.  PELVIC CT WITH CONTRAST: The appendix is dilated with hyperemic wall measuring up to 12 mm in diameter with adjacent extensive inflammatory changes and small amount of fluid.  Minimal cecal wall thickening is noted. The uterus is unremarkable. There is a cystic lesion measuring up to 1.8 cm in the right adnexa, likely an ovarian cyst. There is a round fatty lesion adjacent to the sigmoid colon on the left side, image #73, series 2, which may represent epiploic appendage with layering sarrounding fluid. The urinary bladder is moderately collapsed and contains small amount of contrast material.  A 1.3 cm hypodense round midline structure at the location of the urethra-vagina is seen.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: No fracture is seen.  Sclerotic appearance of the left side of the sacrum may be degenerative.  There is also osteophyte formation at the right sacroiliac joint, likely degenerative.  Mild degenerative changes of the visualized spine is noted.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29697525',
    '10964841-RR-16',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images were obtained from the base of the lungs to pubic symphysis after administration of 130 mL of Optiray nonionic intravenous contrast.  Multiplanar reformatted images were prepared.  FINDINGS: The appendix appears distended up to 8 mm with surrounding fat stranding.  There is no evidence of rupture or fluid collections.  There is no free air or free fluid throughout the abdomen (2A:51, 52, 601B:29, 602B:34).  Otherwise, the remainder of the stomach and visualized loops of small and large bowel are within normal limits.  There is mild bibasilar atelectasis in the visualized portions of the lungs.  Otherwise, the lungs are clear.  The liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, bilateral adrenal glands, and bilateral kidneys are within normal limits.  No free fluid or free air throughout the abdomen.  No mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber and contour. Of incidental note is a retroaortic left renal vein.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The bladder, distal ureters, uterus, ovaries, sigmoid colon, and rectum are within normal limits.  No free fluid or free air throughout the pelvis.  No pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy. Ovaries appear normal.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: No suspicious lytic or sclerotic osseous lesions.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29175678',
    '10965345-RR-54',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS W/O CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis were displayed with 5-mm slice thickness with oral contrast only.  Coronal and sagittal reformats were displayed with 5-mm slice thickness.  CT ABDOMEN: A calcified granuloma is seen in the right lower lobe (2:8).  The lungs are otherwise clear.  There is no pleural effusion.  A small pericardial effusion is unchanged from ___.  Fat stranding centered around the appendix is noted with mild dilatation of the appendiceal tip to 8 mm.  There is slight thickening of the cecal base.  No contrast or air are seen within the appendix despite contrast in the cecum. There is no drainable fluid collection and no free air.  Tiny lymph nodes in the region are prominent but not pathologically enlarged by CT size criteria.  Evaluation of the intra-abdominal organs is limited without intravenous contrast.  Within this limitation, the liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, and bilateral adrenal glands are normal. The kidneys appear normal without nephrolithiasis or hydronephrosis. The small and large bowel are normal in course and caliber. No mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  Atherosclerotic calcifications are seen in the aorta without aneurysmal dilatation.  CT PELVIS: The rectum, sigmoid colon, bladder, and prostate are normal.  No free fluid and no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOWS: No lytic or sclerotic lesion suspicious for malignancy is seen. Exostosis is noted at the left lesser trochanter.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20412708',
    '10984267-RR-11',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Axial helical MDCT images were obtained from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis after administration of IV and oral contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were generated.  DLP: 415.36 mGy-cm  CTDI: 7.65 mGy  FINDINGS: The lung bases are clear and the visualized heart and pericardium are unremarkable.  CT ABDOMEN: The liver enhances homogeneously, without focal lesions or intrahepatic biliary duct dilatation.  The gallbladder is unremarkable and the portal vein is patent.  The pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands are within normal limits.  The kidneys show symmetric nephrograms and excretion of contrast.  There is no focal renal lesion or hydronephrosis bilaterally.    The small and large bowel are within normal limits, without evidence of wall thickening or dilatation to suggest obstruction.  The appendix appears inflamed, with significant mucosal enhancement and increased in caliber at its base measuring 10 mm (2:59).  There is some stranding surrounding the base of the cecum and appendix as well as a small amount of supra-apendicular fluid (series 2 image 57).  There is no evidence of intra-abdominal free air or localized fluid collection.  The aorta and its main branches are patent and nonaneurysmal.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymph node enlargement by CT size criteria.  There is no abdominal wall hernia.  CT PELVIS: The urinary bladder and ureters are unremarkable.  The uterus and adnexa are unremarkable. There is no pelvic wall or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  No pelvic free fluid is observed.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There are no lytic or blastic lesions concerning for malignancy.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29775414',
    '10986405-RR-19',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'PELVIS, NON-OBSTETRIC',
    'TECHNIQUE: Transabdominal and transvaginal ultrasound.  FINDINGS: The right ovary measures 4.5 x 1.4 x 1.2 cm and left ovary measures 1.3 x 2.8 x 1.4 cm.  Several follicles are seen within the left ovary as well as pelvic fluid surrounding it. Of note, no waveforms are obtained on either ovary because the patient could not tolerate the pain.  The endometrium measures 5 mm and is unremarkable.  The right lower quadrant was scanned at the patient''s most tender site in the abdomen.  At this site, there was a tubular structure visualized which may be blind ending, but it is hard to definitively say if this is the appendix.  It measures 6 mm in diameter.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29775414',
    '10986405-RR-20',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Helically acquired MDCT axial images were obtained from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis.  IV and oral contrasts were used.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were prepared.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN: Please note, poor oral contrast intake limits this study. There is periportal edema as well as pericholystic edema, however the gallbladder itself appears unremarkable with no gallbladder wall edema.   The lung bases and imaged portions of the cardiomediastinum appear unremarkable.  The spleen, bilateral kidneys, bilateral adrenal glands, and pancreas all appear unremarkable.  No bowel obstruction or bowel wall thickening is seen.  The appendix is visualized (601B:20). Within it there are appendicoliths. There is no definite peiappendiceal stranding, but there is limited abdominal fat in this area. The diameter of the appendix is top normal (6 mm).  There is no appreciable mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  CT OF THE PELVIS: Trace, physiologic free fluid is seen.  Uterus and adnexa appear unremarkable.  The bladder and rectum along with loops of bowel within the pelvis appear unremarkable.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: No concerning osseous lesions are identified.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27890040',
    '11012879-RR-17',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT acquired axial imaging of the abdomen and pelvis was performed after administration of oral and intravenous contrast.  Multiplanar reformatted images were obtained and reviewed.  CT ABDOMEN: Mild dependent atelectasis is seen at the lung bases.  Multiple small rounded hypodensities are seen scattered throughout the liver.  The largest, measuring 9 mm.  Adjacent to the right portal vein (2, 15), is likely a cyst, but others are too small to definitively characterize.  Gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, and adrenal glands are normal.  Kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically.  There is no hydronephrosis.  Multiple bilateral renal cysts are seen, the largest a 2.7 cm exophytic cyst off the mid pole of the left kidney.  Scattered bilateral renal hypodensities likely also represent cysts, but are too small to definitively characterize.  Multiple borderline dilated loops of small bowel, with multiple air-fluid levels are noted.  The majority of the ingested oral contrast remains within the stomach.  A transition point is seen in the right lower abdomen (2, 52) at which point distal small bowel is decompressed, and unopacified.  There is a moderate amount of edema within the abdominal mesentery of the lower abdomen.  CT PELVIS: The appendix is markedly abnormal, with a calcific density seen at the appendiceal base, consistent with an appendicolith.  Distal to this, the appendix is dilated up to 11 mm, and near the tip of the appendix, a second smaller appendicolith is seen (2, 59).  There is extensive periappendiceal inflammatory change.  No definite sign of perforation is seen.  However, there is a small amount of free fluid in the right lower quadrant adjacent to the cecum, and a small calcific extraluminal density (2, 54).  Pelvic loops of small bowel are abnormal and fluid-filled, with moderate bowel wall thickening.  There is prominent edema in the abdominal mesentery. Similar edema and inflammatory wall thickening is seen in the sigmoid colon, which is situated directly adjacent to the tip of the appendix (2, 59).  There is a small amount of free pelvic fluid.  There is no pathologically enlarged pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  There is no osseous lesions suspicious for malignancy.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
