    'US APPENDIX',
    'EXAMINATION: US APPENDIX:  TECHNIQUE: Targeted gray scale and color Doppler ultrasound images of the abdomen were obtained.  FINDINGS: In the area of pain in the right lower quadrant is a dilated tubular blind-ending structure measuring up to 7 mm in diameter, with a mildly thickened wall. This structure does not compress with transducer pressure.  No adjacent fluid collection is demonstrated. There is no significant hyperemia demonstrated.  NOTIFICATION: These findings were discussed with the surgery attending, Dr. ___, in person, during the study.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21106427',
    '11095671-RR-12',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were obtained through the abdomen and pelvis with the administration of IV contrast.  Multiplanar reformats were generated and reviewed.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS: The visualized lung bases are clear with no focal consolidation or pleural effusion.  The visualized heart and pericardium are unremarkable.  The liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, bilateral adrenal glands appear unremarkable.  Both kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically without evidence of hydronephrosis or renal calculi.  The intra-abdominal vasculature is unremarkable.  There is no free air or free fluid within the abdomen.  Retroperitoneal and mesenteric lymph nodes do not meet CT size criteria for pathology.  Intra-abdominal and intrapelvic loops of small bowel appear unremarkable.  The appendix appears fluid-filled, measuring up to 10 mm with minimal mucosal hyperemia and with mild periappendiceal fat stranding consistent with ____.  The bladder appears unremarkable.  The endometrial cavity of the uterus shows minimal fluid.  A left adnexal cyst is identified.  Pelvic lymph nodes do not meet size criteria for pathology.  There is no free pelvic fluid.  Visualized osseous structures show no focal lytic or sclerotic lesions suspicious for malignancy.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '22040308',
    '11130122-RR-16',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis were displayed with 5-mm slice thickness with intravenous contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformats were displayed with 5-mm slice thickness.  CT ABDOMEN: The visualized lung bases demonstrate mild bibasilar linear atelectasis.  There is no pleural or pericardial effusion.  The liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas and bilateral adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast promptly without hydronephrosis.  Small hypodensities in the left kidney are too small to characterize and statistically most likely represent cysts. The small and large bowel are normal in course and caliber without obstruction.  Within the right lower quadrant, there is a large amount of soft tissue stranding with an organizing fluid collection which measures 11.6 x 4.4 cm, and free intraperitoneal. The proximal appendix is dilated to 22mm (2:57), ending at a pair of small hyperdense foci suggesting appendicoliths (2:59). The appendix beyond this point is not discrete and instead ends in the large collection. The constellation of findings are compatible with ruptured ____.  The aorta is of normal caliber throughout without atherosclerotic change.  The main portal vein, splenic vein and SMV are patent. Small lymph nodes in the right lower quadrant measuring up to 11 mm (2:50) are likely reactive.  CT PELVIS: The rectum, sigmoid colon, bladder and prostate are normal.  Vas deferens calcifications are noted.  There are phleboliths in the pelvis.  There is no inguinal or pelvic lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOWS: No bone finding suspicious for infection or malignancy is seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25172248',
    '11174843-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'STUDY: CT of the abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast; 130 cc of Optiray intravenous contrast was administered without adverse reaction or complication.  Oral contrast was also administered.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were also generated.  FINDINGS:   ABDOMEN: The visualized lung base did show minimal dependent atelectasis.  The liver shows no focal lesion or intrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder shows no stones or wall edema.  Spleen is normal in size and appearance.  The pancreas and adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance with and excrete contrast symmetrically without evidence of obstruction.  The small and large bowels show no evidence of obstruction.  The appendix is visualized and measures up to 15 mm in diameter, is hyperenhancing, and has extensive inflammation surrounding it.  Scattered mesenteric lymph nodes are seen.  There is no organized fluid collection.  There is no free air.  PELVIS: The bladder, prostate and rectum appear unremarkable.  There is no free fluid or lymphadenopathy.  BONES: There are no aggressive-appearing lytic or sclerotic lesions.  Old left transverse process fractures are seen in the lower lumbar vertebrae.'
);


