VALUES (
    '27654342',
    '11426173-RR-14',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS. No oral contrast was administered.  DOSE: Total DLP = 976 mGy-cm. IV Contrast:  130 mL Omnipaque  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Lung bases demonstrate dependent atelectasis.  There is no pleural or pericardial effusion.  Calcified granuloma is noted in the right lung base.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions. There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation. The gallbladder is within normal limits, without stones or gallbladder wall thickening.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There is no evidence of stones, focal renal lesions, or hydronephrosis. There are no urothelial lesions in the kidneys or ureters. There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness and enhancement throughout. Colon and rectum are within normal limits. The appendix is fluid-filled and dilated measuring up to 1.8 cm with adjacent fat stranding, consistent with ____.  There is no adjacent fluid collection or evidence of perforation.  There is no evidence of mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  RETROPERITONEUM: Prominent left periaortic lymph nodes are likely reactive in etiology given underlying ____ (02:44).  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm. There is no calcium burden in the abdominal aorta and great abdominal arteries.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable. There is no evidence of pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy. There is no free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: Reproductive organs are within normal limits.  BONES AND SOFT TISSUES: There is no evidence of worrisome lesions. Abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20609633',
    '11441212-RR-8',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multi detector CT of the abdomen pelvis was performed following IV contrast administration.  No oral contrast was administered.  Multiplanar reformations were provided.  FINDINGS: The imaged lung bases are clear.  The imaged portion of the heart is unremarkable.   Abdomen:  Liver enhances normally without focal lesion.  The gallbladder and pancreas are normal.  The spleen is normal.  Both adrenal glands have a normal appearance.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete promptly.  The abdominal aorta is normal in course and caliber without appreciable atherosclerosis.  There is no retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.   The stomach and duodenum appear normal.    Pelvis:  Loops of small bowel demonstrate no signs of ileus or obstruction.  Within the right lower quadrant, there is an abnormal appearance of the appendix.  There is a appendicular at the base of the appendix and the appendix is dilated throughout its visualized course and measures up to 14 mm. Additional stones are seen within the lumen of the appendix.  Periappendiceal fat stranding is present without definite signs of perforation.  No abscess is seen.  The large bowel contains a mild fecal load.  There is no bowel obstruction.  The uterus and bilateral adnexal structures appear unremarkable. Please note the tip of the appendix does abut the right ovary.  The urinary bladder is distended with bilateral ureteral jets demonstrated.  Trace free fluid could be physiologic.    Bones:  No worrisome bony lesions are seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20078211',
    '11450072-RR-2',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Axial CT images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following the uneventful intravenous administration of 130 mL of Optiray contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were also reviewed.  CT ABDOMEN WITH CONTRAST: The lung bases and cardiac apex are normal.  The liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, adrenal glands, kidneys are normal.  There is no free gas or fluid in the upper abdomen.  Regional vascular structures are normal.  There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  CT PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: The urinary bladder, distal ureters, prostate, seminal vesicles, rectum and colon are normal.  The appendix is dilated, most notably near its tip where it measures 13 mm in diameter (300B:29).  Additionally, it is notable for mucosal hyperenhancement, is fluid filled and note is also made of surrounding fat stranding and thickening of the adjacent fascia.  There is no free gas or fluid in the pelvis.  There is no evidence of appendiceal rupture or appendicolith.  There is no pelvic sidewall or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  OSSEOUS FINDINGS: There is no suspicious sclerotic or lytic osseous lesion.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '22765190',
    '11458481-RR-3',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Serial transverse images were acquired sequentially through the abdomen and pelvis following the uneventful administration of 130 mL of Optiray 350.  Oral contrast was also administered.  Multiplanar reformatted images were generated.  FINDINGS: The lung bases are clear without consolidation, edema, or effusion. The heart is normal in size with no pericardial effusion.  The solid abdominal organs are grossly unremarkable.  Bilateral kidneys enhance symmetrically with no hydronephrosis.  There is mild periportal edema, likely due to fluid resuscitation.  The gallbladder is present but otherwise unremarkable with no gallstones or pericholecystic inflammation noted.  The stomach and proximal small bowel are distended with contrast.  The mid and distal small bowel are largely collapsed with no dilatation or wall thickening evident.  There is a markedly inflamed appendix to approximately 11 mm with hyperenhancing walls and an internal appendicolith situated at the appendiceal base.  Scattered stool is seen throughout the proximal colon.  The more distal colon is largely collapsed.  No diverticular disease is evident.  A trace amount of free fluid is noted in the pelvic cul-de-sac.  Otherwise, no free intraperitoneal fluid or air noted elsewhere.  Mild stranding and thickening of the fascia is noted in the right paracolic gutter.  No pathologic lymphadenopathy is identified within the abdomen or pelvis.  The abdominopelvic vascular structures are unremarkable.  The urinary bladder is incompletely distended with no wall thickening or internal filling defects noted.  The uterus and bilateral ovaries are grossly unremarkable.  No suspicious osseous lesions are noted.  Normal thoracolumbar spinal alignment is maintained.  There are no fractures.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21415312',
    '11463611-RR-8',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS CONTRAST.:  TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. IV Contrast:  130 mL Omnipaque. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Total DLP (Body) = 930 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: There is mild bibasilar atelectasis.  There is no pericardial or pleural effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  A hypodensity adjacent to the falciform ligament is likely perfusional (series 2, image 15).  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is within normal limits.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There is no evidence of focal renal lesions or hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The distal esophagus is unremarkable without a hiatal hernia.  The stomach is decompressed.  The small bowel is normal in caliber without focal wall thickening.  The large bowel is also normal in caliber without focal wall thickening.  The appendix is dilated up to 12 mm with proximal mucosal hyperenhancement and surrounding fat stranding (series 602b, image 27; series 2, image 64).  There is a focal 13 x 13 mm fluid-filled structure in the region of the appendiceal tip, thought to represent the tip of the appendix itself.  There is however subtle focal hypo enhancement along its medial margin (series 601 B, image 26).  There is no extraluminal air.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is trace free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The reproductive organs are unremarkable.  LYMPH NODES: There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Mild atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20864233',
    '11469232-RR-12',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multi-detector CT imaging was performed from the thoracic inlet to the pubic symphysis following the administration of 130 cc Omnipaque intravenous contrast and enteric contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were generated and reviewed.  FINDINGS: The bases of the lungs are clear.  The visualized heart is normal in size and without pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN: The liver demonstrates a 1.1 x 1.0 cm of focal fat deposition within the anterior right hepatic lobe adjacent to the falciform ligament.  The portal venous system is patent.  No intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation is seen.  The gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, and bilateral adrenal glands are within normal limits.  Both kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast normally without evidence of hydronephrosis or solid renal mass.    The stomach, duodenum, and intra-abdominal loops of small and large bowel are normal in caliber without evidence of wall thickening or obstruction.  Within the pelvis the distal appendix is thickened and discontinuous, with punctate adjacent foci of air suggestive of perforation (2:66-72).  In addition, there is regional peritonitis, phlegmonous changes, and reactive serosal inflammation of the adjacent sigmoid colon.  No drainable fluid collection is noted. The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber throughout.  The celiac artery and SMA are patent.  The right gonadal vein appears asymmetrically enlarged and hypodense relative to the left, suggestive of thrombosis.  The urinary bladder is grossly unremarkable in appearance.  BONES: No osseous destructive lesions concerning for malignancy are detected.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20905237',
    '11470408-RR-33',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired contiguous images of the abdomen and pelvis were obtained with intravenous and oral contrast at 5-mm slice thickness.  Coronally and sagittally reformatted images were displayed.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN: Bibasilar dependent atelectasis is noted in imaged lung bases, which are otherwise clear.  There is no pleural effusion.  Heart size is top normal with marked aortic valve calcifications.  There is a dilated fluid-filled appendix measuring up to 10 mm in diameter, which displays hyperemic wall and extensive periappendiceal fat stranding.  There is associated wall thickening of the base of the cecum, which is likely reactive in nature.  There is no evidence of abscess formation or perforation at this time.  The liver enhances homogeneously.  There are multiple focal hepatic hypodensities in segment VIII (2:18), segment V (2:35), segment VII (2:11), which are too small to characterize and likely represent cysts or hamartomas.  Hepatic vasculature is patent.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary duct dilatation.  Gallbladder is not distended without gallbladder wall thickening or pericholecystic fluid collection.  There are no calcified gallstones within its lumen.  The spleen is unremarkable.  The pancreas enhances homogeneously without ductal dilatation or peripancreatic fluid collection.  Adrenal glands are unremarkable.  Kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically without evidence of hydronephrosis or renal masses.  Focal bilateral renal hypodensities are too small to characterize and likely represent cysts.  Imaged small and large bowel loops are normal in caliber without evidence of bowel wall thickening or obstruction.  Intra-abdominal aorta and its branches are notable for calcified atherosclerotic disease and are patent.  Mild ectasia of infrarenal aorta is apparent.  There are no pathologically enlarged mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymph nodes.  There is no free air or free fluid within the abdomen.  A small hiatal hernia is noted.  CT OF THE PELVIS: Bladder, distal ureters, seminal vesicles, and rectum are unremarkable.  There are numerous diverticula within the sigmoid colon without associated inflammatory changes.  There is no free air or free fluid within the pelvis.  There are no pathologically enlarged parapelvic or inguinal lymph nodes.  Small bilateral fat-containing inguinal hernias are noted.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: No suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesion is seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
