INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29866037',
    '11597556-RR-11',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images were obtained from the base of the lungs to the pubic symphysis after the administration of IV and oral contrast. Multiplanar reformatted images were prepared and reviewed.  FINDINGS:   CT ABDOMEN WITH IV AND ORAL CONTRAST: The visualized lung bases demonstrate minimal dependent atelectatic changes.  The visualized heart is normal in size without a pericardial effusion.    The appendix is borderline in size measuring 7.0 to 8.0 mm (601b:20).  There is no significant surrounding fat stranding, but the wall of the tip may be hyperemic with trace fluid content.  Oral contrast has reached the ileum but not the cecum and appendix; hence, evaluation for contrast filling is not possible.    Additionally, there is minimal wall thickening involving the jejunum (2:47, 60ab:21) with mild mesenteric hyperemia raising suspicious for a separate or coinciding inflammatory process; this could be seen with mild jejunitis or mild regional inflammatory changes associated with early ____ in the appropriate setting.    Otherwise, the liver, gallbladder, spleen, bilateral adrenal glands, bilateral kidneys, pancreas, and stomach are within normal limits.    The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber and contour.  There is no mesenteric or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  There is no free fluid or free air.  CT PELVIS WITH IV AND ORAL CONTRAST: The bladder is distended with fluid.  The prostate, rectum and sigmoid colon are within normal limits.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  There is no free fluid or free air in the pelvis.  BONE WINDOWS: There are no lytic or sclerotic osseous lesions suspicious for malignancy.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21101590',
    '11617428-RR-18',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were obtained from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis after the administration of intravenous contrast.  Axial images were interpreted in conjunction with coronal and sagittal reformats.  DLP: 533.51 mGy-cm  FINDINGS: The visualized lung bases are clear. The heart is normal in size and there is no evidence of pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN: The liver is normal in appearance and without focal abnormality. The portal venous system is patent. There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation. The gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, and bilateral adrenal glands are normal. The kidneys enhance symmetrically and are without suspicious solid mass.  The stomach is grossly unremarkable in appearance. The small and large bowel are normal in caliber and without evidence of wall thickening. The appendix is fluid-filled and dilated up to approximately 1.0 cm.  The appendiceal tip appears enlarged and somewhat indistinct (2:65), making early or impending rupture difficult to exclude. There is no retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy by CT size criteria. There is no free abdominal fluid or pneumoperitoneum. The aorta and iliac branches are normal in course and caliber. The celiac trunk and SMA are grossly patent.  PELVIS: The bladder, sigmoid colon, and rectum are grossly unremarkable. There is no pelvic side-wall or inguinal lymphadenopathy by CT size criteria. No free pelvic fluid is identified.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: A sclerotic focus within the left ilium (2:70) likely represents a bone island.  NOTIFICATION: Findings were conveyed by Dr. ___ to Dr. ___ telephone at 23:55 on ___.'
);


