INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27667118',
    '11783205-RR-14',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Contrast-enhanced axial multidetector CT images through the abdomen and pelvis with coronal and sagittal reformats.  DLP: 476 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: Partially imaged lung bases are clear.  There is no pleural effusion.  CT ABDOMEN: The liver enhances homogeneously without concerning lesions or biliary dilatation.  Portal vein is patent.  The gallbladder, spleen, pancreas and adrenal glands are unremarkable.  Kidneys enhance and excrete symmetrically without concerning lesions or hydronephrosis.  Stomach is distended with ingested material and non-dilated loops of small bowel are normal in course and caliber without wall thickening or sign of obstruction.  Colon is within normal limits.  A large appendicolith measuring approximately 12 mm is present at the base of the appendix resulting in a fluid-filled appendix that is dilated up to 1.2 cm with mild adjacent fat stranding.  A second appendicolith is also present more distally.  There is no evidence of perforation at this time.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  There is no intra-abdominal free air or fluid.  Bladder is partially decompressed.  Seminal vesicles and prostate gland are unremarkable.  There is no pelvic free fluid or lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOW: No concerning osteolytic or sclerotic lesion identified.  Abdominal aorta is of normal caliber without aneurysmal dilatation.  Portal vein, superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein are also well opacified.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26189755',
    '11803545-RR-12',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were obtained from the bases of the lung to the pubis symphysis after administration of IV and p.o. contrast.  The patient did not tolerate p.o. contrast well and despite premedicated with anti-emetics.  Multiplanar reformatted images were obtained for evaluation.  FINDINGS:   CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: The lung bases are clear.  The liver is normal without focal lesions.  The gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands, and kidneys are normal.  There is prompt exclusion of IV contrast into the collecting system.  No hydronephrosis is noted.  The stomach and duodenum are normal.  Mild-to-moderate amount of ascites is noted in the perihepatic and perisplenic regions, and also dispersed in the mesentery.  There is no free air or lymphadenopathy in the intra-abdominal cavity.  CT PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: In image 2:58, there is a focal dense material, adjacent to a speck of air.  Given the proximity to the cecum, this likely represent an appendicolith.  There is a distended tubular structure containing this appendicolith, measuring 14 mm in diameter, likely represent a fluid-filled appendix.  There is no evidence of extraluminal air or periappendiceal fluid collection. There is however ascites and periotoneal enhancment. Of note, there are loops of fluid-filled small bowel in the vicinity, which are distinct from this fluid-filled appendix.  Oral contrast is only seen reaching the mid jejunum.  No small bowel abnormality is noted.  The uterus is normal in size.  There is normal follicular activity in the adnexae.  A mild-to-moderate amount of free fluid is noted in the deep pelvis.  BONE WINDOW: No suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesions are noted.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24095893',
    '11815394-RR-52',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT data were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis after the uneventful administration of intravenous contrast. Images were displayed in multiple planes.  DOSE: DLP:  536.79 mGy-cm  FINDINGS: A 4 mm lung nodule in the right lung base is stable in size and appearance since ___. There is no pleural or pericardial effusion.  CT ABDOMEN: The liver enhances homogeneously. There are no focal liver lesions. The portal and hepatic veins are patent. There is no intra or extrahepatic biliary dilatation. The gallbladder is normal.  The pancreas enhances homogeneously. The spleen and adrenal glands are normal. Kidneys enhance and excrete contrast promptly. There are no concerning renal lesions. There is no retroperitoneal or abdominal adenopathy. No ascites is present. The aorta and its major branches are patent and not dilated. The stomach and intra-abdominal loops of bowel are normal caliber and appearance.  The appendix is dilated, measuring up to 12 mm, fluid filled and contains an appendicolith. There is no adjacent fluid collection or free air.  CT PELVIS: The remainder of the bowel is normal. The bladder is normal. There is no free pelvic fluid.  There is no inguinal or pelvic adenopathy.  NOTIFICATION: The findings were discussed by Dr. ___ with Dr. ___ on the telephone on ___ at 7:30 ___, 2 minutes after discovery of the findings.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26374326',
    '11829290-RR-14',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'STUDY: CT of the abdomen and pelvis with IV and oral contrast; coronal and sagittal reformatted images were also generated.  130 cc of Omnipaque intravenous contrast was given without adverse reaction.  FINDINGS:   ABDOMEN: The visualized portions of the lung bases are clear.  The liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas, and adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance with and excrete contrast symmetrically without evidence of hydronephrosis.  There is no evidence of small or large bowel obstruction.  There is no lymphadenopathy, free air, or free fluid.  PELVIS: The bladder, uterus, and rectum appear unremarkable.  There is trace free fluid but no lymphadenopathy.  In the retrocecal region, the appendix is identified.  While oral contrast is seen entering the early portion of the appendix, more distally, the appendix is noted to be fluid filled and enlarged, measuring between 8-10 mm (2; 49 and 601B; 28).  This portion of the appendiceal tip appears to be hyperenhancing, although there is minimal surrounding fat stranding.  A few prominent mesenteric lymph nodes are also noted in the right lower quadrant (601B; 29)  BONES: There are no aggressive-appearing lytic or sclerotic lesions.'
);


