INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24438918',
    '11956695-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Axially acquired images were obtained from lung bases to the pubic symphysis after the administration of 130 mL of Optiray intravenous contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were also displayed.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN: The lung bases demonstrate minimal dependent atelectasis.  The heart size is normal.  The spleen, adrenal glands, kidneys, and intra-abdominal loops of bowel are within normal limits.  Small hypodensities are noted within the right and left lobes of the liver, too small to characterize.  Liver is otherwise normal in appearance.  There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  No free air or free fluid is present.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The rectum and bladder are within normal limits.  There is a left-sided adnexal cyst measuring 2.3 x 2.3 cm, most consistent with normal ovarian follicular activity.  The appendix is dilated and fluid-filled measuring up to 1.2 cm in diameter, consistent with ____.  There is no perforation or abscess formation.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOWS: Irregular lucent lesion with irregular sclerotic rim and cortical thinning is noted in the proximal right femur, incompletely assessed. The portion abutting the femoral neck has a geographic margin but portions more inferior to this appear more irregular.  There is no evidence of periosteal new bone formation or soft tissue component on these images. There is partial sacralization of L5 on the left.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '22649638',
    '11963670-RR-24',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'CT OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS WITH ORAL AND INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST, ___:   TECHNIQUE: After the uneventful administration of oral and intravenous contrast, CT of the abdomen and pelvis was performed using 5-mm contiguous slice thickness.  Coronal and sagittal reformats were created for better anatomic delineation.  There were no prior studies available for comparison.  FINDINGS:   ABDOMEN: Lung bases are clear.  The liver, spleen, pancreas, adrenals, and gallbladder are normal in appearance.  There are two hypoattenuating lesions within the right kidney which are too small to be fully characterized.  However, the kidneys are otherwise unremarkable in appearance.  There is no hydronephrosis or definite stone.  Intra-abdominal bowel loops are normal in caliber.  There is no abdominal lymphadenopathy by size criteria.  The abdominal aorta is normal in course and caliber.  Its major branches are widely patent.  PELVIS: The appendix is dilated, measuring a maximum up to 8-9 mm.  The proximal portion appears somewhat collapsed, but has an avidly enhancing wall. More distally, towards the tip, the appendix appears distended and fluid filled.  Additionally, there is wall irregularity.  Along its superior margin, there is what appears to be a localized outpouching measuring approximately 8 x 8 mm.  This is of uncertain etiology.  It may represent a focal localized perforation or sequela of chronic ____.  There is no drainable periappendiceal abscess.  There is no free air underneath the diaphragm.  There is trace free fluid within the pelvis, a not uncommon finding in menstruating women.  Uterus is grossly normal in appearance.  There is no bowel obstruction.  There is no pelvic lymphadenopathy by size criteria.  Bladder is unremarkable.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There are no suspicious osseous lesions.'
);


