VALUES (
    '27121829',
    '10120826-RR-22',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Oral contrast was administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Stationary Acquisition 5.0 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 24.1 mGy (Body) DLP = 12.0 mGy-cm.    2) Spiral Acquisition 5.0 s, 55.0 cm; CTDIvol = 15.5 mGy (Body) DLP = 850.4 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 862 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Visualized lung fields are within normal limits. There is no evidence of pleural or pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver is homogeneously hypodense, consistent with hepatic steatosis.  There is no evidence of focal lesions.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is within normal limits.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The multiple perisplenic varices are noted, with evidence of a splenorenal shunt.  Spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  Multiple hypodense cysts are seen in the bilateral kidneys.  The largest cyst in the upper pole of the right kidney measures 6.2 x 5.6 cm.  A smaller cyst in the interpolar region of the right kidney measures 4.4 x 3.3 cm, and may contain an enhancing internal septation, though evaluation is limited on this examination (02:40).  Multiple simple cysts are seen on the left as well.  A likely complex cyst is seen in the interpolar region of the left kidney as well, measuring 4.7 x 3.8 cm, with a possibly enhancing internal septation as well (02:35).  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The stomach is unremarkable aside from a tiny hiatal hernia. Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout.  The colon and rectum are within normal limits.  The appendix is prominent, measuring up to 9 mm in diameter, with possible, minimal hyperemia of the wall.  There is no surrounding fat stranding or fluid.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is no free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: Multiple pretty therapy seeds are seen within the prostate.  The seminal vesicles are normal.  LYMPH NODES: A prominent portacaval lymph node is noted, measuring up to 1 cm in short axis.  There is no other definite retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Moderate atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: Multilevel degenerative changes are seen throughout the thoracolumbar spine without vertebral body height loss or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29409510',
    '10158488-RR-24',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT of the abdomen and pelvis.  TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence: 1) Stationary Acquisition 0.5 s, 1.0 cm; CTDIvol = 1.2 mGy (Body) DLP = 1.2 mGy-cm. 2) Stationary Acquisition 3.5 s, 1.0 cm; CTDIvol = 8.1 mGy (Body) DLP = 8.1 mGy-cm. 3) Spiral Acquisition 12.8 s, 44.0 cm; CTDIvol = 8.6 mGy (Body) DLP = 365.0 mGy-cm. Total DLP (Body) = 388 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: Minor dependent changes are found at each lung base, left greater than right.  There is a lesion of intermediate density along the anterior margin of the left lateral segments of the liver measuring 17 mm, probable hemangioma.  A hypodense lesion measuring 19 mm along the posterior right lobe of the liver may also represent a hemangioma but is not well characterized and does not show well-defined boundaries on this study.  There is no biliary dilatation.  The gall bladder appears normal.  The pancreas is unremarkable.  Spleen is normal in size and appearance.  Adrenals appear normal.  There is no evidence for stones or hydronephrosis involving either kidney.  A lesion with bulk fat in the mid-upper pole of the right kidney measures up to 18 mm and is consistent with a small angiomyolipoma.  There is a very small hiatal hernia.  The stomach is nondistended.  There is mild nondisplaced specific distension, but not substantial dilatation of small bowel leading up to the distal ileum.  There is a short segment of largely collapsed ileum (06:18) with wall thickening up to 7 mm in diameter including a target appearance with central intramural edema or fluid.  This is followed by a short-segment of normal bowel and then a thickened terminal ileum (06:20 and adjacent images).  Fluid content to the cecum through mid through hepatic flexure is mildly prominent without wall thickening.  The appendix at terminates in a fluid collection in the right lower quadrant measuring up to 37 x 33 mm in axial ___ (5:62) by 30 mm in height, containing air and fluid.  A direct communication between the dilated appendiceal tip and the collection can be seen (06:22).  Small bowel wall thickening in the area can be explained as a result of associated regional peritonitis.  Uterus is apparently absent.  There is no adnexal mass.  There is slight reactive bladder wall thickening along the right-side of the bladder also consistent with regional peritonitis.  Atherosclerotic changes moderate.  Major vascular structures are widely patent.  There is no lymphadenopathy.  Bones appear demineralized.  There are no suspicious bone lesions.  Moderate compression deformity of the L2 vertebral body is likely chronic.  Moderate to severe degenerative changes affect the lumbar spine.'
);


