    '11418296-RR-4',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Multidetector CT of the abdomen and pelvis was performed with IV contrast only.  Multiplanar reformations were provided.  DOSE: Total DLP (Body) = 385 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LUNG BASES: The imaged lung bases are clear.  The imaged portion of the heart is unremarkable.  ABDOMEN: Hepatobiliary:  The liver enhances normally.  No focal liver lesions seen.  There is mild periportal edema likely secondary to aggressive volume resuscitation.  The main portal vein is patent.  No biliary ductal dilation is seen.  The gallbladder is normal. Spleen:  Normal. Adrenals:  Normal. Pancreas:  Normal. Kidneys:  The kidneys enhance normally.  No hydronephrosis, signs of pyelonephritis or worrisome renal lesion.  No perinephric abnormality.  Mild fullness of the bilateral renal collecting systems likely reflects is significant bladder distention. Aorta:  Normal. Retroperitoneum: No adenopathy or hematoma. Stomach: Normal.  GI: Small bowel appears mostly decompressed through the level of the distal ileum.  There is fecalized material within the distal segment of the ileum with small bowel caliber measuring up to 4.0 cm.  At the level of the terminal ileum, the small bowel is fairly decompressed best seen on series 2, image 30. The ileocecal valve is seen on series 2, image 28 with the cecum positioned just below the inferior edge of the liver.  The appendix appears to originate from the cecum on series 2, image 31 and extends retrocecal.  The appendix can then be traced posteriorly to the level of an appendicolith best seen on series 2, image 35.  Distal to this point the appendix is dilated and measures up to 12 mm in maximal diameter.  There is adjacent free fluid.  Additional elongated appendicoliths seen within the mid and distal appendix.  Findings are concerning for ____.  There is no evidence of perforation.  Adjacent prominent lymph nodes are present likely reactive.  The colon is fairly decompressed.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder is markedly distended.  No pelvic sidewall or inguinal adenopathy.  No free air or free fluid.  BONES: There is a mild levoscoliosis of the lumbar spine.  No worrisome lytic or blastic osseous lesion is seen.'
);


