    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Oral contrast was administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Stationary Acquisition 0.5 s, 1.0 cm; CTDIvol = 1.2 mGy (Body) DLP = 1.2 mGy-cm.    2) Stationary Acquisition 15.0 s, 1.0 cm; CTDIvol = 34.7 mGy (Body) DLP = 34.7 mGy-cm.    3) Spiral Acquisition 15.2 s, 52.1 cm; CTDIvol = 10.1 mGy (Body) DLP = 513.4 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 563 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: Please note the diagnostic sensitivity of the study is degraded by delayed contrast bolus timing.  LOWER CHEST: No confluent airspace consolidation.  No pleural effusions.  Minor atelectasis in the right lung base.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is within normal limits.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  Small simple right renal cyst is noted (series 7, image 21) appear there is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The feeding tube terminates in the mid stomach.  Markedly dilated small bowel loops with air-fluid levels are noted.  There are 2 new transition points visualized; the first in the right mid abdomen (series 5, image 44), and the second just below this in the mid abdomen (series 5, image 48).  The bowel wall enhances uniformly.  No pneumatosis or portal venous gas to suggest bowel ischemia/necrosis.  Distal to these two transition points, the ileum is decompressed.  There is marked thickening of the distal ileal wall (this may be reactive secondary to appendix related inflammatory changes).  The distal ileum is collapsed. No abnormal colonic distention.  Sigmoid and rectal edema is most likely secondary to inflammatory changes in the pelvis.  Again noted is the perforated appendix with an extraluminal appendix (series 5, image 77).  Reactive edematous changes noted in the pelvis.  PELVIS: There are at least 2 rim enhancing collections present in the pelvis, the larger in the left hemipelvis measuring 87 x 41 mm in the axial plane (series 5, image 83) and the smaller in the right hemipelvis measuring 27 x 21 mm (series 5, image 85).  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The visualized reproductive organs are unremarkable.  LYMPH NODES: Reactive mesenteric and retroperitoneal lymph nodes are noted.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Mild atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.  NOTIFICATION: The findings were discussed by Dr. ___ with Dr. ___ on the ___ ___ at 9:32 pm, 15 minutes after discovery of the findings.'
);


