    'EXAMINATION: CT of the abdomen and pelvis  TECHNIQUE: Multi detector CT images through the abdomen and pelvis were obtained after the administration of intravenous contrast.  No oral contrast was administered.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were generated and reviewed.  DOSE: Total DLP (Body) = 761 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LUNG BASES: Lung bases are clear.  No pleural or pericardial effusion.  A right cardiophrenic lymph node is unchanged.  ABDOMEN:   LIVER: The liver is massively enlarged measuring up to 28 cm in craniocaudal dimension.  The liver attenuation is somewhat hypodense which could reflect fatty deposition though more advanced forms of liver disease difficult to exclude.  Main portal vein is patent.  There is trace perihepatic fluid with extensive stranding in nodularity which may reflect hepatitis given clinical per clinical presentation.  GALLBLADDER: Surgically absent with clips in the gallbladder fossa as well as a small amount of fluid which is compatible with recent postoperative status.  The CBD is nondilated.  SPLEEN: Enlarged up to 15 cm.  No focal lesion.  ADRENALS: Normal.  KIDNEYS: Symmetric enhancement and prompt excretion without hydronephrosis or signs of pyelonephritis.  PANCREAS: Normal.  GI: Stomach, duodenum, small bowel loops appear normal.  The appendix is abnormally thickened, dilated up to 1 cm which is concerning for early acute ____.  No overt signs of colitis.  Diverticulosis without diverticulitis.  Trace free fluid noted.  No free air.  VASCULAR: The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber without aneurysmal dilatation.  LYMPH NODES: Scattered retroperitoneal nodes are present.  A left periaortic node measures approximately 1.4 x 0.9 cm, previously present unchanged (02:52).  A portacaval node is enlarged measuring approximately 1.4 x 3.1 cm (02:41).  PELVIS: The bladder is moderately well distended and grossly unremarkable.  Prostate gland and seminal vesicles are normal in appearance.  There is no pelvic free fluid.  There is no inguinal or pelvic sidewall adenopathy.  Small bilateral inguinal fat containing hernias are symmetric.  Post procedural changes are noted within the right anterior lateral wall.  BONES: Exostosis arising from the right inferior pubic ramus is re- demonstrated (2:97).  No suspicious lytic or blastic lesion is identified.'
);


