INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '22360162',
    '12035989-RR-145',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration. Oral contrast was administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Stationary Acquisition 0.5 s, 1.0 cm; CTDIvol = 1.2 mGy (Body) DLP = 1.2 mGy-cm.    2) Stationary Acquisition 5.5 s, 1.0 cm; CTDIvol = 12.7 mGy (Body) DLP = 12.7 mGy-cm.    3) Spiral Acquisition 15.3 s, 52.5 cm; CTDIvol = 17.6 mGy (Body) DLP = 899.5 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 926 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Subsegmental atelectasis in the lung bases.  No pleural or pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions. There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder contains sludge.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  Likely congenital agenesis of the pancreatic tail, anatomic variant.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There is no evidence of focal renal lesions or hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: A nasogastric tube extends to the stomach.  Small-bowel loops are persistently dilated although slightly less distended measuring up to 5 cm (previously 5.5 cm) most prominent in the upper and left abdomen.  There is gradual tapering and diffusion oral contrast in the left abdomen with less well visualized discrete transition point.  Fluid and locules of gas are visualized in the colon.  Redemonstrated are findings compatible with perforated ____ including appendicoliths at the cecal base and in the right lower quadrant as well as extensive surrounding inflammation and numerous small loculated fluid collections:  - 2.0 x 1.3 cm (5:61) and 3.6 x 2.0 cm (5:65) in the right upper pelvis, -2.5 x 2.4 cm (5:56) in the midline lower mesentery, previously measuring 3.4 x 2.3 cm - 4.5 x 2.4 cm (previously 3.7 x 2.6 cm), in the central pelvis abutting the uterus (5:69), and - 3.0 x 2.8 cm (previously 2.8 x 2.3 cm), in the deep pelvis (5:76) posterior to the uterus and to the right of the sigmoid colon.  Fluid within the left ovary measures up to 1.7 cm, overall stable in appearance.  Severe reactive inflammatory changes involving the sigmoid colon which is redundant and courses directly through the pelvic inflammation.  PELVIS: Reactive inflammatory changes involving the bladder, uterus, and bilateral adnexa makes the delineation of structures difficult.  There is no definite air in the uterus or bladder to suggest a fistula.  Numerous calcified fibroids are redemonstrated.  LYMPH NODES: There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: Patent visualized abdominopelvic vasculature.  Stranding noted surrounding the left gonadal veins although it is difficult to detect whether they are patent.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: Small bowel containing periumbilical hernia with fluid and stranding at the skin surface as well as 2.4 x 1.7 cm fluid collection (5:68). Mild, slightly increased anasarca.'
);


