    'EXAM: Right upper quadrant ultrasound.  FINDINGS: The exam is somewhat suboptimal due to overlying bowel gas.  The liver is markedly echogenic diffusely, consistent with severe fatty infiltration, which limits sensitivity in detecting focal intrahepatic lesions, although none is seen.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The proximal common bile duct measures 0.6 cm in diameter.  The distal common bile duct was not able to be seen due to overlying bowel gas.  Gallbladder sludge and stones are seen within the gallbladder.  There is no gallbladder wall thickening.  Sonographic ___ sign was absent.  Pancreas is not seen due to overlying bowel gas.  The main portal vein is patent with hepatopetal flow.  The spleen is normal in size, measuring 11.6 cm in length.  No free fluid is seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20073240',
    '10441211-RR-23',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis were displayed with 5-mm slice thickness.  Intravenous contrast was administered.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were prepared.  CT ABDOMEN WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: Minimal atelectasis is identified at the lung bases.  There is no consolidation or pleural effusion.  The imaged cardiac apex is within normal limits.  There is diffuse fatty infiltration of the liver without focal lesion.  The hepatic veins and portal venous system are grossly patent.  The gallbladder is distended and demonstrates diffuse wall thickening; there is pericholecystic fat stranding though no overt fluid.  Additionally, there is hyperemia of the liver parenchyma surrounding the gallbladder.  In this patient with known gallstones, findings are highly concerning for ____.  No intra- or extra-hepatic biliary ductal dilatation is identified.  The spleen, pancreas, and adrenal glands appear normal.  There is symmetric enhancement and excretion from both kidneys without suspicious focal lesion or hydronephrosis.  The abdominal aorta and its branch vessels are non-aneurysmal and grossly patent.  There is no abdominal free fluid or free air.  The stomach and small bowel loops are normal in caliber and configuration without evidence of obstruction or inflammation.  The appendix is well visualized and is normal.  CT PELVIS WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: The rectum and colon are normal in caliber and configuration without evidence of obstruction or inflammation.  The bladder is distended and appears normal.  Prostate and seminal vesicles are unremarkable.  There is no pelvic free fluid.  BONES AND SOFT TISSUES: No bone destructive lesion or acute fracture is identified.'
);


