INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25360313',
    '10656270-RR-20',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT of the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous contrast.  TECHNIQUE: Helically acquired axial images were obtained from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis after the uneventful administration of 130 cc of Optiray intravenous contrast using a CT enterography protocol.  Coronal and sagittal reformations are provided for review.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: Other than right greater than left dependent atelectasis, the lung bases are clear with no focal consolidations or pulmonary nodules.  There is gallbladder wall thickening, new since the ultrasound examination from ___.  A calcified gallstone is noted within the gallbladder neck.  The gallbladder is mildly distended though not dilated.  There is no dilation of the biliary tree. The liver, spleen, pancreas, both adrenal glands, both kidneys, both ureters, and visualized portions of intra-abdominal small and large bowel are unremarkable.  Note is made of a moderate hiatal hernia.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  There is no intra-abdominal free air or free fluid.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: The rectum, sigmoid colon, bladder, and seminal vesicles are unremarkable.  The prostate appears mildly enlarged, measuring up to 5.0 cm.  There is no pelvic free fluid.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy. The appendix is not well-visualized. The right hemicolon demonstrates fecal loading.  BONE WINDOWS: Visualized osseous structures are unremarkable with no suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesions.  There are multilevel degenerative changes manifested by marginal osteophytic formation and facet arthropathy at the lumbrosacral junction.'
);


