INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27332252',
    '10669036-RR-17',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were obtained of the abdomen and pelvis after the administration of intravenous contrast.  Axial images were interpreted in conjunction with sagittal and coronal reformats.  DLP: ___ MGy-cm  FINDINGS:   CHEST: There is mild dependent atelectasis at the right base.. The visualized portion of the heart and pericardium are normal. There is no pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN: There is stranding and fluid seen surrounding the head, body, and tail of the pancreas, which appear to enhance normally, consistent with pancreatitis. The pancreatic duct is prominent, although not enlarged. Multiple prominent although nonenlarged retroperitoneal and mesenteric lymph nodes are present.  The liver is normal without focal or diffuse abnormality.  The gallbladder, intra- and extra-hepatic bile ducts, and spleen are normal.  There is an incompletely characterized 1 cm nodule of the left adrenal gland. The right adrenal gland is somewhat thickened and lobulated but without discrete nodule. The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast promptly. The ureters are normal in course and caliber.  The stomach is normal. The small and large bowel enhance homogeneously and have a normal course and caliber.  There is diverticulosis without diverticulitis.  The appendix is well visualized and normal appearing..  No retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy. The portal and intra-abdominal systemic vasculature are normal. No abdominal wall hernia, pneumoperitoneum, or free abdominal fluid.  Abdominal aorta is normal in caliber with scattered atherosclerotic calcifications. Other vasculature specifically surrounding the pancreas appears patent.  PELVIS: The bladder and terminal ureters are normal.  There is a fibroid uterus.  No pelvic side-wall or inguinal lymphadenopathy. No free pelvic fluid or inguinal hernia.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There is a chronic appearing compression deformities of L1.  However there is a increased sclerosis within the L1 and potentially S1 vertebrae. Other degenerative changes including Schmorl''s nodes seen within the lumbar spine.  NOTIFICATION: Additions to WET READ were discussed by Dr. ___ with Dr. ___ at 615 on ___.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
