INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25970749',
    '11171757-RR-8',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS W/O CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were acquired from the lung bases through the pubic symphysis without IV contrast and with oral contrast due to elevated creatinine.  Multiplanar reformations were generated.  CT ABDOMEN: There are patchy opacities in the lung bases, with appearance suggestive of infection and/or aspiration.  Concurrent basal atelectasis may be present.  There is also fissural thickening on the left, which could represent atelectasis.  There is no pleural effusion.  A 3-mm pulmonary nodule is noted in the right middle lobe.  The heart is top normal in size without pericardial effusion.  There is mitral annular calcification and multivessel coronary arterial disease.  Evaluation of intra-abdominal viscera is highly limited due to lack of intravenous contrast.  Allowing for such, the liver demonstrates no focal lesion.  There are multiple radiopaque stones within a moderately distended gallbladder which demonstrates diffuse wall thickening and subjacent fat stranding, compatible with ____.  Of note, one of the gallstones appears to be in the region of the neck, probably within bounds of the gallbladder wall.  There is no obvious free fluid or free air.  There is marked thickening of second portion of the duodenum circumferentially.  While this could represent reactive duodenitis, a concurrent separate primary process cannot be excluded.  A small duodenal diverticulum is noted arising from the junction of the second and third portion of duodenum medially.  The spleen, diffusely atrophic pancreas, and right adrenal gland appear within normal limits.  There is thickening of the left adrenal gland, without focal nodularity.  Mild perinephric stranding on the right may be at least in part reactive.  There is no hydronephrosis or hydroureter.  Small and large bowel loops are normal in caliber.  The appendix is normal.  There is normal contrast progression distally into the colon.  Mild thickening is present along the cecal tip, nonspecific. Extensive diverticulosis is present without diverticulitis.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  Extensive atherosclerotic calcifications are seen along the aorta extending into iliac vessels.  CT PELVIS: There is a 2.2-cm bladder diverticulum in the posterolaterally on the right.  The uterus, adnexa, and rectum appear unremarkable.  There is no inguinal or pelvic lymphadenopathy.  No free fluid within the pelvis.  BONE WINDOWS: There is S-shaped thoracolumbar scoliosis.  Severe multilevel thoracolumbar spondylosis, disc space narrowing, and vacuum phenomenon are present.  The lower thoracic spine demonstrates DISH.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
