INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20121749',
    '11181505-RR-16',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT of the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous contrast.  TECHNIQUE: Helically acquired axial images were obtained from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis after administration of intravenous contrast only. Coronal and sagittal reformations were obtained.  Timing was performed using an enterography protocol.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: There is minimal dependent atelectasis at the lung bases.  The lung bases are otherwise clear with no focal parenchymal consolidation, pleural effusions, or pulmonary nodules.  Located within segment V of the liver and segment III of the liver, there are multiple scattered hypodense liver lesions, all of which measure less than 1 cm, that are too small to accurately characterize, however, statistically, likely represents simple cysts.  There is no intra- or extra-hepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is minimally distended; however, pericholecystic fluid is present, more fully appreciated on coronal and sagittal views (301B:29) and (300B:34). The spleen, pancreas, both adrenal glands, both kidneys, and visualized loops of intrahepatic small bowel are unremarkable.  There is no intra-abdominal free fluid or free air.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  Note is made of some mild wall thickening involving the ascending colon at the hepatic flexure, though the colon is decompressed, and may likely be related to decompressed state.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: The appendix is well visualized, with the base containing air, and is non- dilated, and with the tip slightly higher density material, may be related to ingested contents, with no associated ___ inflammation.  The rectum, sigmoid colon, prostate, and seminal vesicles are unremarkable.  There is no pelvic free fluid.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOWS: The visualized osseous structures are unremarkable with no suspicious lytic or sclerotic foci identified.'
);


