INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '23428361',
    '11686464-RR-8',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis during the administration of 130 cc of intravenous Omnipaque contrast material.  Multiplanar reformations were performed.  TOTAL DLP: 799 mGy-cm.  ABDOMEN CT: There is minimal bilateral lower lobe dependent atelectasis.  Dense mitral annular calcifications are seen.  The liver parenchyma enhances homogeneously.  There is no intrahepatic biliary duct dilatation.  The portal vein is patent.  The gallbladder is moderately distended with mild circumferential wall thickening.  There is mild-to-moderate pericholecystic fat stranding within the peritoneal fat of the anterior abdomen (2:30), suggestive of ____.  The mucosa along the gallbladder fundus appears to be focally disrupted, concerning for early or impending perforation (___:28).  Tiny stones are seen layering along the dependent portion of the gallbladder body.  The common duct is normal in caliber.  A punctate calcification is seen in the posterior aspect of the spleen (2:21), possibly related to prior granulomatous disease.  The spleen is otherwise normal.  There is mild fatty atrophy of the pancreas, which is otherwise unremarkable.  The adrenal glands are normal.  A 5.1-cm simple cyst extends from the right lower renal pole.  Additional tiny bilateral renal hypodensities are too small to characterize but are also statistically simple cysts.  The kidneys are otherwise unremarkable.  The stomach and small bowel are normal.  There is colonic diverticulosis without evidence of diverticulitis.  The appendix is normal.  There is no free fluid or free air in the abdomen.  Prominent portacaval nodes measure up to 25 x 13 mm (2:23,28). Small mesenteric and retroperitoneal lymph nodes do not meet CT size criteria. The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber.  Scattered aortic calcifications and mural thrombus is noted.  There are also scattered bilateral iliac artery calcifications.  PELVIS CT: A punctate stone is seen in the dependent portion of the bladder, which is otherwise unremarkable.  There is no free fluid in the pelvis.  No pathologically enlarged pelvic lymph nodes are seen.  BONE WINDOW: No suspicious lytic or blastic lesions are identified.  Multilevel degenerative changes of the thoracolumbar spine are seen.  There is minimal grade 1 retrolisthesis of L4 on L5 and L5 on S1, likely degenerative.  Mild anterior wedging is seen of the T11 and T10 vertebral bodies, age indeterminate.'
);


