INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25093942',
    '11725523-RR-2',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'EXAMINATION: MRCP:  TECHNIQUE: T1 and T2 weighted multiplanar images of the abdomen were acquired within a 1.5 T magnet, including 3D dynamic sequences obtained prior to, during, and following the administration of 10 cc of Gadavist intravenous contrast.  The patient also received oral contrast of 1 cc of Gadavist diluted in 50 cc of water.  FINDINGS: There are mild atelectatic changes at the lung bases without pleural effusion.  Gallbladder wall is thickened, irregular, edematous and progressively hyperenhancing, compatible with known history of ____ (4:30).  There is no gallbladder wall disruption or pericholecystic fluid. Extensive surrounding parenchymal hepatic arterial hyperenhancement is noted (1301:74) as well as the stranding within the pericholecystic fat. There is cholelithiasis, and a suspected 3 mm stone within the proximal cystic duct (4:32).  There is no choledocholithiasis.  There is no concerning hepatic mass. There is no intra or extrahepatic bile duct dilation.  The pancreas demonstrates normal parenchymal bulk, normal intrinsic T1 hyperintense signal, and normal homogeneous enhancement. There is no peripancreatic inflammatory change, aside from minimal stranding adjacent to the head likely related to nearby inflammation of the gallbladder. The pancreatic duct is normal in caliber.  The spleen and adrenal glands are unremarkable. There are bilateral subcentimeter cortical renal cysts.  Enlarged portacaval and porta hepatis lymph nodes are likely reactive.  There is no retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  There is no ascites.  Osseous structures are unremarkable.  NOTIFICATION: Findings, specifically dominant findings of ____ without definitive choledocholithiasis, were discussed via phone by Dr ___ with Dr ___ at approximately 9:20am on ___.'
);


