    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Oral contrast was not administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Stationary Acquisition 4.0 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 19.3 mGy (Body) DLP = 9.6 mGy-cm.    2) Spiral Acquisition 4.8 s, 52.4 cm; CTDIvol = 16.7 mGy (Body) DLP = 876.4 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 886 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: There is a trace right pleural effusion with mild to moderate bilateral dependent atelectasis.  There is no pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions.  There is mild intrahepatic biliary dilatation or periportal edema.  The gallbladder is distended, with a thickened wall and extensive pericholecystic fat stranding, extending to involve the anterior pararenal fascia and right lateroconal fascia (___).  There is a hyperdense gallstone in the gallbladder fundus (2:32) measuring 5 mm.  Findings are compatible with ____.  No evidence of free intraperitoneal air or focal fluid collection.  There is trace perihepatic ascites.  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  Subcentimeter right renal hypodensities are too small to characterize by CT, but are statistically likely cysts.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The stomach is unremarkable.  Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout.  Diverticulosis of the colon is noted, without evidence of wall thickening and fat stranding.  The appendix is normal.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is a small amount of nonhemorrhagic free pelvic fluid (2:71).  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The uterus contains multiple calcifications, as seen on the prior study.  LYMPH NODES: There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Extensive atherosclerotic disease is noted.  There is re- demonstration of complete, chronic occlusion of the proximal SMA with reconstitution shortly distally.  The right iliac to SMA graft is widely patent and fills with intravenous contrast.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  Grade 1 anterolisthesis of L4 and L5 is unchanged.  Moderate multilevel degenerative changes of the lumbar spine are similar to the prior study.  SOFT TISSUES: There is a small right inguinal hernia containing fat.  The patient is status post repair of the previous ventral hernia, with a large abdominal wall soft tissue defect, with adjacent subcutaneous fat stranding.  This is compatible with the known history of abdominal wound dehiscence requiring a VAC closure.  NOTIFICATION: The above findings were communicated in person by Dr. ___ to Dr. ___ (ACS surgery) at 14:30 on ___, 1 min after discovery.  Findings were also communicated via telephone by Dr. ___ to Dr. ___. ___ in the ED at 14:40 on ___, 10 min after discovery.'
);


