    '11199428-RR-15',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN)',
    'EXAMINATION: LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN):  TECHNIQUE: Grey scale and color Doppler ultrasound images of the abdomen were obtained.  FINDINGS:   LIVER: The liver is diffusely echogenic. The contour of the liver is smooth. There is no focal liver mass. The main portal vein is patent with hepatopetal flow. There is no ascites.  BILE DUCTS: There is no intrahepatic biliary dilation.  The common bile duct was not definitely visualized due to large amount of overlying bowel gas and shadowing from multiple gallbladder stones.  A hyperdensity with shadowing in the expected region of the common bile duct is felt to likely represent air within peristalsing bowel as it changed appearance during the exam, rather than stone within the common bile duct.  GALLBLADDER: There are stones in the distended gallbladder which has a thickened and edematous wall, measuring up to 9 mm.  PANCREAS: The pancreas is not well visualized, largely obscured by overlying bowel gas.  KIDNEYS: Limited views of the right kidney show no hydronephrosis.  RETROPERITONEUM: The visualized portions of aorta and IVC are within normal limits.'
);


