INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21888122',
    '11487947-RR-13',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration. Oral contrast was not administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Spiral Acquisition 3.5 s, 46.7 cm; CTDIvol = 10.5 mGy (Body) DLP = 487.4 mGy-cm.    2) Stationary Acquisition 0.6 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 3.3 mGy (Body) DLP = 1.7 mGy-cm.    3) Stationary Acquisition 3.0 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 16.7 mGy (Body) DLP = 8.4 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 497 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Visualized lung fields are within normal limits.  There is no evidence of pleural or pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions.  An 8 mm hypodensity in hepatic segment VI is too small to characterize (02:39).  There is very mild intrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The common bile duct measures 8 mm, larger than expected for patient''s age.  The gallbladder appears distended (601:27).  Hyperdensities within the gallbladder fundus may reflect layering stones (02:41).  PANCREAS: The pancreas has slightly decreased attenuation along the body and tail, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is mild stranding of the mesentery adjacent to the pancreas anteriorly and inferiorly (series 601, image 18).  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There is no evidence of suspicious focal renal lesions or hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  A sub-centimeter hypodensity in the lower pole of the right kidney is too small to characterize but likely represents a simple cyst.  A tubular hypodensity in the lower pole of the left kidney likely reflects two adjacent cysts, although incompletely assessed on the current study (02:33).  GASTROINTESTINAL: The stomach is unremarkable.  Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout. The colon and rectum are within normal limits. The appendix is not definitively visualized, although there are no secondary signs of appendicitis.  PELVIS: Urinary bladder is decompressed.  The distal ureters are unremarkable. The patient is status post closure of a vesicouterine fistula in ___.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: Uterus is heterogeneous, incompletely evaluated on the current study.  There is prominence of the left greater than right ovarian veins which can be seen in the setting of pelvic congestion syndrome.  LYMPH NODES: There is haziness of the fat in the upper central abdomen posterior to the gastric antrum and anterior to the pancreas where numerous conspicuous lymph nodes are identified, possibly reactive in etiology (___:31). Additional conspicuous nodes are seen throughout the mesenteric fat.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Mild to moderate atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


