)
VALUES (
    '20028712',
    '11891669-RR-30',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration. Oral contrast was not administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Stationary Acquisition 4.0 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 19.3 mGy (Body) DLP = 9.6 mGy-cm.    2) Spiral Acquisition 6.1 s, 48.1 cm; CTDIvol = 10.2 mGy (Body) DLP = 489.0 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 499 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: A consolidation within the lingula is likely atelectasis.  Calcified granuloma within the right lower lobe.  Focal pleural calcifications abutting the right lower lobe.  Trace right pleural effusion with associated atelectasis.  Mild left basilar atelectasis.  No pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  A subcentimeter hepatic hypodensity within segment VII is too small to characterize, likely a cyst.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  Mild periportal edema.  The gallbladder is distended, with gallbladder wall thickening and pericholecystic fat stranding.  Discontinuity of the gallbladder wall laterally (601:20) with an adjacent pericholecystic collection is seen along the inferior margin of the liver, compatible with perforation and a contained collection.  Several gallstones are seen within the gallbladder, including one within the gallbladder neck (02:23, 601:121).  PANCREAS: The pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions or pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is no peripancreatic stranding.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  Bilateral, subcentimeter renal hypodensities are too small to characterize.  There is no hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: Moderate hiatal hernia.  Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout.  Duodenal diverticulum is noted.  Anastomotic sutures are seen within the right lower quadrant and deep pelvis.  Scattered colonic diverticulosis, without evidence of acute diverticulitis.  The remaining colon otherwise appears within normal limits.  The appendix is normal.  PELVIS: The bladder appears unremarkable.  There is no free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The uterus and bilateral adnexa appear unremarkable for age.  LYMPH NODES: Multiple prominent retroperitoneal nodes are likely reactive.  There is no mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Moderate to severe atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  Levoscoliosis of the lumbar spine.  A peripherally sclerotic focus within the right femoral head (601:26) may reflect degenerative changes or avascular necrosis.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.  NOTIFICATION: The findings were discussed with ___, M.D. by ___, M.D. on the telephone on ___ at 9:42 pm, 1 minutes after discovery of the findings.'
);


