INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24782843',
    '10235987-RR-22',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD W&W/O C',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multiple contiguous 5-mm thick axial CT images of the abdomen and pelvis were obtained from lung bases to pubic symphysis utilizing 130 mL of IV Optiray.  Prior to this, non-contrast set through the abdomen was also obtained.  Subsequently, coronal and sagittal reformations were performed.  ABDOMEN FINDINGS: The visualized lung bases are clear.  There is no extraluminal air.  The liver, spleen, adrenal glands, and right kidney are unremarkable.  There are three small hypodense lesions, two in the interpolar and one in the upper pole region in the left kidney.  There is no renal calculus, hydronephrosis or hydroureter.  The pancreatic parenchyma is enhancing homogeneously.  There is no definite inflammatory changes, or abnormal fluid collection to suggest pseudocyst or abscess.  There is minimal distention of the pancreatic duct within body and tail.  Pancreatic ductal stent is in place draining into the duodenum.  The patient is status post cholecystectomy.  There is no abdominal lymphadenopathy.  Stomach and small bowel are grossly unremarkable, so is the large bowel.  The appendix is not clearly visualized, however, there is no evidence of appendicitis.  The abdominal aorta and its major branches are within normal limits.  PELVIS FINDINGS: There is a small amount of fluid in the cul-de-sac. Central hypodensity within the uterus is likely related to products of menses.  There is no pelvic lymphadenopathy.  The urinary bladder is grossly unremarkable. There is dropped surgical clip lying in the pelvis.  The subcutaneous tissues are unremarkable.  The osseous structures are intact.'
);


