    '10260237-RR-7',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'MR OF THE ABDOMEN FROM ___:   TECHNIQUE: Multiplanar T1- and T2-weighted MR images of the abdomen were obtained on a 1.5 Tesla magnet including dynamic 3D imaging prior to, during, and after the administration of 7 mL Gadavist gadolinium-based contrast.  1 mL Gadavist mixed with 50 mL water was also administered for oral contrast.  FINDINGS: The liver is normal in signal intensity without evidence of focal mass.  Multiple small layering stones are noted within the gallbladder lumen.  There is minimal gallbladder wall edema and thickening with no surrounding inflammatory change.  Note is made of a stone in the gallbladder neck or distal cystic duct with some surrounding hyperenhancement.  The pancreas is diffusely decreased in signal intensity on T1 pre-contrast images and demonstrates hyperintense signal diffusely on T2-weighted images compatible with ____.  Surrounding peripancreatic inflammatory change and edema is also noted.  There is no evidence of pancreatic mass or ductal dilatation.  The pancreas enhances throughout its course without findings to suggest necrosis.  The common bile duct is borderline dilated, measuring 6.8 mm.  There is no significant intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  There is no evidence of choledocholithiasis.  The spleen, adrenal glands, and kidneys are normal in appearance.  The visualized bowel loops and mesentery are normal.  There is no significant mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  The abdominal vasculature appears widely patent.  The osseous structures are unremarkable.  The lung bases are grossly clear.  There is a small amount of ascites in the upper abdomen.  Small bilateral pleural effusions are also noted.'
);


