INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29234985',
    '10288579-RR-17',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS W & W/O CONTRAST, ADDL SECTIONS',
    'TECHNIQUE: Axial MDCT images were obtained through the abdomen and pelvis with and without IV contrast as per ____ protocol.  Sagittal and coronal reformats were generated.  TOTAL EXAM DLP: 1244 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: There are bilateral small-to-moderate pleural effusions with associated compressive atelectasis, left worse than right.  Visualized portions of the heart and pericardium are within normal limits.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN: The liver is low density on noncontrast images consistent with moderate steatosis, and enhances homogeneously with no evidence of focal hepatic lesions.  There is no intra- or extra-hepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  The gallbladder is normal.  There is no evidence of gallstones.  The adrenal glands are normal.  The spleen is homogeneous and top normal in size. Kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast without evidence of hydronephrosis or renal masses.  There is diffuse inflammatory peripancreatic stranding. Peripancreatic fluid extend from the lesser sac into the anterior pararenal spaces and dissects inferiorly, left worse than right, and along the paracolic gutters bilaterally into the pelvis, but without well defined or walled-off collections. The pancreas in itself demonstrates normal enhancement, although the head and neck of the pancreas have a slightly lower enhancement when compared to the body and tail.  No pancreatic calcifications.  The pancreatic duct is not dilated. There is no evidence of pseudocyst formation, splenic artery pseudo aneurysms, portal vein or splenic vein thrombosis. Splenic vein may be mildly narrowed.  The stomach is collapsed and contains a feeding tube which ends in the proximal jejunum.  There is no evidence of small-bowel obstruction.  Oral contrast courses freely into the colon with no evidence of bowel wall abnormalities.    The abdominal aorta is of normal diameter.  The celiac axis, SMA, bilateral renal arteries and ___ are patent.  There are scattered small mesenteric and retroperitoneal lymph nodes, which are likely secondary to the surrounding inflammatory changes.  CT OF THE PELVIS: The urinary bladder and terminal ureters are within normal limits.  Small amount of stranding and fluid is noted in the pelvis, extending from the abdomen.   Scattered inguinal lymph nodes are noted, none of which meet CT size criteria for lymphadenopathy.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: No blastic or lytic lesion concerning for malignancy.'
);


