    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Helical MDCT images were acquired from the lung bases through the greater trochanters with intravenous and without oral contrast.  5 mm axial, coronal, and sagittal multiplanar reformats were generated.  FINDINGS: Mild atelectasis is noted at the lung bases.  The heart is normal in size.  There are no pleural or pericardial effusions.  Note is made of a moderate sliding hiatal hernia.  ABDOMEN: Diffuse fat stranding and soft tissue density are noted surrounding the pancreas, overall increased since ___. This extends to the root of the mesentery, and throughout the retroperitoneum, surrounding the abdominal aorta, proximal iliac arteries, and celiac and superior mesenteric artery takeoffs.  The superior mesenteric, splenic, portal, hepatic veins and IVC are patent.  There is no parenchymal hypoenhancement suspicious for necrosis.  No intra- or extra-hepatic biliary dilatation is present.  Again seen throughout the liver are multiple lobulated, hypoenhancing lesions, previously demonstrated to be hemangiomas.  A new 1.5 x 1.3 cm hyperenhancing focus is noted in segment VII, which also has a lobulated appaarance.  Gallbladder is surgically absent.  The spleen is normal in size, with scattered ill-defined hypodensities in the posterior aspect, which were not definitely visualized on prior examination.  Note is made of a tiny splenule in the left upper quadrant, anterior to the main splenic tip.  The adrenals are normal.  The kidneys enhance and excrete contrast promptly and symmetrically, without masses or hydronephrosis.  Renal hypodensities are too small to characterize, but likely represent cysts.  Again noted is a partially distended stomach, with retained fluid and air.  Mild wall edema is noted at the C-sweep of the duodenum, adjacent to the area of pancreatic head inflammation.  However, there is no transition point to suggest obstruction.  Distal small bowel loops are fluid filled and nondilated.  Sequelae of abdominal wall incisional hernia repair are present, with multiple surgical staples and residual fat stranding in the anterior abdominal wall.  PELVIS: Again noted is a prominent and fluid-filled appendix, measuring up to 7 mm in diameter, without wall thickening or fat stranding to suggest acute inflammation.  The transverse and sigmoid colon are markedly redundant.  The bladder is partially distended, with stable small right posterolateral diverticulum that fills with contrast.  The prostate is slightly heterogeneous and enlarged, with coarse central calcifications.  Note is made of bilateral fat-containing small inguinal hernias, with a varicocele noted on the right.  There is no free fluid or air.  The bones are demineralized, with mild S-shaped thoracolumbar scoliosis and multilevel degenerative changes.  No suspicious lytic or sclerotic osseous lesions are identified.'
);


