INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20418179',
    '10327710-RR-20',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CTA PANCREAS W/ CTCP',
    'CTA PANCREAS PROTOCOL CT DATED ___:   TECHNIQUE: CTA pancreas protocol.  Axial CT images were acquired after the uneventful administration of 150 mL of Optiray 350 contrast material without reported complication.  Pancreatic CTA examination was performed utilizing standard departmental protocol with reformations in the sagittal and coronal plane.  No oral contrast material was administered.  A limited non-contrast CT through the lung bases, pancreas and mid abdomen was performed prior to the administration of intravenous contrast material.  FINDINGS: The lung bases are clear.  Non-contrast images through the level of the pancreas demonstrate no significant abnormalities or calcifications.  CTA PANCREAS: There is enhancement of the liver parenchyma with multiple 1-5 mm scattered areas of hypoattenuation throughout the hepatic parenchyma.  The liver is otherwise unremarkable without mass lesions.  The portal veins are patent.  There is minimal central intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation (4:26).  No extrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  The gallbladder is normal in appearance without gallstones.  The adrenal glands, spleen, pancreas, and kidneys enhance normally.  No peripancreatic fluid or peripancreatic fat stranding. No pancreatic ductal dilatation.  No signs of pancreatic divisum. There is no hydronephrosis.  There is a tiny left interpolar 5-mm area of hypoattenuation, too small to further characterize, but statistically most likely represents a simple left renal cyst.  Evaluation of the arterial vasculature demonstrates a patent aorta in the abdomen, without dissection.  Patent celiac axis and branch vessels and patent superior mesenteric artery and branch vessels, where seen.  The bowel including stomach, visualized portions of small and large bowel are within normal limits.  No significant abdominal or retroperitoneal lymph nodes are identified.  The osseous structures are unremarkable with normal-appearing vertebral body heights and disc spaces were seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25522072',
    '10374847-RR-23',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Helical MDCT images were acquired from the lung bases to the pubic symphysis after administration of oral and IV contrast.  Multiplanar reformatted images were obtained.  FINDINGS:   CT ABDOMEN: There are tiny bilateral pleural effusions with bibasilar atelectasis in the visualized lung base.  The lungs are otherwise unremarkable.  The visualized heart appears unchanged, without pericardial effusion.  In the abdomen, the liver is normal without focal lesions or evidence of intrahepatic biilary dilatation.  There are two small gallstones in the dependent position of a non- distended gallbladder without evidence of acute cholecystitis.  The spleen and adrenal glands are unremarkable.  The kidneys appear atrophic bilaterally, with the known cystic structure unchanged.  The suspicious small renal lesion in the right interpolar region is again seen without any short interval changes. The stomach, duodenum and loops of nondistended small bowel are within normal limits.  In the pancreas, there is a metallic stent with the one end in the head of the pancreas and the other end in the proximal CBD adjacent to the gallbladder. Interval development of fat stranding along the CDB in the porta hepatis, but no definite collection suspicious for abscess.  The fat stranding area approximately measures 4 x 3cm, best visualized in image 2:24.  There is no free fluid or air in the intra- abdominal cavity. There are scattered small mesenteric nodes, but none of them are pathologically enlarged by CT criteria.  CT PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: The colon is patent with oral contrast and bowel gas.  The bladder is normally distended without focal abnormality.  The prostate is unremarkable.  The loops of small bowel in the pelvis are grossly unremarkable.  The left common iliac has an aneurysmal dilatation, measuring 3.5 cm in diameter, inferior to the imaged volume of prior study .  There is no free fluid or air in the pelvis. There is no lymphadenopathy.  BONE WINDOW: There is significant degenerative disease in the underlying thoracolumbar spine, with multilevel ntervertebral disc space narrowingi and osteophytosis.  Levoscoliosis of the lumbar spine, unchanged.  No acute fracture or dislocation.  No lytic or sclerotic bony lesion suspicious for metastasis.  Moderate aortic calcification is seen but unchanged.'
);


