INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26626481',
    '10559181-RR-35',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CTA CHEST W&W/O C&RECONS, NON-CORONARY',
    'TECHNIQUE: Post-contrast axial images were obtained through the chest with a PE protocol.  Post-contrast axial images were also obtained through the abdomen and pelvis.  Multiplanar reformatted images were generated.  CT CHEST WITH IV CONTRAST: The pulmonary arterial tree is well opacified, and there is no pulmonary embolus.  The aorta is normal in caliber without dissection, pseudoaneurysm or other acute abnormality.  The great vessels are unremarkable.  The heart size is normal.  There is no pericardial effusion.  A small amount of soft tissue density material in the anterior mediastinum is likely residual thymic tissue, which is prominent for a patient of this age.  There is no mediastinal or hilar lymphadenopathy by size criteria.  Lungs are clear without consolidation or pleural effusion.  The tracheobronchial tree is patent to subsegmental levels.  The distal esophagus demonstrates mild wall thickening.  CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: Replaced or accessory left hepatic artery is noted.  The liver demonstrates mild intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  The common duct is larger than on the prior study, measuring up to 9 mm (___).  The gallbladder is distended, but there is no wall thickening, pericholecystic fluid or radiodense gallstone.  No stones are noted within the duct.  The pancreas is normal.  There is no pancreatic ductal dilatation.  The spleen, adrenal glands, stomach and duodenum are unremarkable.  The kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically without hydronephrosis or worrisome renal masses.  The abdominal aorta and its major branches are normal.  There is no free air or free fluid in the abdomen.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy by size criteria.  CT PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: Loops of large and small bowel are unremarkable.  There is no obstruction.  The appendix is normal.  The uterus is enlarged diffusely, with fibroids inferiorly and the suggestion of adenomyosis based on enlargement and heterogeneity.  A nodular density protrudes superiorly from the posterior uterine wall, but probably represents an unusual appearance of the left ovary given an otherwise unremarkable appearance on reformatted images.  The right ovary is unremarkable.  The urinary bladder and distal ureters are within normal limits.  There is a physiologic amount of fluid in the pelvis.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy by size criteria.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: The previously noted minimally displaced fracture of the posterior acetabulum on the left is unchanged.  No new fractures are identified.  The linear density within the left hip joint base is again noted, somewhat changed in configuration.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26626481',
    '10559181-RR-36',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multiplanar T1- and T2-weighted images of the abdomen acquired on a 1.5 Tesla magnet including dynamic 3D imaging obtained prior to, during, and after the uneventful IV administration of 0.1 mmol/kg gadolinium-DTPA.  Multiplanar 2D and 3D reformations and subtraction images were generated on an independent workstation.  MR OF THE ABDOMEN WITH IV GADOLINIUM (MRCP):  Peripancreatic edema is consistent with ____.  There is homogeneous enhancement of the pancreas and the pancreatic duct is normal in caliber.  A fluid-fluid level in the gallbladder is probably explained by concentrated bile.  There are no gallstones. There is a tiny simple hepatic cyst in segment V. There is a small subcentimeter rounded lesion of segment VII which is hyperintense to parenchyma on T2- weighted images and shows contrast enhancement following the blood pool, consistent with hemangioma. There is minimal dilatation of the common duct up to 8 mm. There is no intrahepatic bile duct dilataion. The common bile duct demonstrates normal distal tapering and there is no evidence of choledocholithiasis.  The gallbladder is normal without stones. The spleen, adrenal glands, kidneys, and GI tract are unremarkable.  The central mesenteric and hepatic vasculature are patent.  Multiplanar 2D and 3D reformations and subtraction images generated on an independent workstation were valuable in assessment of the biliary tree.'
);


