INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27824170',
    '10811759-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Contiguous axial images were obtained through the abdomen with the administration of oral and IV contrast.  Multiplanar reformats were generated and reviewed.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN: The visualized lung bases are clear with no focal consolidation or pleural effusion.  The visualized heart and pericardium are unremarkable.    The liver appears low in attenuation consistent with fatty infiltration with some areas of fat sparing adjacent to gallbladder fossa.  The gallbladder, spleen and bilateral adrenal glands appear unremarkable.  Both kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically without evidence of hydronephrosis or renal calculi.  There is swelling of the pancreatic head with slight decrease in attenuation of the pancreatic head, suggesting edema and extensive stranding surrounding the pancreas, with inflammatory change and fluid tracking along the second and third parts of the duodenum, around the porta hepatis and down along the anterior pararenal space as well as posterior to the ascending colon.  There is no evidence of free air.  The common bile duct shows mild periductal enhancement at the level of the head of the pancreas.  No calcified gallstone is noted.  There is no intrahepatic bile duct dilation.    The abdominal aorta appears unremarkable.  The portal vein, splenic vein, and SMV appear patent. An area of soft tissue identical to the prior examination in the portacaval space measures 20 x 9 mm and is unchanged in size compared to the most recent prior examination and most likely represents a portacaval lymph node.  A calcified structure anterior to the esophagus (2:8) is unchanged from the prior examination may represent a calcified node.Intra-abdominal loops of large bowel appear grossly unremarkable.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: Four consecutive thoracic vertebral bodies show presence of Schmorl''s nodes.  Slight anterior wedging of L1 and L2 are noted.  Mild kyphosis is noted. Overall, findings may indicate prior Sc___''s disease.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27824170',
    '10811759-RR-22',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multiplanar T1- and T2-weighted images were acquired on a 1.5 T magnet including dynamic 3D imaging obtained prior to, during, and after the uneventful intravenous administration of 0.1 mmol/kg of gadolinium-DTPA.  Comparison is made to CT examination from ___.  FINDINGS: Extensive periduodenal inflammation is noted around the second and third part of the duodenum.  The inflammatory change extends into the right pararenal space / retroperitoneum and around the hepatic flexure. Hemorrhagic material is present within the inflammatory debris / phlegmon. Minor inflammatory change is noted in the posterior aspect of the head of pancreas, the remainder of the pancreas is normal. The pancreatic parenchyma shows normal precontrast signal intensity throughout.  There is no pancreatic duct dilatation.    The common bile duct appears narrowed as it passes behind the duodenal inflammation but no filling defects are identified and there is no intrahepatic biliary dilation.  Innumerable foci of mildly low signal intensity on T2WI are seen within the moderately distended gallbladder lumen most consistent with sludge and sludge balls are present in the gallbladder, but no definite evidence of gallstones.  The portal vein and hepatic veins are patent.  The spleen, adrenal glands, and kidneys are normal.    Impression: 1.  Extensive inflammation of the second and third part of the duodenum with adjacent retroperitoneal phlegmon.  Appearance is most suggestive of duodenitis however we note the duodenum was reportedly normal at recent EGD, perhaps the inflammation is predominantly mural rather than mucosal. Inflammatory change from duodenal perforation could also have this appearance. Groove or focal ____ is considered though felt less likely as there is minimal MR evidence of inflammatory change of the pancreas.  2.  Colonic edema at the hepatic flexure, felt likely secondary to involvement by the above described process. 3.  Distended gallbladder containing apparent sludge and debris.'
);


