INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26349833',
    '10958256-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'EXAM: CT of the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous contrast.  TECHNIQUE: 64-slice MDCT data were acquired from the abdomen and pelvis after the administration of 100 cc of IV Optiray contrast.  Images displayed in multiple planes.  FINDINGS:   ABDOMEN WITH CONTRAST: The visualized lung bases are free of nodules, consolidations, or effusions.  There are no focal liver lesions.  There is no intra- or extra-hepatic biliary dilatation.  The common bile duct measures 7 mm in maximum diameter (300b:18).  The gallbladder is mildly distended, but the wall is thin and there are no stones visualized.  The pancreas appears normal with no pancreatic duct dilatation.  There is no evidence of peripancreatic fat stranding, pancreatic necrosis, or abscess.  The spleen and adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically.  Patient is status post gastric bypass with staple lines visible at proximal and distal anastomotic sites.  There is no dilation of stomach, small or large bowel to indicate obstruction.  Post-surgical anatomy is incompletely visualized due to the absence of oral contrast.  There is a fat-containing periumbilical ventral abdominal wall hernia (2:50) with a minimal amount of surrounding stranding.  This is most likely incisional in origin.  The diameter of the defect is 20 mm.  There is no bowel within the defect.  PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: The remainder of the small and large bowel are normal.  There is a normal appendix.  There is normal uterus and ovaries.  There is no free pelvic fluid.  The bladder is normal.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  CT ANGIOGRAM: The celiac axis, SMA, and ___ are patent.  There are no concerning bowel loops for ischemia.  BONE WINDOWS: There are no fractures or concerning lytic or sclerotic lesions.'
);


