    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: CTA of the abdomen and pelvis was performed without oral contrast. IV contrast was administered.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN: At the right lower lobe there are several new ground-glass opacities (2:7) consistent with developing infectious process.  No pleural effusion is seen.  The right lung is clear.  Pericardium is unremarkable.  Within the abdomen, a 1 cm segment VII hypodense lesion appears consistent with a hemangioma.  Patient is status post cholecystectomy.  Portal vein is patent.  Splenic vein is patent.  Pancreas is unremarkable.  Spleen is normal. Bilateral kidneys excrete and enhance contrast symmetrically with no evidence of solid masses or hydronephrosis.  Stomach and small bowel are unremarkable.  Abdominal aorta is normal in course and caliber.  No abdominal lymphadenopathy is identified.  There is no abdominal free fluid.  CT OF THE PELVIS: Oral contrast is seen within the patient''s rectum and sigmoid colon presumably from an outside scan in the interim.  There is a metallic object in the patient''s cecum (2:44), also noted on the radiographs.  There is no evidence of diverticulitis, however there is diverticulosis.  Appendix is normal.  Prostate and seminal vesicles are unremarkable.  Bladder is normal.  No pelvic free fluid.  BONES: No suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesions are seen.'
);


