INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '23670641',
    '11112100-RR-16',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CTA CHEST W&W/O C&RECONS, NON-CORONARY',
    'EXAMINATION: CTA of the chest and abdomen, ___.  TECHNIQUE: Volumetric CT acquisition of the chest and abdomen was performed following administration of intravenous contrast per CTA dissection protocol. Multiplanar reformatted images were obtained and reviewed.  CTA CHEST: Thoracic aorta is normal in caliber and contour throughout.  There is no dissection.  Heart is mildly enlarged, with evidence of left ventricular hypertrophy.  There is no pleural or pericardial effusion.  Central airways are patent to the subsegmental level.  There is no pathologic intrathoracic lymphadenopathy.  Evaluation of the lung parenchyma is markedly limited due to respiratory motion artifact, and expiratory phase of scan acquisition.  CTA ABDOMEN: Abdominal aorta is normal in caliber and contour throughout. There is no dissection.  Liver contour is normal.  There is no biliary ductal dilatation or ascites. Evaluation is limited by single arterial phase of contrast.  There is somewhat ill-defined heterogeneous appearance to the parenchyma in the right lobe of the liver. Periportal edema noted.  Main portal vein is just beginning to opacify, limiting evaluation for portal vein thrombus. Small amount of fluid is also seen in the porta hepatis, tracking along the region of the third portion of the duodenum. The duodenum itself is somewhat difficult to evaluate as it is largely collapsed. The stomach is mildly distended, with fluid and ingested material. There is a small amount of pericholecystic fluid though the GB is not fully distended.   Pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands, and kidneys are normal.  There is no free air or free intraperitoneal fluid.  There is misty appearance to the abdominal mesentery in the left upper quadrant, with multiple small lymph nodes.  CT PELVIS: The cecum is moderately distended with a large amount of fecal material.  Fecal material is also seen in the terminal ileum, which is borderline distended, measuring just greater than 3 cm.  Fecal material is seen within the small bowel as far proximal as the mid ileum in the right upper quadrant.  Proximal loops of small bowel are collapsed, however, and there is no sign of obstruction.  No definite mass is seen in the region of the ileocecal valve.  The appendix is normal.  There is no free fluid, or abnormal intra-abdominal lymphadenopathy.  There is no osseous lesion suspicious for malignancy.'
);


