INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26193253',
    '11197798-RR-23',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Contrast-enhanced axial MDCT images through the abdomen and pelvis with coronal and sagittal reformats.  FINDINGS: Lung bases are notable for minimal dependent atelectasis.  There is no pleural effusion.  Liver enhances homogeneously without focal lesions.  However, there is moderate intra- and extra-hepatic biliary dilatation with the common bile duct measuring up to 9 mm in diameter and extends to the ampula.  No definite obstructing lesion is identified.  The gallbladder is not clearly visualized.  A small amount of free fluid is noted inferior to the liver in proximity to the duodenal sweep. The proximal duodenum demonstrates wall thickening, from primary duodenitis or reactive. The spleen is normal.  Pancreas is mildly atrophic but contains a normal caliber duct.  Bilateral adrenal nodules measure 13 x 20 mm on the right (2:27) and 23 x 37 mm on the left. Kidneys enhance and excrete symmetrically, containing subcentimeter hypodensities that are too small to characterize.  There is no hydronephrosis.  The stomach is largely collapsed; again mild wall thickening is noted in the region of the distal stomach and proximal duodenum. This area is in close proximity to the common hepatic duct.  The remaining loops of small bowel are normal in course and caliber without wall thickening or signs of obstruction.  Multiple surgical anastomotic sutures are noted within the colon which is otherwise unremarkable.  The sigmoid colon is notable for diverticulosis without diverticulitis.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  There is no free air.  Extensive atherosclerotic calcifications are noted along the abdominal aorta and its major branches, without evidence of aneurysmal dilatation.  The bladder is partially collapsed.  The uterus is not well visualized and there are no adnexal masses.  There is no pelvic lymphadenopathy or free fluid.  Bones are notable for multilevel degenerative changes in the spine including grade I anterolisthesis of L3 on L4 and minimal anterolisthesis of L4 on L5.  Degenerative changes are also noted in the hips and the SI joints bilaterally.'
);


