    'Abdomen',
    'RENAL TRANSPLANT U.S.',
    'TRANSPLANT PANCREATIC SONOGRAM: Grayscale examination of the right pelvic pancreatic transplant demonstrates no dilatation of the main pancreatic duct. No parenchymal abnormality or peripancreatic fluid collection is noted.  Color Doppler examination demonstrates blood flow throughout the pancreas. Pulse Doppler examination demonstrates patency of the portal vein, with normal direction of flow towards the iliac vessels.  Normal arterial waveforms are also demonstrated within the pancreatic parenchyma, with resistive indices measuring 0.67, 0.67, and 0.64 in the head, body, and tail, respectively.  TRANSPLANT RENAL ULTRASOUND: Grayscale sonographic exam over the left lower quadrant renal transplant demonstrates no evidence of hydronephrosis, mass, stone, or perinephric fluid collection.  The kidney measures 10.0 cm, which is unchanged.  Color Doppler examination demonstrates flow throughout the kidney.  Normal arterial intraparenchymal waveforms are demonstrated in the kidney, with resistive indices measuring 0.70, 0.66, and 0.74 in the upper, mid, and lower pole of the kidney respectively.  These are not changed from the prior study of ___ where the resistive indices ranged between 0.62 and 0.67.  Normal waveforms are also demonstrated in the main renal artery and vein.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26435839',
    '11248793-RR-185',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Axial CT images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following administration of 100 cc of intravenous Visipaque contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were also reviewed.  CT ABDOMEN WITH CONTRAST: The included portions of the lung bases are notable for bibasilar atelectasis. The cardiac apex reveals extensive atherosclerotic calcification of the mitral annulus as well as the coronary arteries.  The stomach, duodenum, spleen are unremarkable.  The patient is status post cholecystectomy.  The native pancreas is not identified.  Note is made of pronounced atherosclerotic calcification throughout the abdomen.  The native kidneys are present and markedly atrophic.  The adrenal glands are unremarkable.  The liver is unremarkable.  Note is made of diffuse hypodensity throughout the mesentery, suggestive of diffuse edema, in addition, there is periportal edema noted through the liver.  There is no free gas in the abdomen.  There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  CT PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: A transplant kidney is present in the left lower quadrant.  The kidney enhances and excretes contrast, and there is no evidence of hydronephrosis. The urinary bladder is notable for diffuse centrally hypodense tubular structures adjacent to it on the right, likely anastomosed duodenum secondary to pancreatic transplant.  Loops are notable for apparent mural thickening and extensive surrounding fat stranding and hypodensity suggestive of surrounding edema.  Notably, there is essentially isodense material within these loops (2:68), which is non-specific in appearance.  The transplanted pancreatic tissue itself is at the periphery of the zone of inflammation, and appears unremarkable with no evidence of ductal dilation. There is no discernable abscess.  There is no free gas in the pelvis.  Note is made of a pessary device.  The rectum and colon are unremarkable, and the appendix is not identified.  There is no pelvic side wall or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  Note is made of diffuse subcutaneous fat stranding over the anterior aspect of the pelvis.  OSSEOUS FINDINGS: There is no suspicious sclerotic or lytic osseous lesion.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '22605409',
    '11303674-RR-29',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images were obtained through the abdomen and pelvis following administration of intravenous contrast material.  Multiplanar reformatted images were generated.  CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: At the lung bases, lungs are clear, without consolidation or pleural effusion.  The heart is normal in size, without pericardial effusion.  In the abdomen, the liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands, stomach and duodenum are unremarkable.  The patient has undergone prior urinary diversion with bilateral ureters extending to a conduit in the right lower quadrant. The kidneys enhance and excrete contrast relatively symmetrically without stones, or renal masses. However, the right renal pelvis and ureter are minimally larger than on the prior study, with slightly slower egress of contrast through the ureter than on the left. This could reflect reflux from the ileal conduit, vs mild stenosis at the anastomosis. Bilaterally, renal cortical thinning is unchanged and consistent with scarring, possibly from prior infection.  There is no free air or free fluid in the abdomen. The abdominal aorta and major branches are within normal limits. There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy by size criteria.  CT PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: Multiple loops of large and small bowel are decompressed without evidence of obstruction.  The ostomy in the right lower quadrant is stable. A large stool-filled structure tracks immediately anterior to the uterus in the left pelvic midline. This is likely a large diverticulum, given long-term stability in its appearance, with no change in size, position and contents since ___.  The uterus again demonstrates multiple fibroids. There are no adnexal abnormalities. There is no free fluid in the pelvis. There is no inguinal or pelvic lymphadenopathy by size criteria.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: Lumbosacral spina bifida with posterior meningocele is unchanged.  There is no fracture or worrisome bony lesion.  Soft tissues are unremarkable.'
);


