INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20804346',
    '11309808-RR-27',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CTA CHEST W&W/O C&RECONS, NON-CORONARY',
    'EXAM: Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis.  TECHNIQUE: Contiguous axial images of the chest were obtained prior to and following the administration of 130 cc of Optiray intravenous contrast. Contiguous axial images of the abdomen and pelvis were also obtained following the administration of the aforementioned intravenous contrast.  No oral contrast was administered.  Reformatted coronal, sagittal, and oblique images of the chest are obtained.  Reformatted coronal and sagittal images of the abdomen and pelvis were also obtained.  FINDINGS:   CHEST: The aorta is normal in contour without evidence of dissection.  No concerning filling defect in the pulmonary arteries or branches is seen to suggest acute pulmonary embolism.  No pleural or pericardial effusion is seen. No mediastinal or axillary lymphadenopathy is seen.  Prominent right hilar lymph node measures 0.9 cm in short axis.  There is mild bibasilar subsegmental atelectasis.  A coarse 7-mm calcification in the right breast is noted.  ABDOMEN: Four intrahepatic lesions are again seen, demonstrating peripheral puddling of contrast, and which have been hyperechoic on ultrasound, consistent with hemangiomas.  The largest is in segment VII and measures 6.1 x 3.6 cm (series 3B, image 79).  In segment VI, on series 3B, image 102, there is a 2.2 x 1.4-cm hemangioma.  Hemangioma in segment IV/V measures 1.9 x 2.1-cm.  In segment II/III on series 3B, image 87, there is a 1.5 x 1.2-cm lesion demonstrating non-continuous peripheral puddling, consistent with hemangioma.  The gallbladder is distended.  Layering stones seen on ultrasound are not as well appreciated on this CT due to differences in modality.  There is a small amount of pericholecystic fluid. Mild central intrahepatic biliary dilatation is noted.  The common bile duct is dilated, measuring up to 1.1 cm in diameter, with relatively abrupt tapering distally.  Just beyond the level of abrupt tapering, there is subtle high density in the distal CBD, best seen on series 501B, image 21, which may represent intraluminal layering sludge/stone or lesion.  The pancreas is enlarged in size compared to the previous study without discrete pancreatic mass seen.  The pancreatic duct, including side branches are prominent. The main pancreatic duct is prominent, but not frankly dilated, measuring up to 2 mm in diameter.  Peripancreatic fluid is seen.  In conjunction with patient''s elevated serum lipase, findings are compatible with ____. No evidence of frank pancreatic necrosis is seen.  No peripancreatic drainable fluid collection is identified.  Mesenteric/upper abdominal free fluid is noted, measuring greater than that of simple fluid (measuring Hounsfield units averaging from 35 to 51), consistent with complex fluid, which may contain blood products.  No upper abdominal free air is seen.  The spleen, adrenal glands, and kidneys are unremarkable.  The portal and splenic veins are patent.  PELVIS: The sigmoid and rectum are relatively collapsed.  The bowel is difficult to trace, given lack of intra-abdominal fat and lack of oral contrast. The transverse colon is at least partially collapsed, distally, and is difficult to trace.  The cecum lies deep in the pelvis.  The appendix is tentatively identified in the midline of the pelvis, is air filled, and not dilated. Multiple loops of small bowel are prominent, but not dilated, measuring up to 2.1 cm in diameter and contain "feces sign" with air and fecal contents seen, which may be due to stasis, possibly from ileus from the upper abdominal findings.   The bladder is distended but unremarkable.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: Mild degenerative changes at the lumbosacral junction are noted with intervertebral disc space narrowing at L5/S1 and small anterior and posterior osteophyte formation.  No concerning osteolytic or blastic lesion is seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29105302',
    '11313489-RR-17',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT helical acquisition was performed from the diaphragm to the pubic symphysis following the uneventful administration of IV and oral contrast.  Multiplanar reformations were provided.  CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: The lung bases demonstrate moderate subsegmental atelectasis.  The patient is post-cholecystectomy.  The CBD is dilated to 13 mm; comparison with the prior studies may be helpful.  The pancreatic duct appears slightly prominent but is not overly dilated.  There is a pancreatic duct stent, which protrudes approximately 2.2 cm into the lumen of the duodenum (300b:27).  There is a large paraesophageal hiatal hernia (2:13). Oral contrast is seen distally and therefor there is no obstruction. There is thickening of the left anterior pararenal fascia (2:31) in this patient with chronic ____.  Suture material is seen at the ileocolonic junction, and there has been prior appendectomy.  There is no focal fluid collection or lymphadenopathy.  CT PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The uterus appears unremarkable.  The bladder and rectum appear normal.  BONES: There is grade I anterolisthesis of L4 upon L5 (301b:39).'
);


