INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29105302',
    '11313489-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Contiguous helical acquisition through the chest, abdomen and pelvis was performed with intravenous and oral contrast.  Coronal and sagittally reformatted images were created.  FINDINGS:   CT CHEST: The heart is top normal in size.  Multiple prominent mediastinal lymph nodes are identified measuring up to 9 mm in short axis in the precarinal space.  The tracheobronchial tree is patent to level of the subsegmental bronchi bilaterally.  There is interlobular septal thickening and scattered ground-glass opacities most consistent with mild pulmonary edema. Also noted are small bilateral pleural effusions and compressive atelectasis. There is no pneumothorax.  No nodules or masses are identified.  CT ABDOMEN: There is redemonstration of a large paraesophageal hiatal hernia. Oral contrast passes beyond the hernia and there is no evidence of obstruction.  The liver, spleen, adrenal glands, kidneys, and bowel are normal in appearance.  The fat stranding fascial thickening involving the left anterior perirenal fascia has resolved.  Again noted is a pancreatic stent protruding approximately 2 cm into the duodenum.  The pancreatic duct is prominent but not dilated.  The common bile duct measures 1.3 cm in diameter, unchanged from the prior study.  Surgical clips are noted in the gallbladder fossa, status post cholecystectomy.  No free fluid is noted in the abdomen. There is no free air.  No mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy is identified.  There is atherosclerotic calcification of the descending aorta. Mild multilevel degenerative changes noted throughout the spine.  There is grade 1 anterolisthesis of L4 on L5 likely degenerative in nature secondary to facet arthropathy.  No suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesions are identified.'
);


