)
VALUES (
    '29059416',
    '11461906-RR-35',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN)',
    'RIGHT UPPER QUADRANT ULTRASOUND: The liver is normal in echotexture without focal abnormalities.  There is no intra- or extra-hepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  The common duct measures 4 mm.  The main portal vein demonstrates normal hepatopetal flow.  There is no ascites.  The gallbladder is normal, without evidence of stones.  There is no pericholecystic fluid, wall thickening, or other evidence of acute cholecystitis.  The pancreatic head and body are unremarkable.  The pancreatic tail is not well seen due to overlying bowel gas.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25444703',
    '11475386-RR-18',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: This study was imported from an outside institution for re-review. A second opinion interpretation was requested as only a brief verbal report of the study was known at the time.  MDCT-acquired axial images of the abdomen and pelvis were obtained with IV and oral contrast at 5-mm slice thickness.  Additional coronal and sagittal reconstructions were performed at 3-mm slice thickness.  FINDINGS: Included views of the lungs demonstrate mild dependent atelectasis bilaterally.  There is no pericardial or pleural effusion.  The heart size is top normal.  A large multicystic mass is seen at the head of the pancreas (2:32), measuring up to 50 x 47 mm axially.  Coarse internal calcifications are seen (2:34).  There are multiple irregular septations with varying thickness.  This lesion abuts the second portion of the duodenum, where there is asymmetric wall thickening (2:31).  Neighboring fat stranding is present.  The distal pancreas appears normal.  The pancreatic duct is not dilated, measuring 3 mm.  The portal vein, SMV, and splenic veins are patent and appear separate from the lesion.  The SMA is preserved. A prominent retrocrural node (2:29) does not meet CT criteria for lymphadenopathy.  The CBD is normal in caliber.  The gallbladder is unremarkable.  A 15 mm right hepatic cyst is present (2:11).  Other subcentimeter hypodense lesions within the liver (2: 11,25,29) are too small for further characterization.  The spleen, adrenal glands, stomach, and kidneys are normal.  Mild atherosclerotic calcification of the abdominal aorta is present.  There is no mesenteric lymphadenopathy, and no free air or free fluid.   The rectum, sigmoid colon, intrapelvic loops of small and large bowel, bladder, uterus, and adnexa appear normal. There is no intrapelvic lymphadenopathy or free fluid.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There is no acute fracture or dislocation.  No concerning blastic or lytic lesions are seen.  Anterior osteophytosis at L2/3 is seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25444703',
    '11475386-RR-19',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CTA ABD W&W/O C & RECONS',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were acquired from the lung bases to the lower abdomen before and after administration of IV contrast.  Multiplanar reformatted images were obtained for evaluation.  CT ABDOMEN WITH AND WITHOUT CONTRAST: There is a small amount of pleural effusion tracking along the left major fissure.  There is no focal airspace consolidation in the visualized lung bases.  The liver is noted with three hypodense lesions, with the larger one in segment VIII, measuring 1.7 x 1.5 cm, with well-circumscribed margin, likely a hepatic cyst.  The other two foci are in the left hepatic lobe, subcentimeter, too small to be fully evaluated.  There is no intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  Vicarious contrast excretion is seen pooling in the dependent portion of an otherwise normal gallbladder.  The spleen, adrenal glands, and kidneys are normal.  There is normal excretion of IV contrast into the collecting system bilaterally without hydronephrosis.  The stomach is unremarkable.  In the second portion of the duodenum abutting the tumor, there is evidence of duodenal wall thickening, unchanged, could also represent tumor infiltration or reactive inflammation.  In the pancreatic head, there is a large heterogeneously lesion, with cystic components and internal calcification, measure up to 4.4 x 4.1 x 4.3 cm, essentially unchanged in appearance compared to the study two days ago.  There is no distal pancreatic ductal dilatation or atrophy.  Major intra-abdominal vessels are patent.  Specifically, the SMV appears to abut the pancreatic head mass, but is without encroachment.  There is a thin fat plane separating the SMV and the pancreatic head.  There is no evidence of encroachment on other adjacent major vessels.  There is normal intra-abdominal vascular anatomy.  A tiny retrocrural node is again seen, measuring 12 x 4 mm (image 3B:135).  Small periportal and aortocaval nodes are not pathologically enlarged.  There is no definite intra-abdominal lymphadenopathy.  No free fluid or air is noted.  BONE WINDOW: There are no suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesions.  Multilevel degenerative changes are mild, with anterior osteophytosis.'
);


