INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21111022',
    '11490406-RR-18',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'CT OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS:   TECHNIQUE: Multidetector CT images of the abdomen and pelvis were obtained with oral and intravenous contrast.  Sagittal and coronal reformations were also performed.  FINDINGS: Shortly after the study, a preliminary interpretation was provided by Dr. ___ to Dr. ___ from surgery in person at 7:50 p.m. on ___ that stated:  "1.  New stranding surrounding the pancreas is compatible with acute ____.  A 0.9 x 1.2 cm cystic lesion in the pancreatic tail (2:18) is grown since ___ and ___ and may be a pseudocyst as patient has had prior ____ versus IPMN. 2.  Cholelithiasis with decompressed gallbladder.  No CT evidence of perforation. 3.  No bowel obstruction. 4.  Perinephric stranding is nonspecific."  CT ABDOMEN: The patient is status post coronary artery bypass graft surgery.  The heart is mildly enlarged, especially right-sided chambers.  There are no pleural effusions.    The liver appears within normal limits.  The gallbladder is decompressed with a small Phrygian cap.  Small layering dependent calcified stones are present.  There is no substantial biliary dilatation.  The entirety of the pancreas shows surrounding fat stranding that is new since the prior CT torso most consistent with ____.  In the pancreatic tail there is a cystic lesion measuring 9 x 12 mm in axial ___ (2:18) which is similar to the recent prior study although increased since the earliest study available from ___, a CT study that showed the lesion as measuring up to 9 x 8 mm in axial ___.  It appears slightly lobular but internal structure is not well evaluated here.    The spleen is normal in size and appearance.  The adrenal glands appear within normal limits.  New bilateral perinephric stranding is probably secondary to pancreatic inflammation tracking into the retroperitoneum posteriorly or perhaps fluid overload.  The stomach, small and large bowel appear unremarkable.  Contrast has passed through the entirety of the colon.  No findings suggest small bowel obstruction.  CT PELVIS: The distal ureters and bladder appear within normal limits.  The uterus, if present, is not conspicuous and may have been removed.  The aorta and common iliac arteries are tortuous.  The major mesenteric arteries and veins appear patent.  Vascular calcifications are widespread without an aneurysm.  There are no enlarged lymph nodes.  A surgical clip is present in the left groin.  BONE WINDOWS: There are no suspicious lytic or blastic lesions.  The bones are probably demineralized to some degree.  Moderate sclerosis at the pubic symphysis is probably degenerative.  Moderate rightward convex curvature is centered along the mid-to-upper lumbar spine.  The L5-S1 level may be partly fused.  A very small hemangioma is present in the T11 vertebral body.  The L2-L3 through L4-L5 interspaces are moderately narrowed.'
);


