    'Chest',
    'CHEST (PA & LAT)',
    'TWO VIEWS OF THE CHEST: The cardiomediastinal contour again demonstrates a tortuous aorta but with a sharp contour.  There is interval midline sternotomy and clips are seen in the upper mediastinum related to graft placement.  There is no focal consolidation or evidence of congestive heart failure. There is a small right-sided pleural effusion, best appreciated on the lateral view. Osseous structures and soft tissues are unremarkable.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27747379',
    '11585755-RR-33',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CTA CHEST W&W/O C&RECONS, NON-CORONARY',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT helical acquisition was performed from the thoracic inlet to the pubic symphysis following the uneventful administration of IV contrast. Multiplanar reformations were provided.  CT CHEST WITH IV CONTRAST: There has been interval aortic valve-sparing aortic root replacement with prosthetic graft and replacement of the ascending aorta and hemiarch.  There is perigraft fluid which is predominantly fluid density, and can be seen in the postoperative setting. There is no evidence of aortic dissection.  There is residual soft tissue surrounding the native aorta which extends to the abdominal aorta, but is also seen around the branches of the great vessels as previously described and imaged on ___. There is no conclusive evidence of endoleak.  At approximately ___ o''clock (on axial images) at the level of the distal graft anastomotic site, several locules of air have a linear arrangement in the craniocaudal direction (3:34).  This appearance is indeterminate, and may relate to residual postoperative gas, but has an appearance reminiscent of surgical packing material (given linearity and confluence).  There is a small amount of retrosternal fluid as well as anterior mediastinal air seen just below the pulmonary arterial bifurcation (3:50).  The lungs demonstrate no focal consolidation, and the trachea and bronchi are patent to the subsegmental level.  There is a trace right pleural effusion (3:73).  The sternotomy margins are well defined with no evidence of interval bony bridging or healing.  CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: The periaortic smooth soft tissue density is again noted.  Note is made of two right and two left renal arteries.  Although intravenous contrast timing was optimized for evaluation of the artery, and not the parenchymal organs, the liver, spleen, pancreas, adrenal glands, kidneys, and bowel appear unremarkable. There is no free abdominal air or fluid collection.  There is no lymphadenopathy.  CT PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The rectum, sigmoid colon, uterus, and bladder appear unremarkable.  There is no abnormal fluid collection or lymphadenopathy.  Osseous structures appear unremarkable aside from the described sternotomy appearance.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
