    '11744921-RR-38',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast.  No oral contrast was administered.  FINDINGS: There is no focal consolidation or pleural effusion at the lung bases. There is a 0.7 x 0.6 cm rounded right paraesophageal high density on the upper most image, which may represent a calcified lymph node, not fully evaluated.  Heart size is normal.  There is no pericardial effusion.  Liver enhances homogeneously with no focal masses seen. Gallstone seen on ultrasound is not definitely appreciated on the CT, likely due to difference in technique.  The spleen is normal.  Peripancreatic fat stranding is seen, which in conjunction with elevated serum lipase, is consistent with ____.  No drainable fluid collection is seen.  The pancreatic head is enlarged without discrete mass or ductal dilatation seen, may be secondary to ____.  Pancreas enhances homogeneously.  The adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast symmetrically with no evidence of hydronephrosis.  Evidence of scarring is seen in the lateral left kidney 2:34, may be sequela of prior infection.  There is no free fluid or free air. There is no bowel obstruction. Loops of small and large bowel appear within normal limits. Prominent retroperitoneal lymph nodes measure up to 1cm in short axis in the left para-aortic region (S2:I38).  A left common iliac node (S2:I57) measuring 1.1 cm in short axis.  CT PELVIS: Urinary bladder appears normal.  There are multiple uterine fibroids, some of which are calcified.  There is a 1.1 cm in short axis left external iliac lymph node (S2:I71).  0.7 cm right external iliac (S2:I68) and 0.9 cm right pelvic side wall (S2:I71) lymph nodes are noted, non-specific.  No free fluid in the pelvis.  There are prominent lymph nodes in the pelvic and inguinal area.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: No suspicious lytic or sclerotic lesions are seen.'
);


