INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27988136',
    '12042083-RR-17',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multiplanar T1- and T2-weighted images of the abdomen acquired on a 1.5 Tesla magnet including dynamic 3D imaging obtained prior to, during and after the uneventful IV administration of 27 mL Magnevist gadolinium contrast. Prior to the examination 300 mL GastroMARK and 450 mL Redicat contrast were administered by mouth.  Multiplanar 2D and 3D reformations and subtraction images were generated on an independent workstation.  MR OF THE ABDOMEN WITH IV GADOLINIUM: There is a small amount of fluid around the pancreatic body and tail consistent with ____.  The pancreatic duct is smooth with normal caliber.  A 7 mm cystic lesion of the pancreatic body adjacent to the main duct has no complex features or solid enhancement. There are numerous gallstones within the gallbladder.  The intra- and extra-hepatic bile ducts are normal in caliber.  Two tiny equivocal filling defects are noted which could represent nonobstructing common duct stones.  One is noted in the common hepatic duct (3:2) and the other in the distal common bile duct (6:29, 5:57).  A 12-mm lesion of hepatic segment IV is mildly hyperintense to adjacent hepatic parenchyma on T2- weighted images and shows progressive peripheral nodular contrast enhancement, consistent with hemangioma.  There is decrease in signal intensity of the background hepatic parenchyma on T1- weighted opposed-phase images relative to in-phase images consistent with fatty infiltration.  There are no concerning hepatic lesions.  There is a tiny simple cyst of the right kidney. The spleen is normal in size.  The adrenal glands, left kidney, stomach and loops of bowel are unremarkable.  The celiac trunk, SMA and renal arteries are patent.  There is no focal bone lesion of concern.  Multiplanar 2D and 3D reformations and subtraction images were essential in evaluation of the biliary tree and pancreas.'
);


