INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26561933',
    '12095158-RR-7',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CTA ABD W&W/O C & RECONS',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images acquired from the lung bases down to the superior iliac crest with IV and enteral contrast in multiple phases.  Multiplanar reconstructions were obtained and reviewed.  CT OF THE CHEST WITH IV CONTRAST: The visualized lung bases show right greater than left small pleural effusions along with mild bibasilar atelectasis.  The visualized heart appears normal in size with no evidence of any vascular or valvular calcifications.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH IV AND ENTERAL CONTRAST: The liver is normal in size with no evidence of any intrahepatic biliary dilatation, portal or venous thrombi.  The spleen is normal with no calcifications granulomas or infarcts.  The gallbladder is distended with gallbladder wall edema.  Two stones are seen within the lumen of the gallbladder.  The common bile duct is not dilated and is seen ending at the duodenum without evidence of any stones, dilatation, or strictures.  Again seen are multiple hypodense well-circumscribed lesions of the pancreas, most prominently in the uncinate process (3B:171, 21 mm x 23 mm).  These are essentially unchanged from prior.  Also noticed is peripancreatic fat stranding which is concerning for ____. There is an oblong hypodense lesion measuring 1cm in diameter posterior to the body of the pancreas in some regions this does have an enhancing wall however this is incomplete.  The bilateral adrenal glands are thickened without obvious nodules.  The bilateral kidneys appear normal with no evidence of any cysts, nodules, or masses.  The bilateral ureters appear normal.  The visualized small and large bowel are normal with no evidence of any stranding, inflammatory changes or nodules.  Mild amount of fluid is seen in the left subhepatic space and around the gallbladder wall.  No obvious abdominal lymphadenopathy is seen.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: Visualized osseous structures appear normal with no evidence of any suspicious lytic or blastic lesions.'
);


