INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26756106',
    '10119863-RR-56',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Oral contrast was not administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Stationary Acquisition 7.5 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 36.1 mGy (Body) DLP = 18.1 mGy-cm.    2) Spiral Acquisition 6.6 s, 51.6 cm; CTDIvol = 26.1 mGy (Body) DLP = 1,347.6 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 1,366 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: There is a 10 mm subpleural left lower lobe nodule (02:10).  No pleural effusion.No pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions. There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder contains small stones or sludge, but is otherwise unremarkable.  PANCREAS: There is mild fat stranding around the pancreatic head and uncinate process concerning for ____.  The pancreatic parenchyma enhances homogeneously.  There is no evidence of associated hypoenhancement.  SMA and SMV appear patent without evidence of pseudoaneurysm formation.  No peripancreatic fluid collections.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  No hydronephrosis.  Simple cysts measure up to 8.3 cm in the upper pole of the left kidney and up to 1.8 cm in the interpolar region of the right kidney.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The stomach is unremarkable.  Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout. There is extensive sigmoid diverticulosis.  There is also diverticulosis of the remainder of the colon.  No evidence of diverticulitis.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is no free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: Prostate and seminal vesicles are unremarkable.  LYMPH NODES: There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Extensive atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26756106',
    '10119863-RR-57',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'EXAMINATION: MRCP WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: T1- and T2-weighted multiplanar images of the abdomen were acquired in a 1.5 T magnet. Intravenous contrast: 9 mL Gadavist. Oral contrast:  None.  FINDINGS: The majority of the images are degraded by motion artifact.  Lower Thorax: A 1 cm left lower lobe pulmonary nodule can be followed up with CT in 3 months as recommended in the CT report.  There is no evidence of pleural effusion or airspace consolidation at the lung bases.  Liver: The hepatic parenchyma appears within normal limits.  The postcontrast images are severely degraded by artifact, but there is no obvious focal liver lesion.  Biliary: MRCP images are technically suboptimal.  There are a few small subcentimeter gallstones lying near the gallbladder neck.  No choledochal stones are identified.  There is no biliary dilation.  Pancreas: There are mild inflammatory changes surrounding the pancreatic head as demonstrated on CT.  The pancreatic duct is not dilated and there are no focal lesions demonstrated.  No evidence of pancreatic necrosis.  Spleen: The spleen is normal in size in appearance.  Adrenal Glands: Both adrenals are unremarkable.  Kidneys: There are multiple bilateral renal cysts as demonstrated on CT.  The largest cortical cyst on the left measures 8.4 x 6.1 x 8.8 cm.  There is no hydronephrosis.  Gastrointestinal Tract:  There is extensive colonic diverticulosis.  The bowel is otherwise grossly unremarkable.  Lymph Nodes: No enlarged lymph nodes identified.  Vasculature: Unremarkable aside from atherosclerotic disease.  Osseous and Soft Tissue Structures: No aggressive bone lesions are demonstrated.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '23921797',
    '10124189-RR-28',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN)',
    'EXAMINATION: LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN):  TECHNIQUE: Grey scale and color Doppler ultrasound images of the abdomen were obtained.  FINDINGS:   LIVER: The hepatic parenchyma appears within normal limits. The contour of the liver is smooth. There is no focal liver mass. The main portal vein is patent with hepatopetal flow. There is trace ascites.  BILE DUCTS: There is no intrahepatic biliary dilation.  CHD: 7 mm.  The distal common bile duct is not visualized.  GALLBLADDER: The gallbladder is contracted and contains a large mobile gallstone.  Apparent gallbladder wall thickening is likely due to its decompressed state.  PANCREAS: The pancreas is not well visualized, largely obscured by overlying bowel gas.  SPLEEN: Normal echogenicity.   Spleen length: 11.4 cm  KIDNEYS: Limited views of the kidneys show no hydronephrosis.   Right kidney: 10.4 cm   Left kidney: 10.1 cm  RETROPERITONEUM: The visualized portions of aorta and IVC are within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '23921797',
    '10124189-RR-29',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration. Oral contrast was not administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Stationary Acquisition 3.5 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 16.9 mGy (Body) DLP = 8.4 mGy-cm.    2) Spiral Acquisition 7.1 s, 56.1 cm; CTDIvol = 18.8 mGy (Body) DLP = 1,056.0 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 1,064 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: There are small bilateral pleural effusions of simple fluid attenuation.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions. There is no evidence of intrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The common bile duct is mildly enlarged, measuring up to 0.8 cm (601:33).  A 5 mm stone is seen in the region of the ampulla.  The gallbladder is contracted and contains gallstones without wall thickening or surrounding inflammation.  There is small amount of abdominopelvic ascites.  PANCREAS: Apparent hypodense appearance of the superior pancreatic neck is likely due to volume averaging from adjacent peripancreatic fluid.  Allowing for this, the pancreas has normal attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  Despite an apparent stone within the ampulla, there is no definite pancreatic ductal dilatation.  There is mild peripancreatic stranding about the pancreatic head and minimal peripancreatic stranding about the body and tail.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There is no evidence of focal renal lesions or hydronephrosis.  There is trace bilateral perinephric fluid.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The distal esophagus is patulous.  The stomach is unremarkable.  Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout. The colon and rectum are within normal limits. The appendix is not visualized, although no secondary signs of appendicitis are seen.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is no trace fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The prostate and seminal vesicles are grossly unremarkable.  LYMPH NODES: A prominent subcentimeter portacaval lymph node is likely reactive (02:27).  Otherwise, there is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: The portal vein, splenic vein, and SMV are patent.  There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '23921797',
    '10124189-RR-30',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Single phase contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen following intravenous contrast administration. Oral contrast was not administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Spiral Acquisition 2.7 s, 35.7 cm; CTDIvol = 13.6 mGy (Body) DLP = 486.3 mGy-cm.    2) Stationary Acquisition 0.6 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 3.3 mGy (Body) DLP = 1.7 mGy-cm.    3) Stationary Acquisition 5.4 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 30.0 mGy (Body) DLP = 15.0 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 503 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Bilateral pleural effusions appear increased since ___.  Minimal compressive atelectasis is noted bilaterally.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  There is no evidence of focal lesions.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is surgically absent since last CT.  PANCREAS: Large peripancreatic partially walled off fluid collection measures at largest dimension in axial plane 11.6 x 6.3 cm with peripheral rim enhancement.  The complex fluid tracks through the anterior omentum causing mesenteric fat nodularity, through the lesser curvature to the subphrenic space superiorly, toward the gallbladder fossa and left paracolic gutter laterally and tracking inferiorly through the mesenteric folds.  Previously noted stones within the pancreas are no longer seen.  The appearance of the pancreas itself is heterogeneous given the interdigitation of the fluid within the parenchyma.  The 2 peripheral areas of hypodensity in the body and neck of the pancreas could represent fluid interdigitating through the parenchyma or early necrosis (___:31).  There is no pancreatic ductal dilatation.  A single area of free air in the anterior mesentery (02:40) is noted without evidence of viscus perforation.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There is no evidence of focal renal lesions or hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The stomach is unremarkable.  Visualized small and large bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout.  LYMPH NODES: Reactive mesenteric and retroperitoneal lymph nodes are noted.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  No atherosclerotic disease is noted.  There is no pseudoaneurysm of the splenic artery and the splenic vein is patent.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: Small amount of fluid in the dependent portions of the soft tissues.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24892555',
    '10251549-RR-3',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Oral contrast was administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence: 1) Spiral Acquisition 4.9 s, 53.4 cm; CTDIvol = 12.4 mGy (Body) DLP = 659.6 mGy-cm. Total DLP (Body) = 660 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Small bilateral effusions and right basal atelectasis is seen. There is no evidence of pleural or pericardial effusion. Cardiac pacer is in place. There is a moderate hiatal hernia with nonobstructed stomach.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout. There are multiple small hypodensities throughout the liver, the largest in segment VIII of the liver with  lobulated borders and measuring 1.1 x 2.1 cm.  These lesions most likely reflect biliary hamartomas or simple cysts, though incompletely characterized.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation. The gallbladder demonstrates layering ___ reflect sludge (02:29).  PANCREAS: There is an irregular focus of pancreatic hypodensity in the distal body of the pancreas (02:24). There is no parenchymal upstream atrophy or main pancreatic duct dilation.  In addition, there is peripancreatic stranding, extending throughout the peripancreatic space. However, no discrete fluid collection is seen.  There are multiple subcentimeter peripancreatic and mesenteric lymph nodes.  There are punctate parenchymal calcific foci in the pancreas, likely from prior ____.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  There is no pseudoaneurysm of the splenic artery. Splenic vein is patent. 1.0 cm accessory spleen is seen.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and uniformly thickened.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There are multiple parapelvic and cortical cysts in bilateral kidneys.  A slightly hyperdense partly exophytic lesion in the midpole of the left kidney measuring up to 2.0 cm may be a hemorrhagic or proteinaceous cyst.  There is no evidence of hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The stomach is unremarkable. Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout.  There is no evidence of obstruction or ileus.  Diverticulosis of the sigmoid colon is noted, without evidence of wall thickening and fat stranding.  The appendix is not visualized.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is small amount of free fluid in the pelvis and right paracolic gutter.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The visualized reproductive organs are unremarkable.  LYMPH NODES: There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy. There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm. Extensive atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  However, there is severe osteopenia and the superior and inferior pubic rami, likely due to prior trauma.  There is total left arthroplasty.  There is right femoral head cystic change, likely due to osteoarthritis.  Degenerative changes of the sacroiliac joint, right worse than left is seen.  There is severe compression deformity of T12, likely chronic however age indeterminate in the absence of prior imaging for comparison.  L3 lucency is also indeterminate in etiology.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits.'
);


