INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29423991',
    '10314359-RR-27',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/O CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT abdomen pelvis without contrast  TECHNIQUE: Multidetector CT images of the abdomen were acquired without intravenous contrast. Non-contrast scan has several limitations in detecting vascular and parenchymal organ abnormalities, including tumor detection. No oral contrast was administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Spiral Acquisition 3.7 s, 40.4 cm; CTDIvol = 16.8 mGy (Body) DLP = 679.7 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 680 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Bilateral left worse than right atelectasis is grossly similar in appearance compared to prior.  No pleural effusions are seen.  There is no pericardial effusion.  A central line terminating in the lower SVC is partially visualized.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: Diffuse hypodensity of the liver is likely due to hepatic steatosis.  The liver is enlarged.  There is no evidence of focal lesions within the limitations of an unenhanced scan.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is isodense to the liver, may be due to sludge.  PANCREAS: The pancreas is edematous.  Previously described area of necrosis in the pancreatic neck/ proximal body is better better evaluated on the prior CT with contrast (02:37).  However, an area of relative hypodensity within the pancreatic neck/ proximal body is again appreciated.  There is no evidence of gas within this hypodensity.  There is minimal increase in peripancreatic edema compared to prior.  There is no pancreatic ductal dilatation.  Again seen is moderate volume pancreatic ascites, extending along the left perinephric fascia and down into the bilateral paracolic gutter and extending anteriorly along the mesentery, similar in extent compared to prior exam.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size.  There is no evidence of focal renal lesions within the limitations of an unenhanced scan.  There is no hydronephrosis.  There is no nephrolithiasis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: 2 enteric tubes are seen, 1 terminating in the antrum of the stomach in the other terminating in the third portion of the duodenum with a metal tip, likely a feeding tube.  Visualized small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber and wall thickness.  The visualized colon is unremarkable.  LYMPH NODES: Multiple periportal, peripancreatic, mesenteric and retroperitoneal lymph lymph nodes are mildly enlarged, likely reactive.  VASCULAR: There is no upper abdominal aortic aneurysm.  Mild atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: Anterior abdominal wall defect with surgical material on the skin is consistent with open abdomen.  Diffuse subcutaneous stranding posteriorly is likely related to anasarca.'
);


