VALUES (
    '29423991',
    '10314359-RR-3',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN)',
    'EXAMINATION: LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN):  TECHNIQUE: Grey scale and color Doppler ultrasound images of the abdomen were obtained.  FINDINGS:   LIVER: The liver is diffusely echogenic. The contour of the liver is smooth. There is no focal liver mass. The main portal vein is patent with hepatopetal flow. There is no ascites.  BILE DUCTS: There is no intrahepatic biliary dilation. The CHD measures 4 mm.  GALLBLADDER: There is no evidence of stones or gallbladder wall thickening.  The gallbladder is mildly distended.  There is no wall thickening or pericholecystic fluid.  PANCREAS: The pancreas is not well visualized, largely obscured by overlying bowel gas.  SPLEEN: Normal echogenicity, measuring 13.7 cm.  KIDNEYS: The right kidney measures 11.6 cm.  The left kidney measures 11.6 cm.  Limited views of the kidneys demonstrate normal cortical echogenicity and corticomedullary differentiation without evidence of hydronephrosis.  RETROPERITONEUM: The visualized portions of aorta and IVC are within normal limits.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '29423991',
    '10314359-RR-31',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'EXAMINATION: MRCP:  TECHNIQUE: T1- and T2-weighted multiplanar images of the abdomen were acquired in a 1.5 T magnet. Intravenous contrast: 11 mL Gadavist. Oral contrast:  1 cc of Gadavist mixed with 50 cc of water was administered for oral contrast.  FINDINGS: There is moderate bibasilar atelectasis, also present on the ___ CT (series 4, image 10).  There is no pericardial or pleural effusion.  The heart size is normal.  There is moderate gynecomastia (series 4, image 7, 4).  There is slight signal drop-off of the hepatic parenchyma on T1 weighted out of phase images, compatible with steatosis (series 13, image 17, series 14, image 17).  A 4 mm cyst or biliary hamartoma arises from segment VII (series 4, image 24).  No concerning hepatic mass is detected, though evaluation for small lesions is limited due to use of non breath hold techniques.  There are ill-defined areas of hyper enhancement along the periphery of the right hepatic lobe and segment IV on the first phase (series 16, image 10, 15), which are less distinct on the delayed sequences, and do not correspond to precontrast sequences signal abnormalities, likely related to either heterogeneous steatosis or vascular shunting.  There is no intra or extrahepatic bile duct dilation.  The gallbladder is collapsed, and appears normal.  No ductal stones are detected.  The pancreas is swollen, with moderate peripancreatic fluid, reflecting known history of ____.  Allowing for differences in imaging technique, the overall amount of fluid is little changed in comparison to the ___ and ___ CT examinations.  The pancreatic parenchyma demonstrates abnormally low signal intensity on T1 weighted images.  There is a focal area of nonenhancement at the pancreatic neck (series 17, image 23, series 4, image 41), suggestive of focal necrosis.  There is a moderate amount of adjacent fluid, similar to the examination from ___.  The main pancreatic duct appears intact through this region (series 9, image 1) and appears normal in caliber throughout the course of the pancreas (series 9, image 3, 2).  The peripancreatic fluid appears slightly more organized since the ___ CT, demonstrating a thin wall (series 4, image 38).  Less-organized fluid extends anteriorly towards the umbilicus (series 4, image 42), minimally changed since ___, and slightly increased since ___.  The spleen is mildly enlarged, measuring 15.2 cm (series 3, image 38).  There is no focal splenic mass.  The adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  The kidneys are normal in size and enhance symmetrically, without hydronephrosis.  No focal renal lesion is detected.  The abdominal aorta, celiac trunk, SMA, and renal arteries are patent and normal in caliber.  The intrahepatic portal and hepatic veins appear patent.  No pseudoaneurysm is detected.  There is severe attenuation of the main portal vein at the splenic and SMV confluence (series 17, image 26, 24), without definite thrombosis.  The SMV and splenic veins appear patent.  There is a umbilical hernia with only partial involvement of the transverse colon (series 4, image 53), without obstruction.  There are no osseous lesions concerning for malignancy or infection.'
);


