    'BILAT UP EXT VEINS US PORT',
    'EXAMINATION: BILAT UP EXT VEINS US:  TECHNIQUE: Grey scale and Doppler evaluation was performed on the bilateral upper extremity veins.  FINDINGS: There is normal flow with respiratory variation in the bilateral subclavian veins.  RIGHT: Echogenic material within the right internal jugular vein with a very small amount appearing to extend into the a right external jugular vein is partly compressible but lacks wall-to-wall color flow, consistent with a tiny partial thrombus.  The right subclavian vein is patent and compressible with normal wall-to-wall color flow.  The right axillary vein is patent with normal color flow and compressibility.  The right brachial, basilic, and cephalic veins are patent, compressible, and show normal color flow.  LEFT: The left IJ could not be imaged due to central line and bandaging.  The left subclavian vein is patent and compressible with wall to wall color flow.  The left axillary vein is patent with normal color flow and compressibility.  Imaging of the left upper extremity from the mid upper extremity to the antecubital fossa was not performed secondary to PICC dressing.  However, the left cephalic, brachial, and basilic veins proximal to the dressing and at the antecubital fossa are patent with color flow and compressible.  A PICC is demonstrated within 1 of the left brachial veins and extending into the left subclavian vein.  No fluid collections are identified.  NOTIFICATION: The findings and impression were discussed with ___, M.D. by ___, M.D. on the telephone on ___ at 2:50 ___, 45 minutes after discovery of the findings.'
);


