    'EXAMINATION: CT CHEST W/O CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Multidetector helical scanning of the chest was  reconstructed as contiguous 5- and 1.25-mm thick axial, 2.5-mm thick coronal and parasagittal, and 8 x 8 mm MIPs axial images.  DOSE: DLP: Acquisition sequence:    1) CT Localizer Radiograph    2) CT Localizer Radiograph    3) Spiral Acquisition 6.2 s, 44.4 cm; CTDIvol = 21.4 mGy (Body) DLP = 850.3 mGy-cm.    4) Spiral Acquisition 6.2 s, 44.4 cm; CTDIvol = 21.4 mGy (Body) DLP = 850.3 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 1,701 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: The lungs are fairly well-expanded.  There is focal consolidation along the anterior right upper lobe (4b:421, 423) with peripheral more ground-glass opacity.  Ground-glass opacity is also seen lateral to this (4B: 426).  Similar ground-glass opacities seen in the anterior left upper lobe (4b:49).  Mild ground-glass opacities seen in the medial right middle lobe (4B: 512).  Larger bilateral lower lobe consolidations are associated with pleural effusions, right greater than left.  This is likely compressive atelectasis related to bilateral pleural effusions, likely due to ____.  Other than within the lower lobe consolidations, airways appear grossly patent to the subsegmental level.  Aerosolized secretions are noted in the upper thoracic trachea (4B:395).  There is no pneumothorax.  There is no axillary, supraclavicular, or mediastinal lymphadenopathy by CT size criteria.  The heart is normal in size, with moderate coronary artery calcifications.  The great vessels are normal in caliber.  The chest wall soft tissues are normal.  This exam is not optimized for the evaluation of subdiaphragmatic structures.  Fat stranding surrounding the pancreas is compatible with known acute ____.  The remainder of the visualized upper abdomen is unremarkable.  No focal lytic or sclerotic osseous lesion to suggest neoplasm or infection is identified.  Mild superior endplate concavity of the T8 vertebral body is stable.'
);


