INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26133494',
    '11137177-RR-17',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CTA CHEST W&W/O C&RECONS, NON-CORONARY',
    'TECHNIQUE: Axial multidetector CT images were obtained through the thorax after the uneventful administration of intravenous contrast. Reformatted coronal, sagittal, thin slice axial images, and oblique maximal intensity projection images were submitted to PACS and reviewed.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) CT Localizer Radiograph    2) CT Localizer Radiograph    3) Stationary Acquisition 0.5 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 3.0 mGy (Body) DLP = 1.5 mGy-cm.    4) Stationary Acquisition 0.5 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 3.0 mGy (Body) DLP = 1.5 mGy-cm.    5) Stationary Acquisition 1.5 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 9.1 mGy (Body) DLP = 4.6 mGy-cm.    6) Spiral Acquisition 3.1 s, 24.6 cm; CTDIvol = 14.5 mGy (Body) DLP = 355.7 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 363 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: The aorta and its major branch vessels are patent, with no evidence of stenosis, occlusion, dissection, or aneurysmal formation.  There is no evidence of penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer or aortic arch atheroma present.  The pulmonary arteries are well opacified to the segmental level, with no evidence of filling defect within the main, right, left, lobar, segmental or subsegmental pulmonary arteries.  The main and right pulmonary arteries are normal in caliber, and there is no evidence of right heart strain.  There is no supraclavicular, axillary, mediastinal, or hilar lymphadenopathy.  The thyroid gland appears unremarkable.  There is moderate sized right and small left pleural effusion.  No pericardial effusion.  Bilateral lower lobe segmental atelectasis with near complete collapse of the right lower lobe.  Additional sites of atelectasis is seen in the right middle lobe.  Mild peripheral ground-glass changes and reticulations in the lingula, also likely related to developing atelectasis. The central tracheobronchial tree is patent.  Minimal secretions are appreciated.  Limited images of the upper abdomen demonstrate ascites..  No lytic or blastic osseous lesion suspicious for malignancy is identified.'
);


