INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21310093',
    '11190737-RR-14',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CTA CHEST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT chest  TECHNIQUE: Axial multidetector CT images were obtained through the thorax after the uneventful administration of intravenous contrast. Reformatted coronal, sagittal, thin slice axial images, and oblique maximal intensity projection images were submitted to PACS and reviewed.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Spiral Acquisition 1.9 s, 30.4 cm; CTDIvol = 10.9 mGy (Body) DLP = 331.3 mGy-cm.    2) Stationary Acquisition 1.5 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 5.2 mGy (Body) DLP = 2.6 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 334 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: The aorta and its major branch vessels are patent, with no evidence of stenosis, occlusion, dissection, or aneurysmal formation.  There is no evidence of penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer or aortic arch atheroma present.  The pulmonary arteries are well opacified to the subsegmental level, with no evidence of filling defect within the main, right, left, lobar, segmental or subsegmental pulmonary arteries.  The main and right pulmonary arteries are normal in caliber, and there is no evidence of right heart strain.  There is no supraclavicular, axillary, mediastinal, or hilar lymphadenopathy.  The thyroid gland is incompletely included in the field of view.  There is a 9 mm hypodense nodule in the inferior aspect of the left thyroid lobe.  Visualized right thyroid lobe is unremarkable.  There is no evidence of pericardial effusion.  There is small bilateral pleural effusions.  Trace amount of fluid is noted within the superior pericardial recess.  Assessment of the lung parenchyma slightly degraded by motion artifact. Small amount of high iso-hyperintense material is seen along the dependent aspect of the trachea and in the right main bronchus which extends into the right lower lobe segmental bronchus causing partial occlusion.  Filling defects are also noted within the distal segmental bronchi in the basilar left lower lobe and apical segmental branch of the right upper lobe. There is ground-glass opacity in the apex of the upper lobes bilaterally, more prominent on the right side.  There is complete atelectasis of the right lower lobe and partial atelectasis of the left lower lobe.  Limited images of the upper abdomen demonstrate severe decreased attenuation of the liver parenchyma.  An enteric catheter is seen within the stomach limited but incompletely included in the field of view.  No lytic or blastic osseous lesion suspicious for malignancy is identified.'
);


