VALUES (
    '26137090',
    '11309272-RR-24',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Single phase split bolus contrast: MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis following intravenous contrast administration with split bolus technique. Oral contrast was not administered. Coronal and sagittal reformations were performed and reviewed on PACS.  DOSE: Acquisition sequence:    1) Spiral Acquisition 3.7 s, 58.7 cm; CTDIvol = 20.4 mGy (Body) DLP = 1,193.8 mGy-cm.    2) Stationary Acquisition 3.5 s, 0.5 cm; CTDIvol = 19.5 mGy (Body) DLP = 9.8 mGy-cm.  Total DLP (Body) = 1,204 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LOWER CHEST: Visualized lung fields are within normal limits.  Small nonhemorrhagic bilateral pleural effusions are new from ___ with substantial atelectasis in both lung bases.  There is an incompletely visualized 6 mm nodular opacity along the right major fissure (2:1; 601:26), not definitively seen on the prior studies.  There is no evidence of pericardial effusion.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates heterogeneous attenuation throughout possibly reflective of diffuse hepatic steatosis with areas of focal fatty sparing.  There is no evidence of focal lesions.  There is no evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is within normal limits.  PANCREAS: Extensive stranding around the head, body, and tail of the pancreas is re-demonstrated with areas of hypoenhancement of the pancreatic body suggestive of necrotizing ____ (02:32, 601:32).  There is stable moderate volume free fluid extending along the body and tail of the pancreas through the anterior pararenal space and left paracolic gutter into the pelvis.  There is loculated/organizing fluid posterior to the greater curvature of the stomach without rim enhancement to suggest abscesses.  The posterior wall of the fundus is difficult to delineate and slightly hypoenhancing (02:31, the 601:25, 602:59).  No rim enhancing/organized fluid collection is seen.  There is no evidence of vascular injury or pseudoaneurysm within the limits of this examination which is not optimized for arterial phase.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  A tiny accessory spleen is noted in the splenic hilum.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  There is no evidence of focal renal lesions or hydronephrosis.  There is no perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: Enteric tube terminates near the ligament of Treitz.  The stomach is unremarkable.  Small bowel loops demonstrate normal caliber, wall thickness, and enhancement throughout.  The colon and rectum are within normal limits.  The appendix is normal.  Residual oral contrast reaches the rectum from prior examination.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder contains a Foley catheter and air consistent with recent Foley placement.  Distal ureters are unremarkable.  There is small to moderate low-density free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The visualized reproductive organs are unremarkable.  LYMPH NODES: There is no retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  VASCULAR: There is no abdominal aortic aneurysm.  No atherosclerotic disease is noted.  BONES: There is no evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: Anasarca has worsened.'
);


