INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26903059',
    '11981239-RR-17',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CTA CHEST',
    'EXAMINATION: CTA TORSO:  TECHNIQUE: Torso CTA: Non-contrast and multiphasic post-contrast images were acquired through the chest, abdomen and pelvis.Oral contrast was not administered. MIP reconstructions were performed on independent workstation and reviewed on  PACS.:   DOSE: Total DLP (Body) = 1,135 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   VASCULAR: Motion artifact limits evaluation of the aortic root.  However, no definite evidence of aortic dissection.  The aorta is normal in caliber.  Coronary artery calcifications are mild to moderate on this non-dedicated exam.  Bilateral renal arteries are patent.  The main portal vein appears patent.  Atherosclerotic calcifications in the abdominal aorta and iliac arteries are mild.  The main, left and right pulmonary arteries are normal in caliber.  This exam is not tailored for imaging of pulmonary embolism but no obvious large filling defect in the central pulmonary arteries to indicate the presence of a large acute central pulmonary embolus.  An incidental accessory left hepatic artery arising from the left gastric artery is a normal variant (series 2, image 102).  CHEST: Pericardial calcifications noted, likely the sequelae of prior pericarditis.  No pericardial effusion.  Calcified mediastinal lymph nodes are consistent with prior granulomatous disease exposure.  A left paratracheal station lymph node is mildly enlarged measuring 14 mm in short axis, likely reactive in the setting of atypical left upper lobe infection (series 2, image 44).  Other mediastinal lymph nodes are prominent but not enlarged by size criteria in the right lower paratracheal and subcarinal stations.  No hilar or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy.  Scattered peribronchovascular nodules and tree and ___ opacities suggest atypical infection in the lingula and remaining left upper lobe (e.g., series 2, image 51, 61).  Several areas of focal mucous plugging are also seen in the lingula and left upper lobe (series 2, image 69).  Peribronchiolar wall thickening is very mild and could suggest also additional component of bronchiolitis.  Bibasilar atelectasis is mild-to-moderate.  The trachea is patent without aerosolized secretions.  Bilateral tiny calcified granulomas is consistent with prior granulomatous disease exposure (e.g.  Series 2, image 67).  Background paraseptal emphysema is moderate and most pronounced in the apices.  No pneumothorax or pleural effusion.  No pleural calcifications.  ABDOMEN:   HEPATOBILIARY: The liver demonstrates homogenous attenuation throughout.  No evidence of focal lesions.  No evidence of intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder is within normal limits, without stones or gallbladder wall thickening.  PANCREAS: Peripancreatic fat stranding is moderate to severe most pronounced in the pancreatic head, uncinate process, and body.  The pancreas is normal in size without evidence of parenchymal calcifications.  The main pancreatic duct is not dilated.  A subtle area of relative hypoattenuation in the pancreatic body (e.g., series 2, image 123, 121) is favored to be edema related to ____.  No definite focal area of necrosis or drainable fluid collection within the pancreas or surrounding the pancreas.  Fat stranding and free fluid surrounding the second portion the duodenum is likely secondary to adjacent ____.  Fluid extends into the retroperitoneum to the left of the aorta (e.g.  Series 2, image 159) and mostly along the right pericolic gutters and anterior to the right anterior pararenal fascia (series 2, image 140-162).  Adjacent peripancreatic vessels are patent.  A few peripancreatic lymph nodes are measurable up to 8 mm in short axis and nonspecific, likely reactive.  SPLEEN: The spleen shows normal size and attenuation throughout, without evidence of focal lesions.  ADRENALS: The right and left adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  URINARY: The kidneys are of normal and symmetric size with normal nephrogram.  No evidence of stones, focal renal lesions, or hydronephrosis.  No perinephric abnormality.  GASTROINTESTINAL: The stomach is underdistended, limiting evaluation.  Other than fat stranding surrounding the duodenum, related to the adjacent ____, small bowel loops are unremarkable.  No bowel obstruction.  Bowel wall enhancement is normal.  The colon and rectum are within normal limits.  The appendix has normal caliber without evidence of fat stranding.  No free air.  RETROPERITONEUM: No evidence of retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy by size criteria, although a few peripancreatic lymph nodes are measurable as above.  PELVIS: The urinary bladder and distal ureters are unremarkable.  No evidence of pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  No free fluid in the pelvis.  REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS: The prostate gland is normal in size.  BONES: No evidence of worrisome osseous lesions or acute fracture.  SOFT TISSUES: The abdominal and pelvic wall is within normal limits other than coarse calcification in the subcutaneous fat of the region likely sequelae prior trauma or subcutaneous injection (series 2, image 128).  NOTIFICATION: The findings and impression were discussed with ___, M.D. by ___, M.D. on the telephone on ___ at 10:49 am, 1 minutes after discovery of the findings.'
);


