INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26078032',
    '11982995-RR-26',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP W/O CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: MRI of the Abdomen  TECHNIQUE: T1- and T2-weighted multiplanar images of the abdomen were acquired in a 1.5 T magnet. No contrast was administered.  FINDINGS: Markedly limited examination without intravenous contrast and non breath hold.  Lower Thorax: Small left and right pleural effusions are unchanged.  Heart is mildly enlarged.  Liver: The liver is unchanged in morphology.  There is a T2 hyperintense lesion in segment III measuring 2.2 cm, previously characterized as a hemangioma in ___.  There is small to moderate volume ascites.  Biliary: The gallbladder is mildly distended.  There is no cholelithiasis.  There is no wall thickening or edema.  Pancreas: Primary pancreatic lesion in the neck is grossly unchanged measuring approximately 1.5 cm (series 4, image 28).  The upstream pancreas atrophic with mild ductal dilation.  Again seen are multifocal lobulated and interconnected peripancreatic fluid collections extending from the lesser sac inferiorly to the left anterior mesentery and superiorly and adjacent to the greater curvature of the stomach.  Largest component of these collections measure 5.4 x 6.6 x 5.0 cm along the inferior stomach and lesser curvature and 3.9 x 5.8 x 5.9 cm in the mid mesentery.  Anterior to the focal lesion in the pancreatic neck is peripancreatic fluid/edema which demonstrates mild complexity when compared to the other collections.  There is no intrinsic T1 hyperintense signal in this location to suggest hemorrhage (series 6, image 25).  Given its location and complexity, this may have represented the site of the pancreatic ductal leak.  Please note that the upstream pancreatic duct in this location is mildly dilated, which suggests that there is likely not an ongoing leak, as if therefore the duct would likely be decompressed.  Overall size of these collections are grossly unchanged compared to recent non-contrast CT.  Evaluation for underlying pancreatic necrosis is limited without intravenous contrast, and although no necrosis is seen, a small area of necrosis would be difficult to exclude.  Spleen: Spleen is normal in size and signal intensity.  Adrenal Glands: The right and left adrenal glands are unremarkable.  Kidneys: The right kidney is surgically absent.  There are few scattered cortical cysts noted in the left kidney with the largest measuring 2.3 cm.  Note is made of a few scattered peripelvic left renal cysts.  There is no hydronephrosis.  Gastrointestinal Tract:  There is a small hiatal hernia.  Mucosal gastric edema is likely reactive.  There is no obstruction.  Lymph Nodes: No enlarged lymphadenopathy is seen.  Omental stranding/fluid is again demonstrated.  Vasculature: Flow voids are grossly preserved.  Osseous and Soft Tissue Structures: There is mild diffuse anasarca.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26078032',
    '11982995-RR-27',
    'MRCP',
    'Abdomen',
    'MRCP (MR ABD W&W/OC)',
    'EXAMINATION: MRCP:  TECHNIQUE: T1- and T2-weighted multiplanar images of the abdomen were acquired in a 1.5 T magnet. Intravenous contrast: 10 mL Gadavist. Oral contrast:  1 cc of Gadavist mixed with 50 cc of water was administered for oral contrast.  FINDINGS: Lower Thorax: Bilateral layering medium-sized pleural effusions are similar to prior.  There is bibasilar subsegmental atelectasis.  Liver: 2-3 mm T2 hyperintense focus in the right hepatic lobe likely represents a small biliary hamartoma (03:33).  No hepatic steatosis.  26 mm T2 hyperintense focus in segment III is unchanged in size when measured in a similar fashion, against demonstrating progressive primarily peripheral nodular enhancement, compatible with hepatic hemangioma.  Otherwise, the liver demonstrates normal homogeneous signal intensity without concerning focal lesion.  The hepatic veins are patent.  Portal vein is patent, however there is narrowing of the inferior aspect of main portal vein near the portal-splenic confluence the region of the mass (for example see series 1203, image 79).  The confluence remains patent.  Patent SMV.  Patent splenic vein.  Biliary: No intra- or extrahepatic biliary ductal dilation.  The gallbladder is distended but otherwise unremarkable.  Pancreas: Centered in the neck of the pancreas is a centrally hypoenhancing 2.0 x 1.7 cm mass consistent with biopsy-proven adenosquamous carcinoma.  There is upstream main pancreatic dilation and parenchymal atrophy, similar to prior.  Additionally, the distal atrophic pancreatic parenchyma is slightly edematous, with peripancreatic stranding.  The pancreatic parenchyma proximal to the mass is within normal limits, demonstrating normal signal and normal caliber duct.  Pancreas divisum is again noted.  There are multiple interconnected peripancreatic fluid collections again noted, significantly larger since prior study of ___.  For instance, along the greater traversed true near the gastric fundus, a large fluid component currently measuring 7.4 by 4.6 cm (04:16) was previously 5.7 x 2.8 cm when measured similarly on the prior study.  This collection is contiguous anteriorly with an ovoid fluid collection draped along the greater curvature of the stomach (see series 4, image 18).  Inferior to the gastric body, a bilobed collection measuring up to 8.3 x 7.4 cm previously measured 5.7 x 5.7 cm when measured similarly on ___ (04:22).  This collection is contiguous with an additional inferiorly projecting collection in the midline which measures up 5.3 x 5.4 cm (04:30), previously 5.5 x 2.4 cm when measured similarly.  Elsewhere in the peripancreatic region there are adjacent smaller but similar appearing likely interconnected fluid collections which are uniformly larger since prior.  No definite nonenhancing debris within the collections.  No evidence of pancreatic/peripancreatic hemorrhage.  Spleen: There is no splenomegaly or focal splenic lesion.  Adrenal Glands: The adrenal glands are normal in size and configuration.  Kidneys: The right kidney is surgically absent.  The left kidney again demonstrates multiple cortical and parapelvic cysts without suspicious features measuring up to 2.5 cm.  No left hydronephrosis or proximal left hydroureter.  Gastrointestinal Tract:  The distal esophagus is within normal limits.  There is wall edema throughout the stomach primarily posteriorly and inferiorly, likely reactive due to peripancreatic inflammation (for example see series 4, image 20 and series 3, image 11).  Stomach is otherwise within normal limits.  The duodenum is normal.  Slight wall thickening/edema of proximal small bowel loops in the left hemiabdomen is likely reactive to peripancreatic inflammation (for example see series 3, image 25).  Otherwise, small bowel loops are normal in caliber and unremarkable.  Similarly, there is likely reactive wall edema of the mid and distal transverse colon (see series 3, image 10), extending to the distal descending colon (03:31).  There is overall small volume ascites seen throughout the abdomen as well as diffuse scattered small pockets of mesenteric fluid.  Lymph Nodes: There is no evidence of retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  Vasculature: The abdominal aorta is normal in caliber without evidence of aneurysm or dilation.  Major proximal tributaries are patent. The common hepatic artery is replaced to the SMA.  The splenic artery is normal.  The IVC is normal in caliber and patent.  The hepatic veins are patent.  Patent portal vein, SMV, and splenic vein.  Osseous and Soft Tissue Structures: There is near circumferential subcutaneous body wall edema worst in the dependent flanks.  No suspicious foci of abnormal marrow signal are seen.'
);


