INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24600926',
    '10022584-RR-118',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/O CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multiple MDCT axial images were obtained from the base of the lungs through the proximal thighs without intravenous or enteric contrast. Sagittal and coronal reformations were derived.  FINDINGS: Examination is limited without intravenous and enteric contrast, but within these limitations in the visualized thorax, there is basilar atelectasis but no pleural effusion or pneumothorax.  Within the abdomen, evaluation of the solid organs is limited without intravenous contrast.  The liver, gallbladder, spleen, and abdominal loops of bowel are normal.  There is a exophytic left renal cyst incompletely characterized by this study. Incidental note is made of a splenule.  Pancreas appears atrophic.  There is no abdominal free air or free fluid and no pathologic abdominal lymphadenopathy, although shotty mesenteric nodes are demonstrated.  There is a moderate-sized hiatal hernia.  There are extensive vascular calcifications.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITHOUT INTRAVENOUS CONTRAST: At the distal descending colon at the sigmoidal junction there is mild pericolonic fat stranding and probable wall thickening.  There is no free air or free fluid. The bladder is not fully distended, and the prominent bladder wall is likely secondary to this. Seminal vesicles are normal.  There are extensive vascular calcifications, and a penile prosthesis is demonstrated. There is no pathologic lymphadenopathy.  MUSCULOSKELETAL: There is no suspicious lytic or blastic lesion.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '28694648',
    '10042037-RR-15',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'CT SCAN OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS PERFORMED ON ___.:   TECHNIQUE: Multidetector CT through the abdomen and pelvis was performed following the administration of both oral and IV contrast material.  Axial, coronal and sagittal reformations were provided.  FINDINGS: The imaged lung bases are clear.  Please note the superiormost aspect of the liver dome is excluded.  The imaged portion of the heart is normal.  ABDOMEN: The imaged portion of the liver appears grossly unremarkable, though note is made of a hyperenhancing lesion within segment VI on series 2, image 19, which measures approximately 12 mm, likely a hemangioma.  No additional lesions are seen within the liver.  The gallbladder appears normal.  The spleen, adrenal glands, and the pancreas appear normal.  There is no portal venous gas.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast promptly.  The abdominal aorta is normal in course and caliber.  The stomach and duodenum appear normal.  PELVIS: There is extensive fat stranding extending from the sigmoid mesocolon superiorly with areas of extraluminal gas.  In addition, there is thickening along the posterior peritoneal margin with adjacent small bowel thickening seen best on series 300B, image 18, likely reactive.  Overall, findings are most likely secondary to perforated ____.  There is no extraluminal fluid collection.  There is mild thickening along the sigmoid colon seen best on series 2, image 67.  The appendix is normal.  No evidence of bowel obstruction.  There is no IMV thrombus or IMV gas seen.  No free fluid extending into the pelvis.  Urinary bladder appears normal.  Prostate gland appears within normal limits.  BONES: No worrisome osseous lesions are seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20410636',
    '10074010-RR-15',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'CT ABDOMEN: Minimal bibasilar atelectasis and bilateral pleural effusions are noted.  Heart size is normal.  There is no pericardial effusion.  There is a small amount of perihepatic free fluid and moderate air surrounding the liver, most prominent near the falciform ligament and gall bladder fossa. The liver, spleen, pancreas, adrenals, intra- abdominal loops of large and small bowel are unremarkable.  There is moderate gallbladder distention noted. There is no evidence of pericholecystic fluid or gallstones to suggest cholecystitis. Intrabdominal large and small bowel are unremarkable without evidence of obstruction. There are multiple foci of air scattered throughout the abdomen.  There is no mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  CT PELVIS: The sigmoid colon demonstrates marked thickening with tiny foci of extraluminal air surrounding the mid sigmoid colon.  There is a free fluid collection measuring approximately 4.8 x 2.1 cm axially just posterior to the bladder extending from the sigmoid colon to the level of the cecum. There is diffuse inflammation in the sigmoid mesocolon.  The bladder, mildly enlarged prostate, and rectum are grossly unremarkable.  Bone windows demonstrate no suspicious lytic or blastic lesions.  There are moderate degenerative changes noted throughout the lumbar spine and hips.'
);


