)
VALUES (
    '28627153',
    '10207476-RR-85',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/O CONTRAST',
    'CT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS WITHOUT IV CONTRAST:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITHOUT IV CONTRAST: The previously noted right small pleural effusion has resolved.  There is a persistent small left pleural effusion which is not significantly changed.  There is minimal atelectasis in the left lower lobe.  The liver is without focal lesions.  A stone is identified in the gallbladder.  The spleen, pancreas, adrenal glands, and left kidney are unremarkable.  In the right kidney, there is a 1.1 cm hypodense lesion that is too small to characterize.  It is identified on prior examination from ___ and is stable in size and likely represents a cyst. There is no retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  The small bowel is normal. Extensive diverticula are seen throughout the colon.  There is minimal focal dilatation of the aorta to 2.5 cm in the infrarenal portion.  This is unchanged.  A colostomy is noted in the left lower quadrant.  TECHNIQUE: Multidetector scanning is performed from the diaphragm through the symphysis without intravenous contrast.  Comparison is made to prior examination of ___.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITHOUT IV CONTRAST: There is a small residual collection in the left lower quadrant.  The catheter is positioned well within the collection.  The collection is decreased in size, largest extends previously 5.7 x 3.4 cm (currently 4.7 x 2.7 cm).  The collection appears to extend towards the suture of the ___ pouch, however, the full extent is difficult to evaluate due to the lack of IV contrast.  There is no pelvic lymphadenopathy.  A small collection in the right lower quadrant is decreased in size, currently measuring 2.8 x 2.1 cm (previously 3.5 x 2.2 cm).  On bone windows, there are no concerning osteolytic or osteosclerotic lesions.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26812989',
    '10276303-RR-10',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT helical acquisition was performed from the diaphragm to the pubic symphysis following the uneventful administration of IV contrast. Multiplanar reformations were provided.  CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: In the right upper quadrant, the proximal transverse colon at the hepatic flexure demonstrates wall thickening and stranding, with dense material in a posteriorly situated diverticulum demonstrating high density material which may be inspissated debris or previously administered barium (2:45-46, 300B:37). There is adjacent inflammatory stranding which tracks inferiorly along Gerota''s fascia in the anterior pararenal space. There is no discrete fluid collection or abscess. Tiny adjacent locules of dependent air are seen, with no evidence of macroperforation or free air throughout the abdomen.  The gallbladder appears normal, and there is no pneumobilia.  The liver, spleen, pancreas, adrenal glands, kidneys, ureters, large bowel, and small bowel also appear normal. There is no focal fluid collection in the abdomen. Likely related to recent C-section, there is an approximately 3.0 x 2.2 x 2.2 cm fluid collection in a subcutaneous location in the left paramedian lower abdominal wall without enhancing rim (2:83). Additional small anterior nodules probably relate to sites of known Lovenox injections.  CT PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The rectum, sigmoid colon, and uterus as well as ovaries/adnexa appear unremarkable.  There is also lack of diverticulosis in the sigmoid colon.  Osseous structures appear unremarkable.'
);


