VALUES (
    '28721403',
    '10625923-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABD AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST:  TECHNIQUE: Contiguous axial multidetector CT images through the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous contrast.  Oral contrast was not administered. Multiplanar reformations were generated and reviewed.  DLP: 900.75 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS:   LUNG BASES: A tiny right pleural effusion with associated right lower lobe compressive atelectasis is noted. The imaged portion of the heart is unremarkable.  CT ABDOMEN: The liver enhances normally without focal lesion of concern. The main portal vein is patent.  No intrahepatic or extrahepatic biliary ductal dilation is seen. The gallbladder is normal. The pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands appear normal.  The kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast promptly. The abdominal aorta is normal in course and caliber with widely patent major branches and only minimal atherosclerotic calcifications noted.  No retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy or hematoma is seen. The stomach and duodenum appear normal.  CT PELVIS: Loops of small bowel demonstrate no signs of ileus or obstruction. Within the deep pelvis in the cul-de-sac, there is a fluid collection measuring approximately 7.2 x 3.3 x 3.0 cm there is adjacent peritoneal thickening and enhancement and no contained free air. There is adjacent decompressed colon which may be minimally thickened. Overall appearance of raises concern for colonic perforation possibly in the setting of acute ____ though no diverticula are clearly seen. The appendix is visualized in the right lower quadrant with an adjacent small peripherally enhancing fluid collection as seen on series 6018 image 28 measuring 1.5 x 3.0 x 1.7 cm. The adjacent appendix is seen on series 601 a image 30 appearing slightly hyperemic though intact. This small fluid collection may communicate with the larger collection in the deep pelvis. No evidence of thrombophlebitis. Mild presacral fluid noted.  The bladder is only minimally distended, and not well assessed. The imaged pelvic organs are within normal limits. There is no evidence of pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy by CT size criteria.  BONE WINDOW: There is mild degenerative change of the imaged thoracolumbar spine.  Alignment is normal.  There are no concerning osteolytic or osteosclerotic lesions identified.'
);


