INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26779520',
    '10895299-RR-52',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'CT SCAN OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS PERFORMED ON ___.: Comparison is made with a prior study from ___.  TECHNIQUE: MDCT was used to obtain contiguous axial images through the abdomen and pelvis following the administration of 130 ml Optiray IV contrast. No oral contrast was administered for this exam.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were provided.  FINDINGS:   LUNG BASES: There is new subsegmental atelectasis at both lung bases.  No pleural or pericardial effusion is seen.  ABDOMEN: There is free intraperitoneal air noted along the anterior edge of the liver which is new from prior study.  Air is also seen tracking along the periportal region as well as posterior to the liver.  Patient is status post cholecystectomy.  There is a stable-appearing small hypodensity in hepatic segment VIII seen on series 2, image 17.  Mild intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation is stable and likely related to prior cholecystectomy.  The pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands appear normal.  Kidneys enhance symmetrically and excrete contrast promptly.  Abdominal aorta and major branch vessels are widely patent.  There is no retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.  The stomach and duodenum appear normal.  PELVIS: Residual dense barium is noted within the large bowel, which is the residual from the contrast administered during yesterday''s CT exam. There are abnormally thickened small bowel loops in the right hemipelvis with surrounding fat stranding, significantly worse compared with yesterday''s exam. Extraluminal gas is noted at this level which is concerning for perforation at this level.  There is loss of fat plane between these inflamed loops of bowel and the urinary bladder with thickening along the bladder wall. This appearance is concerning for impending development of enterovesical fistula. Adjacent loops of large bowel appear thickened, likely reactive.  No dilated loops of small bowel are seen. There is no drainable fluid collection.  The right ovary cannot be separated from the inflamed bowel in the right hemipelvis. The left ovary appears grossly unremarkable.  Patient is status post hysterectomy.  BONE WINDOWS: No suspicious lytic or blastic osseous lesion is seen.'
);


