INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21510024',
    '11052273-RR-12',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'CT SCAN OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS.: Comparison is made with a prior study from ___.  TECHNIQUE: MDCT was used to obtain contiguous axial images through the abdomen and pelvis following the uneventful administration of 130 cc Optiray IV contrast.  Oral contrast was also administered.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were provided.  FINDINGS:   LUNG BASES: The visualized lung bases are clear.  ABDOMEN: The liver contains no focal lesion.  There is no biliary ductal dilatation.  The gallbladder, pancreas, and spleen have a normal appearance. The adrenal glands appear stably thickened bilaterally without focal nodule. A lobulated contour of the kidneys is again noted with several hypodense lesions likely reflecting cysts.  There is cortical atrophy along the upper pole of the left kidney which may reflect scarring related to prior infection or infarction.  Atherosclerotic calcification along the abdominal aorta is noted. No lymphadenopathy, free air or free fluid is seen in the abdomen.  The stomach and small bowel follow a normal course.  PELVIS: Loops of small bowel demonstrate no evidence of ileus or obstruction. A normal appendix is noted in the right lower quadrant.  Colonic diverticulosis is noted.  There is a diverticulum along the distal transverse colon seen on series 2, image 19, which is slightly thickened with mild adjacent fat stranding which may reflect an early ____.  In addition, there is a segment of thickened bowel along the descending colon with adjacent fat stranding and fluid tracking along the paracolic gutter. There is no definite evidence of free air or extraluminal fluid collection. These findings are compatible with acute uncomplicated ____.  The uterus and adnexa appear unchanged with lobulated contour which may be secondary to degenerated fibroids or adnexal cystic structures. The urinary bladder appears grossly unremarkable with distal ureters appearing normally opacified.  BONE WINDOWS: No suspicious lytic or blastic osseous lesion is seen.  Spinal alignment appears preserved.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24972913',
    '11130436-RR-18',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images were obtained from the lung bases to pubic symphysis after the uneventful administration of 130 cc of Omnipaque contrast medium and oral contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were prepared.  FINDINGS: Lung bases demonstrate minimal dependent atelectasis without pleural or pericardial effusion.  The liver is normal in attenuation without focal lesion, intra- or extra-hepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  The portal and hepatic veins appear patent.  The gallbladder is decompressed.  The pancreas, spleen, adjacent splenule, and bilateral adrenal glands are unremarkable.  The kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically without hydronephrosis.  The stomach and small bowel is normal.  At the base of the cecum, at or slightly distal to the level of the ileocecal valve anteriorly, is asymmetric mural thickening and stranding within the adjacent fat.  A rounded hyperenhancing focus is noted, which may reflect the wall of the diverticulum.  The appendix is seen and normal (2:75).  The remainder of the colon is unremarkable.  There is no free air or free fluid in the abdomen. Right lower quadrant mesenteric nodes are slightly prominent and likely reactive in etiology.  No retroperitoneal adenopathy is seen.  The aorta and major branches appear patent and normal in caliber.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: The bladder, prostate, and rectum are unremarkable.  There is no pelvic adenopathy or free fluid.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: There is no lytic or sclerotic bony lesion concerning for osseous malignant process.'
);


