INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '21177686',
    '11324378-RR-60',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images were obtained through the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous and oral contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformations were evaluated.  No priors are available.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH INTRAVENOUS AND ORAL CONTRAST: The visualized lung bases display dependent atelectasis, but no pleural or pericardial effusion. The cardiac silhouette appears enlarged with dilatation of the left atrium.  The liver displays a 4-mm hypoattenuating lesion within the high right hepatic lobe, but is otherwise normal, as is the gallbladder, spleen, stomach with small hiatal hernia, small bowel, adrenal glands, and intra-abdominal aorta. The kidneys display multiple hypoattenuating lesions, the largest extending off the upper pole on the left measuring 3.3 x 3.2 cm, which is clearly a simple cyst and the largest off the lower pole on the right measuring 11 x 8 mm.  Additionally, there a well definied hypoattenuated focus involving the interpolar portion of the right kidney.  Incidentally noted is duplication of the right-sided collecting system, which appears to converge slightly distal to the UPJ.  No free air, free fluid, or pathologically enlarged lymph nodes are present. Incidentally detected is a replaced right hepatic artery off the  SMA.:   CT OF THE PELVIS WITH INTRAVENOUS AND ORAL CONTRAST: Intrapelvic bowel displays marked eccentric wall thickening involving the mid portion of the sigmoid colon with associated inflammatory changes in the adjacent mesenteric fat.  Additionally, there is a well-defined 15 x 20 mm hypoattenuating lesion, which appears intramural.  No signs of microperforation or extraluminal abscess are identified and the remaining bowel displays evidence of mild diverticulosis, but appears otherwise unremarkable.  The urinary bladder and uterus/adnexa appear normal.  No free fluid or pathologically enlarged lymph nodes are present.  BONE WINDOWS: No malignant-appearing osseous lesions are identified.  Mild degenerative changes are noted within the posterior facets of the lower lumbar spine.  Disk space narrwoing noted at L2-L3.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25216936',
    '11325242-RR-10',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'STUDY: CT of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast; 130 cc of Omnipaque intravenous contrast was administered without adverse reaction or complication.  Coronal and sagittal reformatted images were also generated.  FINDINGS:   ABDOMEN: The visualized lung bases are clear.    Small hypodensity seen at the liver dome too small to characterize.  The liver is otherwise unremarkable, there is no intrahepatic biliary dilatation.  The gallbladder shows a single calcified stone that measures 12 mm in diameter, but there is no wall edema or pericholecystic fluid.  The spleen is normal in size.  The pancreas shows no lesions or peripancreatic fluid collection.  The adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance with and excrete contrast symmetrically without evidence of hydronephrosis.  The small and large bowel show no evidence of obstruction.  There is no lymphadenopathy, or free fluid in the abdomen.  Small locules of free air are seen tracking along the anterior abdominal cavity as well as into the retroperitoneum.  PELVIS: The bladder, prostate, and rectum appear unremarkable.  Diverticulosis is present along the sigmoid colon with marked wall edema and surrounding inflammatory fat stranding.  A loculated area of hypodensity with peripheral enhancement measuring approximately 1.9 x 2.7 (2:65 and 601b:34) suggests intramural abscess.  Additionally, multiple adjacent locules of extraluminal gas are present, the largest of which measures 2 x 2.5 cm (2:70). Foci of free intraperitoneal air seen scattered throughout the abdomen and below the diaphragm.  Adjacent to this inflammation in the lower left pelvis is a 20 x 14-mm collection of fluid that does not show any thick wall or rim enhancement, but could represent a developing abscess.  Left inguinal fat containing hernia is seen.  BONES: There are no aggressive-appearing lytic or sclerotic lesions.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24619708',
    '11337676-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Contiguous MDCT images through the abdomen and pelvis were performed after the administration of intravenous contrast.  Axial, coronal, and sagittal reformats were acquired.  FINDINGS:   CT OF THE ABDOMEN: Lung bases are clear.  No focal hepatic lesions.  Two gallstones are seen, but no evidence of acute cholecystitis.  The pancreas, spleen, and bilateral adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys are homogeneously enhancing and excreting urine without evidence of hydronephrosis, stones or masses.  No retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  There is no free air and no free fluid.  The portal venous, systemic venous and systemic arterial system of the abdomen and pelvis are normal.  Diverticulosis in the descending and sigmoid colons is seen with focal thickening of the sigmoid colon with adjacent inflammatory changes consistent with ____(series 2, image 66 through 72).  There is early intramural abscess formation (measuring about 2.5 x 2 cm)(series 2, image 67). No free air is seen.  CT OF THE PELVIS: The urinary bladder and uterus are normal.  There is a 1.9 cm likely functional cyst in the left ovary. Trace pelvic free fluid is noted.  There is no lymphadenopathy.  BONES: There are moderate degenerative changes at L5/S1 with intervertebral disc disease.  There are no suspicious lytic or sclerotic bony lesions.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '27923482',
    '11404878-RR-12',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast.  Multiplanar reformations were obtained and reviewed.  FINDINGS: The partially imaged lungs show a 3 mm triangular nodule in the left lower lobe (2:8), unchanged from the CT from ___.  The partially imaged heart is unremarkable as well.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: The liver, spleen, both adrenals, both kidneys, pancreas and gallbladder are unremarkable.  Note is made of a duodenal diverticulum.  There is a small hypodensity in the lower pole of the left kidney that is too small to characterize.  The small bowel loops are unremarkable.  The colon shows sigmoid ____ with contained microperforation containing air and significant fat stranding in the left lower quadrant.  No abdominal free fluid or free air is present.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: The rectum, bladder, prostate, both seminal vesicles and appendix are normal.  No pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  Small amount of pelvic free fluid is present.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: The visible osseous structures show a bone island in the left iliac crest.  Also noted is moderate rotatory scoliosis of the lumbar spine with degenerative endplate changes, but no suspicious lytic or blastic lesions or fractures are present.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '26292867',
    '11427738-RR-42',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT-acquired axial images were obtained through the abdomen and pelvis after administration of 130 cc IV Optiray contrast and oral contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformats were displayed.  CT ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: There is dependent subsegmental atelectasis at the lung bases.  There is a 4-mm hypodensity in the right lobe of the liver which is too small to further characterize, but likely a cyst (2:14).  The gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, and bilateral adrenal glands are normal.  The kidneys enhance and excrete contrast symmetrically without evidence of hydronephrosis or hydroureter.  The opacified stomach and intra-abdominal loops of small and large bowel are normal.  There is no free air or fluid in the abdomen.  No mesenteric or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy meeting criteria for pathologic enlargement is noted.  CT PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: At the junction of the descending and sigmoid colon, there is wall thickening and free fluid.  There is a tiny focus of gas, which may be intramural, but could represent a contained rupture.  There is free fluid adjacent to the inflammed segment of colon.  There is a small amount of free fluid within the dependent portion of the pelvis.  The urinary bladder, distal ureters, uterus, adnexa, and rectum are unremarkable.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy meeting criteria for pathologic enlargement.  BONE WINDOWS: No suspicious lytic or sclerotic osseous lesion is identified.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '25194934',
    '11441483-RR-29',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT acquired axial images were obtained from the lung bases through the pubic symphysis after administration of 130 cc Omnipaque intravenous contrast material, as well as enteric contrast.  Coronal and sagittal reformats prepared and reviewed.  FINDINGS: The lower chest is unremarkable.  ABDOMEN: The liver enhances homogeneously and is without focal abnormality.  The gallbladder and biliary tree appear normal.  The pancreas, spleen, and adrenal glands appear normal.  The kidneys enhance normally and excrete contrast symmetrically.  There are two small hypodensities in the left kidney which are too small to characterize.  The stomach, duodenum, and abdominal loops of small and large bowel are of normal caliber, without wall thickening, or associated mass.  The appendix is normal.  There is no bowel obstruction.  There is no ascites, fluid collection, or pneumoperitoneum.   The portal, splenic, and mesenteric veins are patent.  The abdominal aorta is not enlarged and its main branches are patent.  There is no retroperitoneal, periportal, or mesenteric lymphadenopathy.  PELVIS: There is marked sigmoid colonic wall thickening, mucosal hyperemia, and extensive fat stranding.  This is on a background of extensive diverticulosis.  There is an 18 x 12 mm pocket of fluid with small foci of air which do not appear to be endoluminal, and may represent early abscess formation (2:66, 601:39).  The bladder is normal.  Fibroid uterus.  There is a 3.0 x 2.4 cm simple cyst in the left adnexa (2:64).  There is no pelvic free fluid or mass.  There is no pelvic or inguinal lymphadenopathy.  MUSCULOSKELETAL: There are no lytic or sclerotic osseous lesions concerning for malignancy.'
);


