INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20326816',
    '11737161-RR-16',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'RENAL U.S.',
    'TECHNIQUE: Sonographic images were obtained of the bilateral kidneys.  FINDINGS: The right kidney is normal in appearance without hydronephrosis, masses, or stones and measures 10 cm.  Note is made of prominent columns of Bertin.  The left kidney is also normal in appearance measuring 10.8 cm in the craniocaudal dimension, without masses, stones, or hydronephrosis.  A 1-cm anechoic cyst is seen in the left upper pole.  The bladder demonstrates some irregular thickening at the inferior aspect, possibly from treatment change or layering debris.  Limited images of the gallbladder demonstrate no wall thickening or pericholecystic fluid.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20326816',
    '11737161-RR-17',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'CT OF THE ABDOMEN AND PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST:   TECHNIQUE: Multidetector scanning is performed from the diaphragm through the symphysis during dynamic injection of 130 cc of Optiray.  No prior studies are available for comparison.  DLP: 903.6 mGy-cm.  CT OF THE ABDOMEN WITH IV CONTRAST: The lung bases are clear.  In segment VII of the liver there is a 1.5-cm lesion which is hypodense and measures 58 Hounsfield units.  Emanating from the left portal vein and extending towards the liver capsule there is a tubular hypodense area which may represent a thrombosed portal vein branch.  There is some triangularly shaped area of increased perfusion adjacent to it.  The gallbladder contains a high-density material possibly vicarious excretion of contrast from prior CT examination.  The spleen is normal in size.  The pancreas is unremarkable.  The duodenal diverticulum is noted.  The adrenal glands are normal.  The right kidney is unremarkable.  In the left kidney there is a mild hydronephrosis and there is lack of excretion of the left kidney.  Only the cortex is enhancing.  No contrast is seen in the collecting system as it is seen on the right kidney.  In the upper pole of the left kidney there is a 0.9 cm hypodense lesion that is too small to characterize.  The left ureter is mildly distended and can be followed into the pelvis.  There is some fat stranding surrounding it.  CT OF THE PELVIS WITH IV CONTRAST: There is massive wall thickening in the sigmoid colon.  This is relatively hypodense.  In the sigmoid mesentery there is a small collection that measures 2.5 x 2.3 cm and contains air inclusions.  It has an enhancing rim and sits superior to the sigmoid colon.  A soft tissue extending from the sigmoid colon to the bladder with enhancing fistula tract.  Massive wall thickening of the bladder overlying the fistula tract.  This measures about 4.0 cm in width and 1.5 cm in depth.  There is air in the bladder.  The prostate gland is normal in size.  The wall thickening in the colon is very eccentric and while the normal lumen of the colon is seen towards the left side of the pelvis the wall thickening extends to the right side and there is oral contrast seen within this area of the wall consistent with a sinus tract.  On bone windows, there are no concerning osteolytic or osteosclerotic lesions.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '20326816',
    '11737161-RR-18',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN)',
    'RIGHT UPPER QUADRANT ULTRASOUND: There are several well-circumscribed echogenic round lesions in both lobes of the liver, three on the left and two on the right.  The largest of these measures 1.6 x 1.3 x 1.2 cm in the right lobe of the liver.  These lesions do not demonstrate internal vascularity, although this assessment was somewhat limited by cardiac motion.  There is no intra- or extra-hepatic bile duct dilation, and the common bile duct measures 3 mm.  The main portal vein is patent with hepatopetal flow.  The gallbladder is  unremarkable without stones, sludge, wall thickening, or pericholecystic fluid.  There is no ascites.  Limited evaluation of the pancreas due to overlying bowel gas is unremarkable.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24590497',
    '11761802-RR-20',
    'CT',
    'Neck',
    'CT NECK W/CONTRAST (EG:PAROTIDS)',
    'TECHNIQUE: MDCT of the neck was performed with 2.5 mm axial sections obtained from the aorticopulmonary window through the mid orbital level, during the dynamic IV administration of 70 cc of Omnipaque.  Reformatted coronal and sagittal images were generated and reviewed.  DLP: 1003 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: Evaluation of the aerodigestive tract demonstrates no exophytic mucosal mass or mass effect.  Evaluation of the cervical lymph chains demonstrate no pathologic lymphadenopathy by imaging criteria.  The thyroid gland is normal.  The salivary glands are unremarkable in appearance.  The neck vessels enhance bilaterally without significant stenosis or definite evidence of dissection.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24590497',
    '11761802-RR-21',
    'CT',
    'Head',
    'CT HEAD W/O CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Contiguous axial MDCT images were obtained through the brain without administration of IV contrast.  Reformatted coronal and sagittal and thin section bone algorithm-reconstructed images were acquired.  DLP: 1003 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: Area of hypodensity in the left frontal lobe involving gray-white matter, concerning for cytotoxic edema in the setting of a subacute infarct.  No definitive space occupying lesion is identified.  There is no evidence of intracranial hemorrhage.  The ventricles and sulci are normal in size and configuration. The basal cisterns appear patent. No fracture is identified.  The visualized paranasal sinuses, mastoid air cells, and middle ear cavities are clear.  The globes are unremarkable.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24590497',
    '11761802-RR-22',
    'MRI',
    'Head',
    'MR HEAD W & W/O CONTRAST',
    'MRI OF THE BRAIN WITHOUT AND WITH CONTRAST, MRA OF THE BRAIN WITHOUT CONTRAST:   AND MRA OF THE NECK WITHOUT AND WITH CONTRAST, ___:   TECHNIQUE: Routine ___ enhanced MR examination of the brain, comprising T1-weighted axial SE and sagittal MP-RAGE sequences, post-contrast administration, the latter with axial and coronal reformations.  Axial 3D- and 2D-time-of-flight MRA of the cervical and cranial vessels, respectively, and coronal 3D VIBE MRA of the cervical vessels was performed during dynamic intravenous contrast administration, with review of all axial and coronal source and subtracted images, respectively, on a separate workstation.  In addition, axial T1-weighted SE sequence with fat saturation was performed through the cervical soft tissues, into slab acquisitions, prior to contrast administration.  FINDINGS: The study is compared with the cranial NECT and the cervical soft tissue CECT, performed roughly 17 hours earlier.  Corresponding to the findings on the cranial CT is a somewhat rounded region of T2- and FLAIR-hyperintensity involving left frontal gray and subjacent white matter, with mass effect and effacement of the regional gyri.  This process demonstrates no "blooming" susceptibility artifact to suggest blood products and an unusual well-defined, ovoid 17 mm (AP), relatively "cystic" focus, in its peripheral portion.  Allowing for the T2- "shine-through" of both portions of this process, there is no focus of slow diffusion to suggest acute ischemia.  The process demonstrates overall intermediate T1 signal, other than in the more cystic component, above.  Following contrast administration, there is only at most faint low-level enhancement of the central portion of this process.  There is no pathologic parenchymal, leptomeningeal or dural focus of enhancement, elsewhere.  Otherwise, there are only scattered both punctate and larger T2-/FLAIR-hyperintense foci in bihemispheric, predominantly subcortical white matter, without diffusion- or enhancing correlate; though nonspecific, these likely represent sequela of chronic small vessel ischemic disease.  There is no focus of slow diffusion, elsewhere.  There is normal flow-related enhancement in the included intracranial portions of both internal carotid and proximal middle and anterior cerebral arteries.  There is normal, symmetric arborization of MCA branches, with no significant mural irregularity or flow-limiting stenosis.  There is normal flow-related enhancement in the distal vertebral arteries, with dominant right and hypoplastic left vessel, as well as the basilar and bilateral superior cerebellar and posterior cerebral arteries, without significant mural irregularity or flow-limiting stenosis.  Anterior and robust bilateral posterior communicating vessels are demonstrated, with no aneurysm larger than 3 mm.  The axial fat-saturated and T1-weighted sequences demonstrate no abnormal intramural hyperintensity associated with the cervical, vertebral or carotid arteries to suggest blood products related to acute dissection.  The included aortic arch and great vessel origins are unremarkable, without flow-limiting stenosis.  The common and cervical internal and external carotid arteries are normal in course, caliber, and contour.  They demonstrate normal flow-related and contrast enhancement, without significant mural irregularity, flow-limiting stenosis or evidence of dissection.  Slightly dominant right and slightly hypoplastic left vertebral arteries demonstrate normal course, caliber and contour from their subclavian origins through the vertebrobasilar junction.  There is no significant mural irregularity, flow-limiting stenosis or evidence of dissection.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24590497',
    '11761802-RR-23',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multi detector CT images were obtained from lung bases to the proximal femurs after the administration of intravenous and oral contrast material.  Multiplanar reformatted images in coronal and sagittal planes are provided.  DLP: ___.___ mGy-cm  FINDINGS: Please see the dedicated chest CT report for thoracic findings.  ABDOMEN: The liver is normal in size and homogeneous in enhancement.  There is a vague hypodensity in segment 4B which is too small to characterize(5:49), and is of uncertain significance.  The portal and hepatic veins are patent and there is no intra or extrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation.  The gallbladder is surgically absent.  The common bile duct is not dilated.    The spleen is homogeneous in enhancement and normal in size.  The adrenal glands are normal in size and shape.  The pancreas is homogeneous in enhancement without ductal dilatation or peripancreatic fat stranding.   The kidneys are normal in size and display symmetric nephrograms and contrast excretion.  There are no concerning mass lesions seen in the kidneys.  The ureters are normal in caliber along their course to the bladder.  There is some fluid seen tracking into the right para-renal space.  The distal esophagus is normal appearing.  The stomach is underdistended but unremarkable.  The small bowel contains contrast and does not show abnormal dilatation or focal wall thickening.    There is sigmoid ____ with wall thickening and fat stranding, and evidence of perforation.  There is a 2.5 x 2.3 cm fluid collection with faint rim enhancement and a single locule of air in the anterior pelvis suggestive of phlegmon or early abscess (5:97).    There is no aneurysmal dilatation of the abdominal aorta.  The aorta and its major branches are patent.   There are no pathologically enlarged retroperitoneal or mesenteric lymph nodes by CT size criteria.  PELVIS: The bladder is under distended, but grossly normal.  There is a large exophytic uterine fibroid which measures 4.1 x 2.9 x 3.1 cm.  There are no pathologically enlarged pelvic sidewall or inguinal lymph nodes by CT size criteria.  The rectum is unremarkable.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES AND SOFT TISSUES: No hernias are seen there are no concerning lytic or sclerotic lesions seen.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24590497',
    '11761802-RR-24',
    'CT',
    'Chest',
    'CT CHEST W/CONTRAST',
    'CHEST CT ON ___:   TECHNIQUE: Multidetector helical scanning of the chest was coordinated with intravenous infusion of Omnipaque 350 nonionic iodinated contrast agent reconstructed as contiguous 5- and 1.25-mm thick axial, 5-mm thick coronal, parasagittal, and 8 x 8 mm MIPs axial images, read in conjunction with neck CT ___, followed by scanning of the abdomen and pelvis to be reported separately.  FINDINGS: Supraclavicular and axillary lymph nodes are normal size.  Thyroid is unremarkable.  There are no abnormalities in the soft tissues of the wall of the chest and upper abdomen suggesting malignancy.  Aorta and pulmonary arteries are normal size.  Lymph nodes in the mediastinum, hila, retrocrural and diaphragmatic and internal mammary stations are not enlarged.  Aside from bands of atelectasis predominantly in the lower lobes, lungs are clear.  Tracheobronchial tree is normal to subsegmental levels.  There is no pleural or pericardial abnormality.  Retention of contrast in small left axillary and upper mediastinal veins is unexplained.  There are no findings in the chest cage suspicious for malignancy or infection.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '24590497',
    '11761802-RR-29',
    'MRI',
    'Head',
    'MR FUNCTIONAL BRAIN BY PHYS/PSYCH',
    'TECHNIQUE: Multiplanar T1 and T2 weighted sequences through the brain were acquired before and after administration of intravenous contrast.  Perfusion and spectroscopy were performed. Functional MRI was obtained on a 3 Tesla magnet with Echo Planar/BOLD technique. The functional paradigms include analysis of the motor areas during the alternating movement of the hands, feet and tongue.  Language paradigm during the mental process of generating words with different letters.  Additionally axial MP-RAGE sequence was obtained as a reference anatomical image, arterial spin label sequence, DTI sequences in axial projections and tractography with 36 directions was obtained on a separate workstation.  FINDINGS: The area of increased FLAIR signal and T2 signal in the left frontal lobe appears similar compared to prior with an oval cystic component measuring 18 mm in AP dimension and 10 mm in transverse dimension.  Very faint enhancement just medial to the cystic component is again seen.  Several scattered FLAIR hyperintense foci in the subcortical white matter suggest sequela of chronic small vessel ischemic disease, although these are nonspecific.  Major intracranial arteries and venous sinuses appear patent.  Perfusion imaging demonstrates decreased relative cerebral blood volume in the left frontal abnormality. No significant hyperprfusion is noted on the ASL sequence.  MR spectroscopy does not include the cystic component of the lesion.  In the adjacent area of FLAIR signal abnormality, there is uniformly decreased NAA, which is nonspecific.  There is no definite tumoral spectrum.  Choline and creatinine peaks are roughly the same in the abnormal and normal areas.  The functional paradigms demonstrate the expected activation areas during the movement of the hands and feet in the primary motor cortex.  During the movement of the right hand,  BOLD activation areas are visualized  in close relation with the left frontal lesion posteriorly (image 5, series 400).  The language paradigm, demonstrates the majority of the activation on the left cerebral hemisphere adjacent to the left opercular region, likely related with dominance,  areas of BOLD signal are visualized posterior to the left frontal  lesion during the movement of the tongue (image 9, series 600). On the DTI/Tractography images there is no significant deviation of the association, commissural and cortico-spinal tracts'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '23780894',
    '11795159-RR-7',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABDOMEN W/CONTRAST',
    'CT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS WITH CONTRAST AND RECONSTRUCTIONS.:   TECHNIQUE: MDCT axially-acquired images were obtained from the lung bases to the symphysis with oral and IV contrast administration.  Multiplanar reformatted images were obtained.  CT ABDOMEN WITH CONTRAST: The lung bases are clear without nodule, opacity or effusion.  The visualized heart is unremarkable without pericardial effusion.  Focal fatty infiltration is present at the ligamentum teres hepatis.  A simple cyst is noted in the peripheral right lobe of the liver.  No liver mass is detected.  The gallbladder is normal in appearance without intraluminal stone detected.  The pancreas, spleen, adrenal glands, and visualized large and small abdominal bowel is within normal limits.  The kidneys enhance and excrete symmetrically.  No free fluid or free air is present within the abdomen.  CT PELVIS WITH CONTRAST: Within the sigmoid colon, there is focal wall thickening and inflammatory stranding surrounding a focal outpouching consistent with ____.  Several small adjacent foci of fluid is noted and an adjacent puctate foci of air is detected within the inflammatory change (2:72). No drainable fluid collection is detected within the pelvis.  OSSEOUS STRUCTURES: Sclerotic foci within the L1 and T11 vertebral bodies demonstrate spiculated margins and are most consistent with bone islands.'
);


