    'Abdomen',
    'PELVIS, NON-OBSTETRIC',
    'EXAMINATION: PELVIS, NON-OBSTETRIC:  TECHNIQUE: Grayscale ultrasound images of the pelvis were obtained with transabdominal approach followed by transvaginal approach for further delineation of uterine and ovarian anatomy.  FINDINGS:   LMP: ___.  The uterus is anteverted and measures 9.9 x 5.8 x 3.3 cm. The endometrium is homogenous and measures 9 mm.  The right ovary is normal.  The left ovary is not definitively visualized but no adnexal mass is appreciated. There is a small amount of free fluid in the pelvis, which appears complex.'
);


INSERT INTO radiologyreport (
    clinicalcaseid, noteid, modality,
    region, examname, text
)
VALUES (
    '22429578',
    '12043129-RR-8',
    'CT',
    'Abdomen',
    'CT ABD & PELVIS W/O CONTRAST',
    'EXAMINATION: CT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS:  TECHNIQUE: Axial MDCT images were obtained through the abdomen and pelvis without IV contrast, as per team request given thyrotoxicosis. Oral contrast was provided.  Sagittal and coronal reformats were generated.  TOTAL EXAM DLP: 363 mGy-cm.  FINDINGS: Lung bases are clear. There is no pleural or pericardial effusion.  CT of the abdomen: Evaluation of solid abdominal viscera is limited by lack of IV contrast.  Within this limitation, the liver is homogeneous with no focal hepatic lesions identified. There is no intra or extrahepatic biliary duct dilatation. The gallbladder is predominantly collapsed. The adrenal glands, pancreas and spleen are normal. Incidental note is made of a 2.1 cm accessory spleen. The non contrast-enhanced kidneys are normal with no evidence of hydronephrosis or renal masses. The stomach is collapsed. There is no evidence of small bowel obstruction. The appendix is visualized and there is no evidence of acute appendicitis.  Diverticula are seen along the sigmoid colon with surrounding fluid most prominent on series 2, image 67.  The intra-abdominal aorta is of normal diameter. Vessel patency cannot be examined in this evaluation. There are multiple mildly prominent central mesenteric lymph nodes,  none of which meet CT size criteria for pathological enlargement although these are striking by number. There is trace free fluid. There is no free air.  CT of the pelvis:  Within the right pelvis there is a small amount of free fluid and stranding near several diverticula and epiploic appendages along the lower sigmoid.  (series 2, image 67). The uterus is normal. The urinary bladder is normal. The rectum is contrast filled. Multiple small pelvic lymph nodes are also noted.  Osseous structures:  No blastic or lytic lesion concerning for malignancy.  NOTIFICATION: The findings were discussed by Dr. ___ with Dr. ___ on the telephone on ___ at 12:24 AM.'
);


